      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-12-02_WidnesExaminer_art0008" n="art0008">
        <head>WARRINGTON DISTRESS COMMITTEE.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON DISTRESS COMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>Royal and Ducal Parties.—The Duke and Duchess of Devonshire who aro among the guests invited to Sandringham for the Queen's birthday party-sire arrang- ing for their usual Christmas and New Year house parties at Chatsworth. The Duchess is in much better health than lately, and if this improvement continues, the entertainments at Chatsworth will be on their customary magnificent scale, and it is possible that the King and Queen will be present. There are to be private theatricals as usual, fur which certain indis- pensable guests have been bespoken, mid it is said that Princess Dewy of Pleas has written a little play of her own, which will form part of the proposed theatrical entertainments. The King devotes more time than ever to paying country house visits, but the Queen is more attached to her home surroundings, and their Majesties have only a comparatively small party at Sandringham in celebration of the Queen's birthday. Among the guests are Lord and Lady de Grey. Lord de Grey is treasurer of her Majesty's household and is acting as treasurer of the Queen's Fund for the Unemployed. The ambition to have a Motor Car.—Srhe Ladies, Automobile Club, which has the Duchess of Sutherland for president, mustered in great force at the Olympia I Show, at which many magnificent cars were seen. It j is the one ambition of the Society woman to have a I motor car, and the more splendid and costly it is, the more determined she is to have it. Some of the cars on j view seemed to reach the limit of luxury. More atten- tion is now paid to fittings end general appointments I of the car, those for touring purposes' having fitted dressing cases and jewel safe and cunningly devised recesses for wardrobe cases and so on. Anything up tb , £2OO or £3OO, may be paid for such fittings alone, which I in some instances include a " First Aid " box in case of I accident on the road. The majority of the latest cars are painted green and this is the predominant colour I in the motoring dress of the present day. The green car is not so showy as the wesite or cream and gold, but I it is favoured because it is less] liable to frighten ' horses, and attract the unwelcome attention of the police. Motors and Nervous Irritability.—The civility which one invariably meets with from omnibus' conductors seems to be less in evidence among electric-car and motor-bus attendants. This no doubt arises from the fact that they have a larger number of passengers to attend to, than in the days of the smaller and horse- drawn conveyances, but it is believed by many that the motor age will destroy much of the good humour of the road. One can quiteunderstand that the increased tension on the,rnind in motor travelling, would be nfpro- ductive of ryous irritability, and perhaps a less courteous manner than we have been accustomed to from those in charge of public conveyances. The Bridge Craze.—Bridge is played as much as ever among all classes of Society, and some of the best players are to be found amongst the women votaries of that engrossing pastime. There is talk of introducing two new rules into the game. One is the suggestion. that when "no trumps " are declared, there shall be no I " dummy," the dealer's partner playing the hand as in ! "whist" without exposing the cards on the table. The I idea is to deprive the player of a"no trimm"declaiation of the great advantage ho or she has in having "dummy's" hand upon the table. This however is an advantage that comes to all in turn, and the proposed alteration would rather diminish the sporting element in the game, so that it is not likely to commend itself to regular players. The other suggestion is, that the hand dealt to the dealer's partner must remain on the table until the dealer has said whether he or she makes the declara- tion, or " leaves it." This of course is intended as a precaution against hints either intentionally or uninten- tionally given as regards making the "declaration." There is no doubt that this ought to be made one of the strict rules of the game, though it would naturally prolong the playing of the hand. The Bridge Toilette.—Dainty little lace cnatees with elbow sleeves give a charming finish to the Bridge toilet, for either afternoon or evening wear—these are usnally made up in the form of a loose bolero, over a soft lining of coloured erkie de chine, and fastened down the front with little bows of panne,or caught together with jewelled clasps. The sleeves are of elbow length, simpl3 trimmed with a guttered ruffle of lace, which may be divided iuto the centre by a bracelet of velvet, threaded through is jewelled slide. Fur Wraps.--The fur stole, worn with ends falling to I the right and left over one shonlder,is no longer donned I by the irreproachable turned-out woman, who is now I pinning her faith to the long flat fur scarf which fastens beneath the chin, and falls from thence almost to the feet. And save where ermine—of the best and freshest—is available little white fur is worn by well- I dressed women. The " mas-ms "have conceived a great affection for white hats, and white furs, which more I often than not, are white in name only, and the irre- proachably turned-out will therefore have none of i -them' .</p>
        <p>About Umbrella Handles.—Umbrella handles, covered in frog. alligator, elephant, or pig skin, and mounted with silver or gold, are among the novelties or the season, and promise to he well sought after for Christ- mas and New Year glfts. Those in gun metal however, are still enjoying a conside,•able vogue, very handsome specimens being combined with ivory and gold. Ivory handles—exquisitely carved by Japanese workmen, aro again to the fore, and these, when combined with gold or silver, form very desirable possessions. Taken altogether the best taste is evident among the new umbrella handles, 'little in tho way of eccentricities or freaks, finding a place in the wide and very charming selection.</p>
        <p>Laze Making.—The latest-craze, which to a great extent has taken the place of the harp in fashionable drawing rooms, is that of lace making, and at the moment, the pi llor cover .d in some delicate shade of silk, with its fringe of bobbins, and a piece of lace in a' more or less—generally less—advanced stage, is a familiar object. It has been said that the making of Dillow lace shews off pretty arms and hands—and incidentally handsome rings and bracelets—quite as well as the harp and doubtless this view of the matter has had considerable weight with fitshionable women. The Cause and Treatment of Chilblains.—Cbilblains are, like corns, easier to prevent than cure, and those who suffer from them in winter time should endeavour to obtain relief by improving their general health. They arise from defective circulation, which may be con- stitutional, or produced by cold, as is often still further</p>
        <p>aggravated locally by the wearing of tight boots an gloves. That is vvhy chilblains uaually form on the hands and feet. Fo invigorato the circulation is the first thing to try to do, by wearing only thoroughly aired warm woollen clothing, and living upon a nourishing diet of heat-giving foods. If cod liver oil can be taken so much the better, and plenty of outdoor exercise, is of course, always essential to good health. Locally, chilblains should be gently rubbed with camphorated oil or turpentine oil. hut more importance should be attached to the constitutional treatment as the best means for getting rid of them.</p>
        <p>MAD GE.</p>
        <p>Inaugural Meeting. The first meeting of the Warrington Distress Com- mittee was held at the Town Dail on Monday when the business was chiefly of a formal character. There were present :—Alderman Tinnion and Councillors Barton, Evans, Dr. Joseph, Pemberton, SaeHand, Stott, Wilkinson, Wilson and Wright (representatives of the Town Council), Miss Constance Broadbent, Mrs. Clay and Mrs. Wilkinson the Rev. Dr. Mercer, and Messrs. A. J. Jolley, R. Bolton and S. Davies (representatives of the Board of Guardians), and Mrs. Joseph Harrison, the Rev. J. Yonge and Messrs. J. Haley and T. S. Steel ; (co-optative members). Alderman TINSION was unanimously elected chairman of the committee, Cohncillor Stott • being appointed deputy chairman. _</p>
        <p>lengthy discussion took place with reference to the admitting of the press and the public to the meetings. It was felt that there were times when it would be advisable to sit in camera owing to the delicate nature of some of the inquiries to be made, but at other times, namely, at the regular meetings of the committee, it was decided that both press and public should be admitted. These meetings will be held at G. 30 in the evening. . . .</p>
        <p>Seven members we-re appointed to dram up the Standing Orders for the regulation of the proceedings of the ceommittee, and a sub-committee was appointed to coni sider the hest method of establishing a labour bureau to asceriain the extent of the existing distress. The Chairman and Deputy-Chairman were instructed to wait upon the Town Clerk and the Borough Treasurer with the object of obtaining temporary officers to carry on the work of the committee.</p>
        <p>Princess Nicholas of Cr,;ecei a gracefUl-loo's- ing and "deciddly p;.4:tty .womr.n.. She ris bright conversationally. and i 4 a -favourite ;Cith our King and Queen.- She loves light colours, and they suit her, but she looks splendid in brown. She thoroughly understa!:ds ti art of dress, as most of the bighly-boa Ilttsiaii ladies do. .A .Anitsx's WEDDING GIFT.</p>
        <p>Sir Philip Darne-Jones i; painting for Lady Palmer a ' portrait of Countess FOO Welcher', in her stadio. in St .:7 James's - Palace, for pre- sentation to Countess Vrida Cleichen (Countess Feo's sister), on her forthcoming marriage. Sir Philip is painting Countess Feo in her working dress and long aproa as he stands at work on a statue. It nialies' a 'charming picture, and constitutes a wedding present doubly welcome both as a personal souvimir mid 'a gift im- possible of duplication.</p>
        <p>Illustrated in our sketch. is a pretty afternoon gown, suitable for home, wear on reception days or other serni-dresoy occasions: The material of which' it is fashioned is one of the new cash- meres, supple- in texture and: delightfully 'easy -to drape. The bodice of the frock is arranged in the fashionable trosoover Style. and has a finger-width of mink outlining each edge. In the opening is a dainty little vest of Irish crochet. The fulnesA of the. bodice is drawn into a high, shapely belt of silk. &amp;domed in front with four handsome buttons. A ,slight novelty is intro-</p>
        <p>(laced into the sleeves by leaving the inside of the sleeve plain and close-fitting, and carrying all the fulness to the outside of the arm in two puffs, separated by a narrow band of gauging. The skirt is quite plain over the hips, but, being much.- gored, 3 ; 'very full about the feet. You will observe that it, is made in the fashionable tunic form, the edge of each skirt being out- lined with a narrow band of the fur.„-.</p>
