      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-12-02_WidnesExaminer_art0010" n="art0010">
        <head>CO-OPERATIVE DRESSIIAZ/NO.</head>
        <p>CO-OPERATIVE DRESSIIAZ/NO.</p>
        <p>A co-operative sewing club is the scheme started by a couple of New York women a week or so ago, says a. writer in a New York paper. For them, as club directors, it represents a com- fortable livelihood. For women who may join as members, it will be a way of procuring smart, well-cut gowns at little cost. "The women who can afford to pay 25d01. to have a costume made up will not bo interested in our club," ono of its originators said. "Women who must economise in dress, yet wish something more exclusive than a ready-made garment and something smarter in cut and fit than the ordinary dressmaker can give, are the ones to whom this organisation will appeal. We provide sewing parlours, machines, fashion plates, and the services of a cutter and fitter who for years has been employed in one of the leading women's tailoring establishments in the city. A member may bring materials for any garment to the clubrooms, have it cut in real tailor fashion and 'fitted perfectly, and then may finish the garment at home if she is com- petent to do so. A woman who does not under- stand dressmaking at all may come here time after time to finish a garment, and she will be directed how to proceed; but she must be able to do good plain sewing and know how to run a sewing machine for herself. We keep all the leading fashion magazines on our tables, and expect to consult with our members. as to the best colours and styles - when they are in doubt upon such points. We will try to advise them of pretty, effective trimming .for gowns which require adornment Of the kind, and will save them the bother of shopping for linings and bindings when they desire it.' ;</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-12-02_WidnesExaminer_art0011" n="art0011">
        <head>NEW FrATS.</head>
        <p>NEW FrATS.</p>
        <p>Never was winter miliMery mere fascinating than during this year of 1905. The grotesque smallness of the summer hat has given place to mor3 moderate and becomino•., proportions, with. out undue development in the opposite direction. Pictured in our illustration are two typical ex- amples of smart winter headgear. The upper hat, or rather 'Mono, is a charming arrangement of white felt and sapphire-blue velvet. The up- turned brim of the toque is made of fine glossy white heaver felt. and bear, on one side a beauti- ful bird, arranged with outspread wings and tail.</p>
        <p>White, tlack, and various tones of blue zningk in its plumage. Tho skilfully-draped crown is en- tirely made of the sapphire velvet. The seam(' hat is an excellent illustration of tho extremely popular fur toque. Made entirely of ermine, or an excellent imitation thereof, the sole touch of colour is given by a bunch of La France roses arram-ed with their foliage on the round up- turned brim.</p>
