      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-12-02_WidnesExaminer_art0012" n="art0012">
        <head>RINSE GQWNS.</head>
        <p>RINSE GQWNS.</p>
        <p>Panne is being much used for 'afternoon dresses, the skirts as a whole being'very. full and plain, all the .-dressmaker's art being called Mto play in the construction of the bodices. A gown of this material was fashioned with an abnor- mally high lace collar measuring quito three inches in height, and cut in one with the yoke, on .;to which the reliet corsage appeared to he buttoned by means of a series of small tabs, out- lined with gold thread or rilk cord of a contrast- ing colour.</p>
        <p>A . new apparatus, the " aquarneter," has been devised for assisting in the compilation of wea- ther forecasts. It gives the exact percentage of moisture in the approachini winds. The baro- meter gives some such indication, but the height of the barometer depends on wind pres- sure and temperature - as well as on • mois- ture. The • wet and dry.-. &amp;nib thermome- ters constitutq an antiquated instrument. and are not sufficiently reliable. Lain results when an atmosphere nbarly satwated with mei- TO-DAT'S RECIPE. stare becomes lowered in temperature., The ROAST GROUSE.—Cleanse and truss a couple of nearness cr otherwise of a 'wind to its saturation nice grouse, wash the livers, and eautrz them point, is, therefore, a most important question. lightly with those of a couple a fowls. 20z. of It has had to be ,determined hitherto by recourse batter, a 'chopped shallot, a sprig of Parsley, and to elaborate apparatus. By using the aquameter, one of thyme. 'Pound 2oz. of scraped veal, add- however, the ,exact, percentage of moisture can ing the livers, 1,0±„. of scraped fat bacon. loz. of be obtained. By the opening and shutting of two butter, and 2oz. of breadcrumbs. previously sitn- taps, and the raising and lowering of a mercury rnered to a paste in good stock. Work M one ree.ervoir, says Srienee Siftings, a measured quan- egg and one yolk, add salt and pepper to taste, tity of air is drawn into a glass vessel, and and rub it throned' a wire sieve. ~stuff the crops ',bused in et-mint-4 with anhydrous phosphoric tne pitrts wit;!tne rarce, secure it with tram:, acid, which is a rapid water absorbent, ,Ine rise tie a piece of fat bacon on the breast, and roast of mercury in the narrow glass stem of the vessel them about thirty minutes, basting them very then gives the exact percentage of the moisture frequently. Serve the grouse garnished with in the air. The instrument has been made b: picked watercress, browned breadcrumbs, and Mr. J. J. Hicks, of Hatton-garden, London, bread sauce in separate boats.</p>
      </div>
