      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-12-02_WidnesExaminer_art0016" n="art0016">
        <head>GLEANINGS.</head>
        <p>GLEANINGS.</p>
        <p>THE LIFE OF RADIUM. , Several attempts have been mane to deter- mine the "life" of radium. i.e.., how long an atom of radium takes to disintegrate. Professor Rutherford, of Montreal, has been making a determination bawd on the number of charged particles expelled per sccoud. •As a roult he concludes that an atom of .radium would take about 1,850 years to disintegrate completely.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-12-02_WidnesExaminer_art0017" n="art0017">
        <head>REAL FLYING MACHINE.</head>
        <p>A REAL FLYING MACHINE.</p>
        <p>A flying machine which can rise in the air by ee force of its propeners alone has been tried in Paris. It is the invention of the brothers Dufaux, of Geneva. They call it a helicoptere, that is, a screw-fiier. It, weighs 38.11 b., and will carry 1541 b. more. Members of the Aero Club consider that the heliceptere will aid Featly in solving the problem of the flying machine of the "heavier than air" type..</p>
        <p>MOUNTAIN SICKNESS.</p>
        <p>! Two French medical men—Drs. Guillemard and Moog—have mado a stay at the Mont Blanc Obiervatory, with the astronomer, M. Janssen, for the purpose of studying the inflame° of the temperature and climate of high altitudes upon general nutrition. Their conclusions are that the diminished tension of the oxygen of the atmosphere clogs the process of oxidation, and this sets up an elaboration of toxic substances the retention of which causes symptoms of auto- intoxication and accounts for the ' symptoms of mountea sickness. Acclimatisation, however, ' results in a few days, and the symptoms_pass away under circumstances similar to those Which accompany the passing of the crisis in infectious , disorders.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-12-02_WidnesExaminer_art0018" n="art0018">
        <head>AN IMPROVED RESPIRATOR.</head>
        <p>AN IMPROVED RESPIRATOR.</p>
        <p>Attention is directed in a French scientific paper to a respirator designed by AI. Bachelay for nle in places where foul air is prevalent. It consists of a half-mask of :aluminium, made to ,cover . the nose and mouth, and adapted to fit the face and jaws by means of an inflated tube of thin indiarubber, a sort of pneumatic attach- ment, which renders it comfortable to wear when tightly adjusted by securing the head straps. It contains a valve for the air expired and a long tube serves to convey pure air from a distance for hrcathnlg, either the mouth or nose. The air su ply is filteif d through a small chamber filled with antiseptic cotton wool placed at the further extremity of the inlet tube.</p>
        <p>i Bums AND AVAIOSPILERE.</p>
        <p>A curious test for atmosphere has been tried in tho French Senate. General Billot, who occupied a certain fauteuil, suffered from head- ache, which he attributed . to oxide of carbon given off by the heating apparatus. Tho engineer responsible for the heating apparatus gad care;pl examination and tests made without discovering any defect: Ho then had recourse to what may be described as the bird test. Bengalce birds, as they are called, are credited with being easier to asphyxiate than any other member of the feathered tribe. One of these ,was procured by the engineer, who left it hanging in its cage for eight :hours over the fauteuil in which General 13illot had contracted headache. As at the' end of that time the bird was as lively as ever, it is concluded that nothing was wrong with the heating apparatus. • DETERMINE AGE OF FISHES.</p>
        <p>It has been contended 'that the age of a fish can be determined by counting the number of lines of growth in the scales. Dr. Marett Tim% however, in a paper on the development of scales in fishes, throws some doubt on this method. Ho raises the question whether fishes de not periodi- cally shed -and renew their scales as birds do their feathers. And he concludes that there is acme reason to suppose that they do. Experi- enced fishermen on the east coast of Scotland, he points out, are convinced that fishes." moult" in this way. And if this is so, Dr. 'rims contends that the above test for age will be invalidated. The suggestion that the renewed scale might have thee same number of lines of growth as the one. which it rePlaced he regaixls as improbable. It seems, however to be a fact that the scales in different parts of the fish's body have always the same number of lines, and this suggests that if the scales are shed they are renewed in the exact likehess of their predecessors.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-12-02_WidnesExaminer_art0019" n="art0019">
        <head>ETHER WAVES.</head>
        <p>ETHER WAVES.</p>
        <p>Another discovery with regard to ether-waves, or rather pulses, is announced by Dr. H. Hahn. It has long been known, • says the Athenceum, that the Röntgen rays will discharge any charged body on which they fall, and Professor Right has contended that they would also im- press a charge upon a body previously without One. Dr. Halm has no established by experi- ment that all bodies upon which the rays fall receive a positiv6 charge, although the effect is often masked by the secondary rays which they at the same time produce. His explanation of the phenomenon is that the pulse is absorbed by the bbdy impinged - upon it, thus breaking borne ValerlC7 bonds, whereby both positive and negative electrons are set Gee. Given a suffi- ciently strong impact, both ions and electrons are projected outside the body; but the elec- trons, having less mass, leave first arid in greater numbers. Thus their projection leaves the body with a negative charge. Dr. Hahn also thinks that his experiments decide conclusively the question of the true nature of the Gamma rays, which he regards as ether pulses and as the counterpart of the Röntgen rays.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-12-02_WidnesExaminer_art0020" n="art0020">
        <head>PURE WATER BY ELECTRICITY,</head>
        <p>PURE WATER BY ELECTRICITY,</p>
        <p>It is problble that electric purification of drinking water will soon be introduced into the borne. This method is based upon the germ- killing effects of ozone, which is cheapl,y engen- -1 :.(.‘red by electricity. If an electric discharge 1 ' tAl:es place between two glass tubes, one inside the other, whose surfaces facing each other are coated • with metal, ozone is developed in the apace between the tubes. . The ideal . apparatus would -be one which every housekeeper could put up in the kitchen, and by utilising the elec- tric current of the .cominon 'electric-light wires purify every glass of chinking water. Such an apparatus seems to have been - successfully made by a French engineer, M. 'Otto. It is of , very simple construction, and takes up little space. It. consists principally of a small, closed box, the tnel enver of which is made conductory with the bottom.. In the box is an 2ZOTIe developer, hp intarupter, And a tin tube:. .- Through the latter the trzAtie, which first has to pass through a cotton stopper to free it from dust and germs I rontained in the air, is conducted into the water I and mixed therewith. .If much ozone has been 1 uliorbpd, the water becomes phosphorescent in l the darlz.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-12-02_WidnesExaminer_art0021" n="art0021">
        <head>RADIO-THORIUM.</head>
        <p>RADIO-THORIUM.</p>
        <p>Sir William Ramsay has described at length In a paper read at the Liege Congress the results of his discoveries in connsction with the new radio-active substance which he propos,ss to call radio-thorium. From. 250 • kilogramme of thorianite from Ceylon, • he obtained,: after treat- ?s grammes of a. barium carbonate per, ,sessnig `a radio-activity equal to that of 14 milli- grammes of radium bromide. Dissolved in hydro- bromic acid, this left a residue of what appeared to be radium bromide, of which one part .was more soluble than the , other. The more soluble Part proved to have the rthdio-activity not of radium,, but of thorium; and this it possessed even after all the, thorium ,was eliminated. The new substance has a soluble . sulphate, while that of radium is insoluble, and it • also forms an in- soluble oxalate. Its solutions produce an emana- tion several hundred thousand times raore active than those of thorium, :ad it aprears to be quite different from actinium. Its discoverer thinks it probable that it forms the immediate product of ordinary thorium, and that helium, which is present in large quantities in the original mineral, is its ultimate prodwit. .</p>
        <p>THE AQU.A'METEP.</p>
      </div>
