      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-12-02_WidnesExaminer_art0024" n="art0024">
        <head>140( 1; .11,, or .s 3 11.47 41. • k 4 ~1- TN. – '</head>
        <p>140( 1; .11,, or .s 3 11.47 41. • k 4 ~1- TN. - '</p>
        <p>IN* lt N. TO&amp;</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON BOARD OF GUARDIANS</p>
        <p>The Cost of Vaccination.</p>
        <p>A meeting of the Warrington Board of Guardians was held on Wednesday, Mr. J. Billinge presiiding. There were also present : Rev. I). Mercer, M.S. Clay, Mrs. Wilkinson, Miss Broadbent, Dr. Murray, Messrs. A. J. Jolley, IL Forshftw, S. Davies, G. Thompson, A. Brooks, Longton, J. Cook, IL FiannaNtn, M. W. Seton, J. C. Stittori (clerk), J. F. Awhery .(master), A. Wright (supeliatendent of Paricste schools). lIOUSE COMMITEE'S BUSINESS.</p>
        <p>Prollationer-ii-in;e-ii-t-e-hing-c--;M-pie-ted her three years engagement, intimated to the House Committee her intention of leaving the Board's service, and it was resolved to appoint in her place Hiss Annie Cooke of Newton-le-willows. Mrs. Jane Poole, hospital maid, wrote resigning her post,and it was resolvedito promote to the vacancy May Williamson, the matrons maid, at a salary of XlG'per annum and the usual allowances,—The filling up or the vacancy was left in the hands of the matron.—The Male Voice Choir were granted permission to give a concert zit the house on Thursday rzvening. On the motion or Mr. FORSHAW seconded by Mr. THOMPSON the minutes wore confirmed. THE COST OF VACCINATION.</p>
        <p>A letter was revived from the ,Corwen Board of Guardians asking tile Board to support the following resolution relating to vaccination expenses anti for- ward ir to the Local Government Board ;—that in the opinion of this Board the Vaccination Acts should be thoroughly revised, and the cost of administration of the same amended to the extent that only the children .of paupers be vaccinated and re-vaccinated at the expense of the ratepayers. That a copy be sent to the Local Government Board, local members of Parliament, the Association of Poor Law Unions and to each Union in England and Wales. The CLERIC said that if the Board was desired he would place the letter on the agenda for consideration at the next meeting..</p>
        <p>Mr. A. J. JOLLEY thOught the letter should lie on the table. It was not the practice of the Board to deal with matters of that kind except through the Association of Poo-r Law Unions which already had the matter in band and was endeavouring to bring pressure to bear on the Departmental Committee. The letter suggested a very important question of principle which the Board would certainly not he unanimous upon. The CHAIRMAN : The Association is already con- sidering the question. Mr. Tiromesos said that if 'they could do anything te.alter the laws on the question it would be a very good thin'''. He found on referring to the year book for 1805-67 when he first became connected with the I Board the vaccination expenses amounted to £152 its, 10d. In 1994-5 the expenses came to £1,210, anincrease of £1,063. Some persons whose children were vaccinated at the ratepayers' expense ought . to be ashamed to seek the services of a medical &amp;Facer of teat Union. It was a disgrace to I many people and they should remember that in having their children vaccinated at the Union expense they were accepting relief just as if they formally applied for the same. Then. algain. it was a great injustice to medical men in private practice. lie moved that the question come up for further consideration at the next meeting. _ Mr. ASHCROFT seconded and said doctors in the town had told him that they had no chance in the matter of vaccination as people who could afford to pay had their children vaccinated free of charge by the Union medical officers. Mr. JOLLRY moved an amendment that the letter lie on the table. He said it opened up a very wide question and personally he would respectfully point out that Mr. Thompson'l attitude appeared to him to be entirely wrong. It was certainly opposed to the whole Spirit of the legislation under which Vaccination was carried on. It was not, a question of relief. They might just-as well say/that education was a question of poor relief, because the great bulk of the cost of educa- tion in this country was a charge upon the revenue of the country through rates or taxes. Vaccination was being put upon a similar footing. Vaccination officers were not merely union officials, they were public officials discharging public functions. To suggest that a person availing himself of the law was the recipient of relief was to place nn entirely wrong construction on the matter. Vaccination was a public duty to be carried out at the public expense for the public protection. Most of the Guardians would no doubt like to see the expense reduced. Steps had already been taken to secure a reduction but effect had</p>
        <p>not been given to it by an order of the Local Gover ment Board. A Departmental Committee had sat and , the Council of the Association of Poor Law Unions I had also dealt with the matter on several occasions and 'were doing all they could to induce the Local Govern- ment Board to issue a revised scale. They could not make the changes suggested by Mr. Thompson without fresh legislation and it was hopeless to expect legisla. tion at the present state of affairs dealing with a matter of that kind. Something could, however, be done in the direction 07 economy by an alteration of administration.</p>
        <p>Mr. BROOKS seconded. He did not see that it would do any good for a dumber of Unions,to begin discussing vaccination in the abstract. It such a central authority as the Association of Poor Law Unions had the matter in hand, he thought the Board might fairly rest assured that they would deal with it in the most prac- tical way possible. If at any time the Board could strengthen the hands of tho Association by resolution then would be the time to discuss the vaccination ques- tion, and not at the present juncture !hen Unions were at six's and seven's. _</p>
        <p>The CfrAintas pointed ont and slid the Prescot Union took 'the same attittide that Mr. Thomp- son suggested, and went to the Local Government Hoard who assured them that the matter was under favourable. consideration,and in all probability would in c' e course receive attention. Mr. Jolley's amendment was carried.</p>
        <p>FAMILY_ NEGLECT AT RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>At Runcorn ou Monday, Wm. Henry Johnson, a boat- man, was sentenced to three month's hard labour for negiecting hiR wife and children who are chargeable to. the common fund of the Runcorn Union. Tne prisoner received a similar sentence in April last.</p>
        <p>A superstition that dies hard is that to do household washing and cleaning takes , 6 hard work, takes drudgery. It is proved every day of the week that with Fels- . Naptha soap the element of drudgery is altogether taken out of the work. That is to say, when Fels- Naptha soap is used, as it should be with cold or tepid water. Not hot water as that drives the naptha away too</p>
        <p>quickly. It should not be forgotten that Fels-Naptha is more than s,oap, that its use is simpler, and its results far above what</p>
        <p>any mere soap can do. Go by the simple directions on wrapper and if not amply convinced of its work-saving, clothes - saving, time - saving qualities, grocer returns money. Fels-Naptha 39 Wilson street London EC</p>
      </div>
