      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-12-02_WidnesExaminer_art0028" n="art0028">
        <p>FTER an attack of , influenza it Iseerns almost impossible for you to get really NN ell again. You feel weak and tired. Your appetite is not at all good and you do not sleep well at night. Trifles irritate you and sometimes you feel as though you could not go on with your daily work. This is a time of danger for you because the system is really down and is in no fit condition to fight disease.</p>
        <p>run</p>
        <p>One of the greatest helps to complete recovery after influenza is Iron-Ox Tonic Tablets. They are a real tonic for your• whole system. They bring you strength and give you vitality. They help you to throw off that wearing weakness which seems to pull you down and keep you ailing and miserable. iron-Ox Tablets are not only a great tonic, they are a gentle laxative as well.. They purify and enrich your blood and strengthen your digestive organs which are often weak after a heavy cold or an attack of influenza.</p>
        <p>- We are supported by the written words of Doctors, Nurses and men and women like yourself, when we tell you that Iron-Ox Tablets can bring strength, can help you back to health when other remedies fail. Iron-Ox Tablets are well-known to the medical profession throughout the United Kingdom, and we have several hundred letters on file from Doctors, telling of the benefits which their families and patients have derived from this remedy.</p>
        <p>Now, if you are weak and atling, if you find it hard to get back your strength, if you find that the after-effects 'of that bad cough or of that attack of influenzi are keeping you in a state of weakness, be guided by these words and go to-day to your own cherrist and buy a box of so Iron-Ox Tablets for One Shilling. If your chemist has not got them, they will be sent post-free for is. by the Iron-Ox Remedy Co., Ltd., 20, Cockspur-st., London, S.W.</p>
        <p>A</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-12-02_WidnesExaminer_art0029" n="art0029">
        <head>RUNCORN BOARD OF GUARDIANS.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN BOARD OF GUARDIANS.</p>
        <p>The Cost of Vaccinatioa.</p>
        <p>The fortnightly -meeting of the Runcorn Board.ef Guardians was held at Dutton on Tuesday, when there were present Messrs. A. R. Norman (presiding), C. Roylance. J. Belshew' IL Eyes, P. Monnfleld, G. Egerton-Warburton, .1. Stokes, A. Broom, T.'. Baxter, C. E. Linaker. J. H. DIA/les, J. Smith, T. Wright, H. Macon-Islay, T. Guest, H. M. Davies, S. Woodward, NV. Harrison, S. Horton, J. L. Jones, .1. Fryer, J. Spruce,W. M. Morris, P. Collins, T. Wood, J. W. 'Temperley, A. R. Norman, J. Whitley. C. E. Litetker, pin.. W. Weir, J. B. Chadwick, G. F. Ashton (clerk), IV. E. Hovel (assistant clerk), De Prez. Cook and Pearson (relieving officers)) and J. Cunnah (Workhouse Master). The Corwen Union forwarded .the following resolu- tion which they asked the Board to support "That in the opinion of this Board the Vaccination Act should he thoroughly revised and fhb cost of the administra- tion of the same amended to the extent that only children of paupers should be vaccinated or re-vaccinated at the expense of the ratepayers." Mr. Linaker moved the supporting of the resolution. Tine cost ef vaceinttion NV;I3 onsiderahlo end he knew of cases where people who could afford to pay had been vaccinated at the expense of the ratepayers. He thought it was a monstrous shame that such a thing shonld be allowed. Mr. Davies secon 'zed. Mr. Morris moved that the resolution be allowed to lie on the table. Vaccination was compulsory and they ought to hear the cost of it. Mr. Collins seconded the amendment. On being put to the vote only the proposer and seconder of the amendment voted for it. Mr. %Yowl ward moved that the Board ask the Local Government Board to revert to the old order of things. He was entirely in sympathy with the expression 'nude by Mr. Linaker and considered it desirable in the interests of the community that the old order should he reverted to. Mr. Linaker: In any event the ratepayers should be relieved of the tremendous cost of vaccination. Mr. Woodward also movel that a cony or his resolu- tion be forwarded to every Board of Guardians in the country caking them to support it. The Chairman: With regard to vaccination we are like the County Council and the Education Act. The ,County Council have an Act to administer and yet many people blame them. According to that it is reasonable for the Board of Guardians to be blamed for administering an Act which involves them in expense which they pennet avoid. Mr. Linaker : The two eases are not quite alike. The complaint against the Ctunty Council is not about their administering the Act, but ahnut the extravagant way in which they administer it. (Hear,hear.) Mr. Woodward's motion was carried. INCREASE IN TIIE COUNTY COUNCIL CALLS. Mr. Wo-odward stated that he had received a call from the County Cenncil for £8,283 19e. 54. as com- pared with £7,905 03. *I. for the past half year, and -£6,859 113 s. Od. fur the corresponding period of last year. The call was made lip as follows :—General county purposes, £2,187 Es. 5d.: general police purposes, £351 Os. 7d.; police district purposes, £82.5 01.; general elementary eduention. .C.:3,333 13s. 4d. ; special elemen- tary education. /243 25.34. ; general higher Mile tion, £972 3s. 44. The increase in the call was due p I in- eipally to the general comity rate which had gene up £lOO, and to education which bad increased by £2OO. RESIGNATION OF MRS. CRADWICK. The Clerk reported that Ire. Chadwick had resigned from the Ladies Visiting Committee. It was decided to accept the resignation with regret. Tun KINOSWOO9 sANITORIUM. It was decided to appeal agaisst the decision' of the Magistrates at the Quarter Session with respect to the assessment of the sanitorium at Ringswood. .</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-12-02_WidnesExaminer_art0030" n="art0030">
        <head>INDUSTRIAL BETTERMENT AT MESSRS. CROSFIELD'S WORKS. --- Mrs. Cranston's Impressions.</head>
