      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-12-02_WidnesExaminer_art0037" n="art0037">
        <head>Examiner, Saturday, December 2, 1905.</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, December 2, 1905.</p>
        <p>(laces and are regularly drilled in the quickest and calmest way of leuviug the budding on the sound of the fire signal.' Eh girl mast rapidly and closely follow the one in front of her, hut nit touch her. Witnout confusion 121 girls ca.a be out of a room in thirty seconds after the alarm. There is an ambulance brigade of over fifty members, two of whom served through tne South African War- Upon completing the third year of this service men are given uniforms. - Fire statious are placed in different parts of the works, with the names of men in each section conspicuously posted. Badges are worn by the anabidenee men. There is a resident staff of. two men at the mains station,which is well equipped for the treatment of serious injuries, as well As first aid cases. Here there is kept a monthly record of the number anthkind of accidents and the departments where they have occurred. A report is sent to the Departmental Managers to assist them • in preventing a recurrence. The plant covers acres of ground, making necessary, a system of railways within the works. Asa precaution against accident, railway shouters wear scarlet coats, and all passages leading to a crossing are closed by chains requiring a few seconds to unhook, and rehook after passing. Much attention is paid to education. No girls or boys under fourteen are employed. A few years ago it WU found that only 14 per cent, of those under seven- teens were attending the evening classes in Warrington. Wishing to raise the average and encourage attend- ance the firm offered to pay the fees for all school courses, and give a money prize to everyone under seventeen years who could show a good report from his teacher. The year following this proposal found 38 per cent, at school. The plan was then extended to all male employees under twenty, with the result that 73 per cent then attended the evening classes. Much gratified at the success of this voluntary attendance,the firm decided that in future attendance at evening classes should be compulsory. When "under 20 " is the com- pulsory limit, boys who enter the factory at fourteen will thus have sis: years' schooling with . fees paid and an equal amount of cash as prise money. - Just as much interest is paid to the education of the girls, who are being indncecl to attend evening classes on the same plan, to learn needlework, cooking and domestic economy. Turning to the lighter side of induStrial betterment, la great deal of purely social work is done, encouraged by the firm, but managed by the employes themselveit. It is the firm's policy to give every facility for the pro- motion of recreative features, where a desire for them is manifested by the workers themselves, to give sug- gestions, advice and money, where needful, but to leave , the Retire manaeement entirely in the hands of the employes, considlring industrial betterment to be upon , a more enduring basis under such circumstances.</p>
        <p>There is a ver'y pretty social hall in the works. The colour scheme is yellow. :fany palms and ferns are placed in the hall, uhich has a stage with shifting scenery at one end. The firm gave the hall, but the employes bought the plants and paid for the decora- tious and stage. Lectures, concerts and dances are given here. There are various athletic and social clubs,a dramatic society, gymnasium, lunch rooms and technical library. The most distinctive feature at Crosfleld's is the music. In certain departments at stated hours during the day the girls sing while at work. It is very pleasant for a visitor to be greeted by such sweet old songs as " Bonnie Dundee " and " Blue Bells of Scotland." In the beginning the girls had formed a musical dub taught by one of their number. They did so well that this year they have a trained musical director paid by the firm. The brass band, composed of men, took the prize at the National Brass Band contest at Crystal Palace, last year, in which twenty-five bands were entered.</p>
        <p>It was an inspiration te go through the Croseeld factory with Dr. larkel, and to find a man of such keen business ability so genuinely imbued with the real spirit of social service, ooe who regards industrial betterment as practically beneficial to his employds and conse- quently the truest means of promoting the lest interests of the business. Briefly, the policy of the firm is to maintain suchl conditions in the factory that all employes may find "work to bottle spontaneous expres- sion of their best impulses."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-12-02_WidnesExaminer_art0038" n="art0038">
        <head>ALWAYS WELCOME IN WARRINGTON.</head>
        <p>ALWAYS WELCOME IN WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>THE...</p>
        <p>WHITE ROSE.</p>
        <p>By GERALD BISS.</p>
        <p>CHAPTER XXIX.</p>
        <p>WITHIN a few minutes Sir Hugo Verschoyle himself was round at Half Moon Street.</p>
        <p>His first step was to place two special con- stables on duty outside the house and to establish an inspector in the hall, which had been lighted up. . . .</p>
        <p>Then- he asled to see Dina. Though very pale and shaken, she had re- covered her nerve.</p>
        <p>"Thank you for coming so 'promptly," she said, as;he took her extended hand. "You did quite right to send round to my private house," he replied. " This is a most terrible and mystcrio Is business. - Tell me your story." Bina told him ,all she knew in a few words, and he listened attentively without comment.</p>
        <p>" Humph," he said meditatively, when she had finished. " What a desperate, dastardly attempt But what a mercy ,you and Mike were at hand ! "</p>
        <p>"Poor old Mike," said the girl softly, show- ing weakness for the first time. Then she added, "God hleis him."</p>
        <p>" It helps to cpnfirm me in the idea," went on Sir Hugo, " that this '-White Rose' busi- ness is sonic deep-laid plot with a definite object, organised by some powerful body of persons—not the work cf an irresponsible lunatic."</p>
        <p>Bina started. Again the idea which had once or twice before crossed her mind flashed through, nd this time with more conviction, horrible though it was.</p>
        <p>" You sec," continued Verschoyle, who had not noticed her :sudden start, " these people, whoever they are, prefer to take such a desperate risk as .to-night's attempt to the' chance of Lord Ben Nevis's reaovery, when it is rox certain all will be revealed. I promise ycu that the house shall not be left un- wateied until we know the truth and have these ra urderers laid by the heels. I will have an inspector on guard, in the hall just outside the door and two poliCemen on the watch in the street.</p>
        <p>" Thank you," said Dina, "that will take a great weight off my mind." " And mine, too," said Sir Hugo grimly. Then he added " Did this ruffian, obviously disguised, suggest anyone to you ? Did you secm a recw;oise-him " " There did seem to be something familiat abaut him. arid I can't tell what. It may be imaf;Mation, but , I think it was the heavy eyebrows." •</p>
        <p>h- e;• ac- , nlaterl the chief of the police. " nut caret you imt a name to him ? Can't you suggest someone, however improbable ? It might help to put.u3 on the right track." " Frankly, I can't," answered I3ina. \rah a puzzled, loot in her eyes. " I have been</p>
        <p>worrying, about it ever since. It may come to me. and if it'does r vjl lct. you know with- on instant's del Iv.</p>
        <p>" Please do," sil Verschoyle rither dis ippointecliv. "Id give my soul to get on the track -(.1 thi3 linrrible plot. leave you now, as I want to go nnind to the yard, but I will come in again. litter," The night passed without any further alartn, and towards morning Bina yielded to the demands of nature and fell asleep. To her surpriSe, when she woke, she found her mother by her bedside.</p>
        <p>" Oh. mother dear," she said, eager to forget their last parting, " how good of you to com,.: 1 I knew you vold."</p>
        <p>The duchess j bent- dawn and kissed her hastily, " How is Ben ? " the girl asked eagerly. " Lord Ben Nevis is still unconscious, and there is no change." answered her mother ; but the doctors did not e,:pect any." Bina covered her eyes with her hand for an instant. Then she sat up in bed. "I must get up. Ben may need me at any moment. I've overslept myself. I'm afraid." " Your father akd I need you, too, dear," said. hmr mother. " Don't you remember about to-morrow ? "</p>
        <p>"No what abou tit ? " asked Bina without interest.</p>
        <p>"The King's-visit to us, of course. Surely y,on must remember ? " " Oh, I had forgotten. ITry,v unfortunate."</p>
        <p>" I v-;.m going 'aciwn to the Castle to-day tc rake all the final preparations." went on the duchess more sharply, " and I have come to Letch you."</p>
        <p>" Vetch me ? " echoed Bina in surprise. " I can't leave Ben."</p>
        <p>" Your father and I insist upon your being present for the royal visit," answered her 1 mother. " Apart :corn the fact that we reccgrlise no engagement between Lord Ben ; Nevis and you, he is in good hands, and you can do tiothing by remaining here in such a compromising. position, and we cannot allow Such discourtcsy to the King." " The King would not expect me Under the ' circumstances," said Dina, ignoring the first part of her mother's speech.. '" He is far too f,ymoci thetic." " The fact remains that we insist," repeated the duchess', growing annoycd. " Get up and conlr.. along with nie at once." , Mother," said the girl sadly, " how can '1 yoa be so unkind, so unlike •ourself ? Don't make me unhappy. I've, got enough to bear :</p>
        <p>Von are .making your father and myself very unhappy," reioined her mother angrily, feeling the h.c.rror her position. " Please don't argue further with me.''</p>
        <p>" I shan't argue. any more," answered Bina " but you may be sure that I shall not Lave Be:. He---he might die while I was ay.:ly. I shk:ll. xvr:te to the King myself ; Le'il understand, I know." duchess set her lip*, and her face grew very Nv hit e.</p>
        <p>" Once and for all, do you mean to defy your father and me ? " she asked in a low voice, full of concentrated. Passion: " You force me to," was the girl's answer. " Why. oh why, are on so unkind ? " . 4 . .</p>
        <p>Without another word the duchess turned on her heel and Lit the room, biting her lip. She was not in the habit of being defied, still less of being beaten, and, above all, she hated herself and the part she was forced to play. Bina's defiance tas practically the death- knell of the conspiracy she 'had sacrificed so much for, and she realised her mistake too late to draw back. Now she knew that Sir Andrew Wentworth would get nd chance to save the situation. . In a few minutes Bins, white in face and heavy at heart, was at Ben's bedside. He was still lying unconscious. "No change, your' ladyship," said the nufse softly. " hut the doctors say that Lord nen Nevis is going on all right. So we didn't disturb yoh, as you needed, your sleep and will require all the strength you have, before his lordship is well again.' " Thank you," said Bina 'simply, as she sat down to watch with hope and fear in her heart. ' An hour later she heard someone enter the front room and she rose to see who it was.</p>
        <p>It turned out to be Charlie, looking very glum. He kissed his sister warmly. " Buck ttp, old girl," he said, with rough sympathy. "It will ho all right. But," he went on less cheerily, " I've come to tell you that I shall have to leave you this afternoon. The fat's fairly in the fire at home, and the guv'nor is tearing up and down the library like a-caged lion, storming and raging furiously. Even the mater can do nothing with him. She, too, is frightfully upset about your refusal to leave Ben for the King's visit. I sympathise with you myself and think you are quite right ; but it ,means I've got to go, whethei I want to or not.. And," he con- cluded gruffly, "I feel such an unspeakable beast, leaving you and poor old Ben ; hut there's no option." "Dear old Charlie," whispered the girl, kissing him affectionately, "you are a dear, and I know you can't help it. Go and make the best of it for me at homo." '</p>
        <p>" You can bet y-our soul I will," he res- ponded warmll 1,1 There's somethine at thf</p>
        <p>"IT IS ABLE TO SUPPORT LIFE."</p>
        <p>COCOA</p>
        <p>IS more nourishment</p>
        <p>_ 10 cups of any ordinary cocoa, and isabsoufelyfree</p>
        <p>from chemicals. NOURISH ES,_</p>
        <p>WARMS, STRENGTHENS,</p>
        <p>bottom of this business I can't fathom ;bu we are both grown uo now, and I don't set why we should be sat on. I don't see wb; yon should be made Unhappy tor some fad of the guy'nor's or the mater's." . .</p>
        <p>And for the first time in their lives the brother and the sister, faced with a really serious problem, showed the Hampstead temper ; and it was clear that the duchess's determination had come out, though hithertc unsuspected, in the younger generation. "We'll work them between us after all," were Charlie's parting words. "So don't fre; Bina, old girl, and mind you wire me constant- ly about old Ben." After he had gone the girl sat down and wrote to the King who had always been especially gracious to her since she had been a child. In her desoair and misery she felt that re was not a ttme to stand upon slily ceremony. C'urther, she had taken the responsibility of the situation upon her own shoulders and was unwilling to draw back. . She had hardly despatched the letter before Sir Hugo Verschoyle entered. She greeted him eagerly, as she had grown to look upon him as an old friend. "Any news ? " she asked anxiously. He shook his head negatively. " How is the patient ? " " Just the same, but no worse." she an- swered. "In fact the doctors say better, as this stillness gives the heart a better chance of healing." "Thank God ! " ejaculated Sir Hugo, fer- vently; and Bina, craving for sympathy, .nstinctively warmed to him still more. "Aunt has had to go out to get some things," she explained as they sat down. He nodded.</p>
        <p>"Have you thought at all who the dis- guised man could be ? " he asked, plunging into the middle of things. "1 can't fix him, somehow," Bina answered, knitting hee brows: " His name seems to be on the tip of my tongue one minute and then it eludes me, but I hope it will come back."</p>
        <p>" It's very annoying," said Verschoyle, " but don't worry ; it can't be helped. It may come to you any minute; and it might be very useful. Of one thing I am certain, Lady :Sabina. and that is that he was in all probability the man who in tile first instance stabbed Lord llen Nevis. Do you know how ac got into the house ? " " No," she answered ; " I was so excited that I never really thought." " Well, last night I searched Lord Ben Nevis's dressing- table and pockets but could find no sign of a latch-key. Obviously the assassin stabbed him in the back just as he was puttirg the key into the door, and he must lhavr picked it up by instinct. Bence his easy entrance ; and probably he hid in that great curtain in the hall, waiting his opportunity. What is more, he must have known the house or something about it. But otherwise the thing is shrouded in mystery. However, these few facts are making me seriously con- sider a different class of conspirator." Bina started as the irritating and wholly ridiculous thought she could not dismiss again intruded. He sat talking over various aspects of the case for a little while longer and then left, promising to call again. and making Bins in her turn promise to send for him insta.ntly should Ben Nevis show signs of consciousness or become delirious. The day drawzed slowly on ; but Lady Crizelle's indomitable cheerfulness kept Bina from brooding. It was only broken by the doctor's visits, but in the late afternoon Bins heard a horse stop at the door.</p>
        <p>It turned out if) be em orderly sent bv tit King himself with a svmpathetic not: fr Bina, and orders to enquire after the ivitient.</p>
        <p>The graciousness of the action and th€ kindle ac,.':,•ptance of Ii -r apology put‘new lt into the girl, aml she felt that it would help Li put things right at honic more than anything • So she sat straight down and wrote a letter to her mother, telling her enthusiastically of the King's kinkiness, and endeavouring, to makeup the quarrel of the morning which had distressed her so. " Believe me. mother dear," she wrote, " if I did not love Ben more than life, if it woull not have broken my heart to leave him, whea I May lose him altogether, I wouldn't lutve defied you: Forgive me and never be lisrsh to me again. Ido not,love you and father less for loving Ben more." That 'night she insisted on sitting up with Ben.</p>
        <p>_ "I'll see how he goes on. auntie, she ar. pied with a pathPtic eagerness, " and perhme; I'll go to bed a little later." So Lady Grizelle shook her head and gave in with an affectionate kiss. - '</p>
        <p>" Poor child," she murmured, " I should have done the same myself at her age." Bina sat by the bed in a warm white dress- ing gown, holdim; his hand as though with the fixed intention of saying him. When the clock struck midnight she remembered that it was time to call the nurse to give him some 11:m641:neut. She went into the front room to speak to her and paused. for a minute by the tire, lean- ing against the mantelpiece. On it lay a calling card, which she picked up idly. , Branksea," she murmured, and then in an . instant it flashed across her. • _</p>
        <p>.... She felt as though the excitement would choke her. She knew the assassin now ; it was Prank- sea 1 She knew him by • the eyebrows: nod the card had supplied the hint her mind had been groping after all day. At that moment She heard the sound of a voice, feeble and indistinct, from the back TOOM.</p>
        <p>------- 4' Send the inspector for Sir Hugo Ver- schoyle as quickly as possible," she 'whisper,..,l eagerly to the nurse, as she darted back late the other room.</p>
        <p>—Lancet. • ;0, • ONE CUP OF 4-• • 4 wu.'4 ,4544 " 10 contain.</p>
