      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-12-02_WidnesExaminer_art0039" n="art0039">
        <head>CHAPTER XXX.</head>
        <p>CHAPTER XXX.</p>
        <p>WHEN Bina reached the bedside Ben was groaning and murmuring incoherently. He had moved slightly and showed signs of rest- lessness.</p>
        <p>The girl took his feverish hand in hers again, and ts coolness seemed to quiet him temporarily. He lay still once more with no sound but a slight moaning. She sat down again beside him with her face white and drawn and a feeling Of corn- pressioq round her heart. She realised that there all alone in the commonplace little room she stood on the brink of the awful mystery which had convulsed the country, face to face with the unknown, which intuitively she dreaded. But it was better; she felt, that of all people she should be the first to know, if her intuition should prove correct. What she feared she hardly knew, but she was painfully conscious of an unspeakable horror in her heart. -</p>
        <p>! Suddenly a thought entered her head, and I she sent the nurse for a pencil and some paper. " Wait next door, nurse," she. said in a quiet voice, which showed no trace of the tumult within : " I will call you if I want on. Send Sir Hugo Verschoyle in the instant he arrives." Then she resumed her watch. It was fully ten minutes before Ben spoke again. " Bina . my poor darling ! " he mnr- mured, and with a sob in her throat the girl wrote it down. Her feelings were Mixed. On the one side there was a glow of triumphant th,mght that his first words had</p>
        <p>been of her, and on the other they seemed to hint that there was trouble ahead for her. Another long interval, which seemed to Bina hours and hours, and then she distinctly caught the words, "white rose." Soon after she had recorded them faithfully on the paper, she heard a hurried step, ane Sir Hugo Verschoyle, half dressed and wrap. ped up in a heavy fjir coat, entered the room. Motioning to him to be silent. Bina handed hint the paper. He read the words two oi three times carefully and nodded his approval to her, evidently pleased at her thoughtful- ness.</p>
        <p>Then, drawing up another chair, he took a seat beside her to watch and to listen. "The doctor? " he whispered in a low voice. "Has been sent for," answered Bina, "and will be here in a few minutes?' A strange lock flitted across his keen face which suggested his eagerness that the sick man should have time to reveal the hidden</p>
        <p>secret before an opiate should silence him. . .</p>
        <p>It was a mental baitle between the man and the detective. the intellectual and the human. He had, however, n.,t bong to wart as each moment the incoherrurt words grew more frequent and the sufferer more re.siless. .</p>
        <p>"-White rose . . • . Litl3. . . .mur- der . . . horrible . . . fools . . . idiots . . . plot . . poor darling . . . Hampstead House . . • King, my God, what shall I do ? . . Oh, the fools . . the duke mad . . . Lir. Stuart . . . the duchess, too . . . God forgive . . . Bonnie Prince . . Branksea . . . br,lt for life . . serious . . . all mad . . . . . . - white rose' . . cold moon . . . motor . . murder."</p>
        <p>And so on the words began dropping .thick and fast from his lips, Sir Ilugo writing them sown as thcmgh in a dream. struck dumb at the astounding solution of the situation, made so clear by these incoherent words. In a flash the whoe dread secret chWned upon him, taking his breath 'away, but he continued to hear and write mechanically. 'lt was excusable, he felt, that h detective should have missed .the clues, obvious though they now seemed. It would have been a sheer im- possibility to suspect such a thing I. .</p>
        <p>Bina, meanwhile, stood as though turned ta stone, her face as white as a winding-sheet, and a look of horror in her dark eyes. She, too, was struck dumb and szetned only :lolly conscious of What was going on. But each word sank into her heart like a drop of molten lead, branding and burning its way into her very existence. The two watchers exchanged neither a word nor a glance, as the minutes sped on. Such part of them as was not concentrated upon the delirious ,words of the sick man seemed In ralysed, and their thoats were too dry for speech. It was a strange scene. The stugla lamp was deeply shaded, and the light in the room dim. The flames of the fire flickered grotesquely upon the ceiling. The far side of the room and the corners were shrouded in gloom, as though it were twilight, while the lied itsolf was the lightest spot from the reflection of its own white linen.</p>
        <p>On it lay the sick man, haggard and un- shaven, his hair cut short, and his moustache tousled and unliciupt. His blue pyjamas lent a tint of colour, breaking the monotony of the bed-clothes, and in his pale face the eyes burnt like two blazing coals. Beside him stood the girl with white face,, old and drawn, and set, staring eyes, and her white, soft dressing-gown gave her a ghost-like look. Nearer the foot of the bed .sat the other man, engrossed with the, paper in front of him and writing hurriedly as each word dropped from the sick man's lips. His dress, too, was un- conventional. A fur coat with the collar turned up obviously concealei the shortcom- ings of a hurried toilet beneath. As he leant forward, the light glinted across his high cheek bones and Roman nose, showing a pair of closely compressed lips and knitted brows, on which the veins stood out in knots. And in the dim room there was no sound but the uneven voice of the delirious man followed by the soft scratching of a pencil on paper. The situation was too tense to last, and it was a relief when the doctor entered. In his hand was a hypodermic syringe. Bina -pidly gave way to him and the nurse, and Sir Hugo .rose from his seat with his precious paper in his hand. By common Consent they went into the front room without uttering a word. Bina went over to the fire. A shiver passed through her frame despite the warm room, and a low moan of horror forced its way between her tightly shut 'teeth. In an instant Sir Hugo, who had been watching her with a look of grave pity in his sympathetic grey eyes, which criminals called cold, was at her side.</p>
        <p>He took one of her nerveless, unresisting hands in his.</p>
        <p>"Forgive me," he said gently, "hut adver- sity makes friends of us all. God knows how I feel for you I " " God help me," murmured the girl almost inarticulately, " what shall I do ? " The man stood silent in the face of the question. The hopelessness of the situation paralysed him as it paralysed her, and had paralysed Ben Nevis, and lie, too, wanted to think and could not.</p>
        <p>Bina broke the silence, speaking almost passionately. " It's that committee. The disguised man was Lord Branksea ; it came to me in a flash 1 I see it all now, and now I know who the mysterious ' Mr. Stuart ' is and why they don't want me to marry Ben 1" ' Tell me about this ' Mr. Stuart,' " said the great detective quickly; and Bala told him all she knew, speaking rapidly. " Humph," he murmured softly, as the doctor entered the room, all unconscious of the tragedy which had been going on under his nose.</p>
        <p>" Lord Ben Nevis is quite quiet now," he said to Bina in a low voice, "and I hope he will sleep till morning. I have left him la charge of the nurse and wish you to to to bed, please, Lady Sabina." The girl made a movement of protest but Sir Hugo, gently assuming command, inter- vened.</p>
        <p>" Please insist," he said, " as Lady Sabina is greatly overwrought by what has just occurred in the next room. But lam certain that without a sleeping draught she will not sleep, so may I suggest one ? " "T had intended to give one anyhow," answered Dr. Sholto pleasantly. "You will break down if you don't obey my orders, and I shan't allow you in the room at all, Lady Sabina."</p>
        <p>. He went into the back room and returned with something in a glass. "By the way," he said, " Sir Andrew Wentworth, my old friend and colleague, has expressed a particular -desire to see Lord Ben Nevis, in whose case he is profoundly in- terested. He may be of great assistance to us in managing what is a very difficult and anxious case."</p>
        <p>" Yes, certainly," said Bina gratefully. Then suddenly her manner changed abruptly, and she spoke in an unnatural hard voice and with considerable decision. " No, cer- tainly not, I mean, Dr. Sholto ; on no account must he enter the house. He is one of the :onunittee," she added to Sir Hugo in a low voice.</p>
        <p>To Dr. Sholto her sudden change of front was an enigma, but he ascribed it to over- wrought nerves.</p>
        <p>" lust as you wish, of course," he answered soothingly, handing her the glass. Now, please drink this." " Drink it," said Verschoyle kindly, but firmly, " and I'll see that he doesn't come. - I'll do my best about the other matter and :ome in to see you to-morrow morning after I have seen the, Home Secretary, if you will go to bed."</p>
        <p>"Thank you," said the girl gratefully, as the drank the opiate. " I'm tired, and I ;an't think. go to bed. I think." Then she swayed and would have fallen forward if Sir Hugo had not caught her in his strong arms.</p>
      </div>
