      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-12-02_WidnesExaminer_art0060" n="art0060">
        <head>EXAMINER.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER.</p>
        <p>DECEMBSR 2nd 1905.</p>
        <p>Mr. ARNOLD-FORSTER THE WAR OFFICE A.ND proves how true a • THE VOLUNTEERS. Britoil he is by never admitting that he is beaten. He reprssents the new attitude which he has assumed to the Volunteer movement as a triumph of his policy, whereas it is in fact a climb down. The medical examination he observes has taught the Army Council all it wanted to know. Of 187,003 Volunteers who have been examined •some 28,030 were pronounced to be medically unfit. Unfit for what ? This is a vastly important question, to which the War Secre- tary considers it inconvenient to refer. The circular ordering the medical examination of members of the Volunteer force was distinctly and unquestionably illegal. S') much RS this has since been admitted. Yet there were command- ing officers who expelled men from their regiments because they would not submit to an examination the proclaimed object of which was to test their 'fitness for service abroad. There would have been no difficulty of any sort about a medical examina- tion if its purpose had not been such as was stated in the notorious circular. The Volunteers are not required for service abroad, but for ernergcies at home, and Mr. ARNOLD_ .FORiTER had no more right to order an examina- tion into their fitness fot foreign service than his colleague, the First Lord of the Admiralty, has to revive the press gang. Of the 25,000 men who are pronounced to belmedically unfit, we are not even now told whether they are merely unfit for work which they never undertook to do or whether they are unfit for the ordinary work of a Volunteer force. In the latter case, of course, they should be required to retire ; but if the verdict pronounced upon them means nothing more that that they are unfit for service abroad it ought to be ignored, as it certainly will be by Mr. ARNOLD-FORSTER'S successor. We have come to the end, as it would now appear, of the system of .harassing the Volunteers with the view of familiar- ising the minds of the people with the idea of con- -scription. Volunteering is now to be encouraged after a fashion. The kind of encouragement given is, however, open to strong objection. It is the Volunteer going into camp who gets all the new advantages that are offered. Mr. Ar.soLo-FonsrEn explains that the corps which increase their camp attendance will gain in grants, and that the corps which diminish their camp attendance will lose. Now, camp attendance is not, and ought not to he made, the test of efficiency There are many thousaods of Volunteers—as many, say, as underwent the medical examination—to ; whom attendance in camp is a simple impossibil_ ity. An immediate consequence of leaving work for the pnrpose of living in camp for a week- or a fortnight would be loss of employment. Even Mr. ARNOLD-FORSTER might be expected to recognise this, though he carries his head in the cheids more than is reasonable. A Volunteer may be fully efficient without going to, Salisbury Plain, or elsewhere, for camping purposes, and -efficency should meet every requirement, and be the only test to belapplied. To reduce the grants of those corps which cannot send a certain num- ber of men into camp would be to perpetrate a -decided wrong. There may be local reasons why a ••corps cannot recruit men capable of camp attend ance, and yet such a corps may be one of the most 'efficient in the service. The truth is that Mr. ARNoLD•Foasr en is still hankering to turn the 'Volunteer force into something else, and the Volunteers, whose number has greatly diminished under the rule of the present War Secretary, are to he congratulated on the fact that very little , time now remains to him for further exasperating 'experiments.</p>
        <p>.</p>
        <p>•. , Mr. Chamberlain PROTECTIONIsT FARE. and his trusty hench- men are fond of statis- . 'tics, but we doubt whether they will make any Ilse of the German official returns showing the •consumption of various " meats 'in that paradise of Protection. Lest they forget, we will just -•- record the fact that pork, which may tie considered °tie of the staple forms of diet, grows ever dear in Girmany, and the number of slaughtered swine has decreased in a year from, roughly, three and a Ilalf million beasts to three million. Tnen what are the nenple eating ? Official figures now show that '25,907 horses have been slaughtered during the year, against 23,827 last year, though it is</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-12-02_WidnesExaminer_art0061" n="art0061">
        <p>worth noting that these figures do not include horses slaughtered for food by private persons which would make the increase in horse consump- tion still more pronounced. Again 1,017 dogs were slaughtered for human food, against 762, in 1904. People who are inclined to be led away by Mr. Chamberlain's eloquence and statistical jugglery should try this diet. They will then get a fairer idea of what Protection means.</p>
        <p>The enthusiasm which</p>
        <p>_ LIBERAL WOMEN. is pervading all the ranks of the Liberal party is being evidenced in a marked degree by the striking progress of the Women's Liberal Association. Unfortunately women have not the Parliamentary vote, but by their influence they , can do is great deal for the great cause of Liberal- ism. The members of the Association are thoroughly realizing that fact and are entering , into the work of the organization with a vigour and earnestness which augur well for the coming campaign. The success of Monday's fusction at the club was really remark- able,the attendance being so large that the reading room was crowded for three concerts. Mr. A. 11. Crosfield had a very cordial reception and his remarks which were brief but to the point were ! listened to with manifest interesr. He empha- sised the immense importance to women of the maintenance of Free Trade for, as chancellors of the home exchequer, they would he the first to feel the effects of higher prices. This is a point which should always be borne in mind in defend- ing our fiscal policy and Mr. Crosfield may rest assured that the members of the Liberal Women's Association will do all in their power to win the battle for Free Trade.</p>
        <p>The question of a THE MINIMUM WAGE minimum " living " QUESTION wage for the Cor- poration employees will come before the Town Council again, at the meeting on Tuesday evening next. It will be remembered that at the September meeting Councillor Stott brought forward a motion to the effect that no able-bodied man in the employ of the Corponition be paid less than 2.15. per week. This proposal was stigmatised as outrageous by Alderman Smethurst, and criticised in various quarters. Finally it received only six votes and was defeated. Councillor Barton has now taken the matter up and the agenda contains the more , reasonable proposal by him of a 21s. minimum ' and a week's holiday once a year for each man with two year's service, or more. There is a reasonable prospect of a 21s. minimum being incorporated in the Standing Orders some time, if net on Tuesday next. A Corporation ought to be a model employer, and it should not be' neces- sary thi pass special resolutions in order to secure a wage of 21s. per week for each able-bodied man in the municipal service. The description" able- bodied," however, adds to the difficulties of the question, for the Corporation employs men who are not all able-bodied, and who are not ill-paid for the work they do at a less wage than 21s. per week. The various aspects of the question will have to be thoroughly thrashed out before the resolution can be agreed to, even 'ay those who may be pre- disposed in favour of it. We hope to hear a really good case presented this time, for on the previous occasion Councillor Stott' s motion was over. whelmed by the weight of argument and opinion on the other side.</p>
        <p>1 His Worship the Mayor is THE "JUDICIAL one of those men who must HUMORIST." have his little joke. If he kept his jocularity for private consumption, only his friends could complain, but when he jokes officially and about serious things, it is getting high time the Mickado's Lord High Executioner put him "on the list." Post- prandial orations provide his Worship with occa- sions on which he excels himself—iinfortunately. At the Mayoral dinner—when there was some excuse for him saying just what he liked—he ridiculed his own councillors. That they did not resign in a body has ever since been a matter of surprise to us. Last night (Tniirsday) the Mayor had his fling once. more in the course of the Caledonian Association's annual dinner. Pity it is, that he did not imbibe a little of the Celtic caution before he res- ponded for the "Town and trade of Warring ton." This is a serious and dignified theme, but his Worship treated it in his most facetious vein. There are agitators in the Town Coined : the Mayor says an agitator "used his own brains for the benefit of his own pocket, and that is his living." He thinks that the ideas of such a person should be ignored. Can it be wondered at,there• fore, that the " Agitators" ignored his re-election ? After expressing disgust at the way " things dre going on," His Worship proceeded to poke fan at the newly appointed Distress Committee, and the Echicatiou Committeee. The latter he charged with having done little but put is. on the rates—which is not true either in substance or in fact. It must be a puzzle to the ratepayers to know what Alderman I3olton means by speeches of this kind, but they will, we think, he convinced by this that as a "judicial humorist" he " never would be missed ! "</p>
        <p>NOTES AND NOTIONS. An old woman charged with begging at St. Helens on Thursday said she had come from Runcorn, over the " animated transporter bridge."</p>
        <p>* * * The newly-erected isolation hospital at 'Dutton boasts of one matron, one caretaker, one nurse, one medical officer, awl one patient. A happy family truly ! * * The" largest vessel " has come up the Ship Canal to Manchester this week. She is the twin-screw, 10,300 ton steamer Ayrshire, and measures 460 feet by 59ft. With .a full complement, of passengers on board for Austiclia and New Zealand she will come down the Canal on Sunday morning, probably passing Latchford about noon. . .</p>
        <p>A. Warlington correspondent has been writing to the " Daily Dispatch" to tell all about the intelligence and capabilities of his eighteen-months, old retriever bitch. She goes to the newsagent's every morning for her master's paper and with a can and a mat bag calls for the milk and potatoes. Then in the evening she travels over 300 yards for the supper beer.</p>
        <p>A world-famous man, in-the person of the Bev. T. H. Harris, will preach at Golbornerstrect Baptist Church to-morio* evening. Mr. Harris is the minister who was an eye-witness of many of the Congo atrocities under the rule of King Leopold of the Belgians, and has since done so much to expose the crimes perpetrated in the name of civilization. * * * Protectionists have been vainly endeavouring for some time tl exploit the distress of the unemployed, but the height of impudence is surely reached by the Tory poster in the New Forest which proclaims that "every vote given to a Radical means another British workman out of a job." Fortunately the feverish days when such political fairy tries were effective have passed into the limbo of obscurity. * Inquiries made by the Bolton Education Com- mittee show that in twelve schools there are 266 juvenile smokers. The committee have drawn up a circular for distribution in the schools of evil effects of juvenile smoking, and the attention of the parents is also called to the matter. If inquiries were made among the Warrington newsbori the returns would show, we think, an abnormally high percentage.</p>
        <p>* Sonlethinr, has happened to disturb the bucolic minds of the people of Winwick. On Thursday a stranger descended in their midst and held forth on " E:Shrephtenominology the Science of Letters by their phenomenal outlined forms and inalecular shape in nature." We hope the inhabitants were edified and availed themselves of the opportunity of discuss- ing such a mementous subject. * * * Mr. A. H. Crosfield (the president) will preside at the annual dinner of the Warrington and District Bowling League at the Lion Hotel on Thursday even- ing next, when the Championship cap and medals will bn handed to the winning Ball's Head team, and medals to the runners-up. Bowling is increasingly popular in Warrington and there is no more respected patron of the sport than the prospective Liberal candidate for the Borough.</p>
      </div>
