      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-12-02_WidnesExaminer_art0063" n="art0063">
        <head>Examiner, Saturday, December 2, 1905.</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, December 2, 1905.</p>
        <p>"After the morning bath take a deep breath, retain it as low, as possible, then slowly expire." Such is the ad—ice given by a professor of physical develop- ment, according to the British Medical Journal."</p>
        <p>* * * The Warrington Distress Committee showed a lack of consideration for the Press representatives at their inaugural meeting on Monday. The scribes waited in an ante-room for upwards of an hour pending the decision of the committee as to whether the meeting should be public or not. At the end of that time they were advised to take their departure as the com- mittee had not at that stage embarked upon the dis- cussion as to their admission or otherwise. The com- mittee might at least have, entertained the matter earlier. _ _</p>
        <p>The market value of Protection is rapidly falling, and is already only half price. A publication from the pen of C. A. 'Vince, M.A., of the Tariff Reform League, entitled " Mr. Chamberlain's Proposals," and originally published at one shilling, is being advertised for sale on the bookstall at one of the railway stations in Manchester as follows : • MR. CHAMBERLAIN'S PROPOSALS.. HALF PRICE. Go. EACII. • * * One hears of pecular missives being received in strange ways at times, bat the experience of a Widnes greengrocer is perhaps unique. On opening a barrel of apples he found a letter amongst them from a young lady in Smith Utiion, Maine, U.S.A.' which read as follows :—' I put this letter in the barrel of apples hoping to receive a letter from the one who opens it. It will be such a novelty to get such a letter from across the ocean. If you should write to me please tell me what customs you observe at Christmas. I should like to introduce something new in these parts. —Yours truly, Miss Baisca V. PHILBROOK, South Union, Maine.''</p>
        <p>* * * There has been an interesting discussion in the " Manchester Guardian " lately relative to the origin of the familiar phrase "Grand Old Man." The only point that seems to be established is that the phrase is much older than in its application to Mr. Gladstone. " A little story that most people have forgotten may, or may not, help to fix the date of the arpropriate of the title of " Grand Old Man" to Mr. Gladstone. Once when the arrival of Mr. Gladstone was expected at a certain town an enthusiastic Conservative happened to be in the railway station. Lying on the platform he noticed a portmanteau, the sight of which seemed to rouse him to fury. He fell upon it fiercely and slashed it to pieces with his pocketknife. It bore the hated initials—" G. 0.11."</p>
        <p>CRAWFORD'S</p>
        <p>MAYFAIR"hb</p>
        <p>A NEW BISCUIT FOR AFTERNOON TEA.</p>
        <p>BISCUITS.</p>
        <p>• The Diocesan C.E.T.S. Bazaar. The receipts at tilt; recent Bazaar in St. George's Hall, Liverpool, in aid of the Diocesan Church of England Temperance Society, the Police Court and Prison Gate Mission Sic amount to about £2,100. This sum will be slightly increased from various sources before the accounts are closed. Galvanized Booting 'Sheets, Roofing Felts, tcc. Large stock.—J. S. GARLAND, 51 to 55, Bridge at., Warrington.—Advt. A Dangerous Crossing. A rather serious accident happened at the London and North western Railway Crossing at the end of Gatewarth-street, near Sankey Bridges Station, on Monday evening. At about half past five a youth named Graham, who is employed by Mr. Benjamin Grimshaw, Gatewarth Farm, was returning from town in charge of a pony and milk float and on reaching the crossing he opened the gates. The horse and cart had almost passed to the other side when the boy in accordance with his usual custom recrossed the lines to close the gate. While doing so an engine from the direction of Warrington approached, and dashed into the float, smashing it almost to pieces. The horge was knocked round and fell to the ground breaking its leg and back; and it had to be shot later in the evening. This is the second accident which has occurred:in recent years at these crossings.</p>
        <p>vPUBLIC BENEFIT. UNDER THE CLOCK FOR BEST BOOTS IN LANCASHIRE.—AdVt. Good Templary.—A New Lodge Instituted. For some time it has been felt that there was need for a further extention of the order of Good Templary in Warrington and on Tuesday evening a new lodge was instituted by Bro. G. Clarke, D.E.S. in the Horse- market-street Cocoa Roams. 10 members joined the lodge and there was a good number of visitors from Earlestown, Widnes, Latchford, and from the " Temperance Mission " lodge. It was decided to call the lodge the "J. A. Lee Memorial " and to meet on Tuesday. The officers for the first quarter were elected as follows :—C.T. Bro. D. Smith, V.T. Sister Mrs. Sheath,chaplain,Sister M. Brobbin, Marshall, Bro. Groves, D.M. Sister E. Brandwood, Guard, Sister Stringer, Sentinel, Sister Matthews, Treasurer, Bro. Hughes, Financial Secretary, Sister Horne, P.C.T. and L.D., Bro.' slaidment. During the evening, encour- aging speeches were delivered by the instituting officer, Bro. J. Sheath (Widnes Consolation), Bro. Roberts (Temperance Mission) and Bro. Dale (Latchford).</p>
        <p>Bold-street Young Men's Bible Class. The Rev. Joseph Mniiing delivered an address at the Bold-street Wesleyan Young Men's Bible Class on Sunday on "Abstainers for the sake of others." The speaker said the spread of joint stock enterprise in connection with the brewing industry was doing much to hinder the work of temperance reformers. When a man became directly interested in the trade, said the speaker, they might ha sure that he would do all in his power to its Interests. An interesting discussion followed in which the class took part.</p>
        <p>PUBLIC BENEFIT.-UNDER THE CLOCK P.OR BEST BOOTS IN LANCISIIIRE.—Advt.</p>
        <p>Cycling Club Social. A social evening and dance in connection with the Cable Works Cycling Club was held at the Winmar- leigh Cafe on Monday, at which there were present about 60 members of the staff. A most enjoyable pro- gramme of vocal and instrwnental music was given by the members of the club, and after a short interval dancing commenced. A presentation was made •on behalf of the members to Mr. Salthouse, the late hon. secretary, who is leaving the town. Mr. Clements, the vice-president, referred to the enthusiasm which Mr. Salthouse created and inaintaitiod from the very inception of the club. That function, he said, was a proof, if proof were needed, of the spirit of sociability prevailing amongst the staff of the company. Iron Fencing of every description. Lowest prices.—I. S. GARLAND, 51 to 55, Bridge st., Warrington.—Advt. Newsagent's Shop Gutted. A serious fire occurred on Tuesday morning at 3, Scotland-road, a nemsagant's lock-np stoop. kept by Mrs. Dickenson. Fier buil) tad had visited the shop a little earlier, for the purpose of obtaining possession of a bag with which to fetch the morning newspapers, and at that time there was, no sign of a fire anywhere. NO match was .struck. Shortly afterwards flames broke out, and despite the energies of the fire brigade which was quickly oa the scene, under the direction of the Superintendent and Deputy Superintendent, the whole of the contents of the shop were soon destroyed and a large amount of damage was (lane to the building itself. The origin of the fire is a mystery. The damage, whieh is estimated at several hundred pounds, is partly covered by insurance.</p>
        <p>Local Oils!fellowship. A meeting of the " Lap.' Garibaldi " Lodge of the National 1.0. of Odeßellows (Warrington District) was held at the Blue Lion, Market-place, on Saturday Bro. John Wooley, N.G., presided. The question of being represented at the next A.M.C. to be held at Berwick was freely discussed also whether a member should be nominated for the Executive Committee. It was pointed out that the district had been honoured by having a member on the executive, ant eventually it was decided to send a notice of motion to the District Committee that the district be represented at the A.M.C., and that it candidate be adopted for the executive. Another important matter was that of members being expelled who had been convicted of felony. Nominations for officers resulted in P.G. JO3. W. Perry being appointed noble grand ; Bra. Richard Dannett, vice grand ; and Bro. George Hart, elective secretary. During the evening a vote of sympathy was passed to the lodge treasurer, Bro. Smith, on the loss of his wife. ASK for CUNNINGHAM'S HIGH-CLASb UINERA.L WATNII.S.—Advt, Parish Church Tea Party. The annual congreg itional gathering in connection with the Warrington Parish Church was' held in the Parr Hall on Thhrsday evening and was in every wiy a great success. An Jexcellent tea was provided, the wants of about 1,103 diners being ably superintended by ladies connected with the parish. After tea a dance was held, and pending the clearing of the body of the hall for this purpose a cloncert was given in the crush room. At this an excellent musical programme Was given •by Messrs. PI M. Darby and W. Brandreth, pianoforte duet; W. Mennen and W. Wadsworth, 'duets ; Miss Starkey, mandoline solos ; Miss. Lester, coon songs ; the Misses Lester, Japanese fan dance ; land the choir boys of the Parish Church who gave two part songs. At the conclusion of the concert dancing was commencei and despite the - somewhat crowded state of the room, was kept up with zest until midnight, the music being supplied by Mr. IL Anderton's band. _ _</p>
        <p>Oil Stoves, Coke Stoves, Lamps, Zco.—.T. S. Garmalio, 51, to 55, Bridge St., Warrington.—Advt. Band of Hope Concert. The Sankey Bridges Wesleyan schoolro )111, was filled' almost to overflowing on Monday evening when the first concert of the se ism in counestion with the Rand of Hope was held. Mr. R. Nelson presided and de- livered an address setting forth the evils of intemper- ance in a most interesting manner. The following programme wts rendered by the members of the Dalai of Hope and friends :—Ftecitations bytNlaggie Wood- . ward, Annie Sankey, May Summerfield, May Butler, Lily Wilding, Annie Lightfoot, Fred flatfield and Miss A. Furness ; Songs by the Misses A. Furness, E. Woodland, and Mr. E. Walker ; mandoline solos by Miss A. Routledge. A.quartette party from the Warrington Concertina Band gave selections from " Maritatia " and " Poet and Peasant," The concluding item was a hurnottrous dialogue entitled " Wanted a wife," the follawing taking part: . —The Misses L. Hardy, L. Johnson, E. Suellow, R. Cartledge, L. Wilkes, and Messrs P. Jacksm and W. Wilkes. On the motion of Mr. J, Corradine seconded by Mr. S. Blythe a vote of thanks was passed to all who had taken part in the proceed- ings. The singing of the Doxology brought a mast . successful gathering to a close: Mr. E. G. Rickers ably ! acted as accompanist., . Sale of Work at Latchford. - -</p>
        <p>The annual sale of work in connecti et with Christ . Church,Latchford,waslheld in Pali sit room,Gmppeahall . road, on Saturday, and was opened in tits presence of a large gathering by Mrs. Peter Rylands. The Rev. :G, C. Briggs, the vicar, who presided said the sale was being held in aid in the first pLace of the endow- ment fund. At present the endowmentovas :£lOl a : year and they ought not to be satisffed until they had brought it up to ii2Cll. (Appian:is). 'rite new gate and pathway another object of the sale was a very necessary itnprovemeat, especially since the church lind been enlarged. (114 tr, hear). —Mrs. Rylande gr ace fu!ly performs d the oin-3e allotted to her. —Mr. John Fairelongla proposed a vote of thanks to Mrs. Rylands.—Captain Waring seconded, and the motion was carried, Mrs. Itylands suitably: responding.—The following ladies had charge of the stalls : Plain work stall : Miss Fanny Smith, Mrs. John Fairclonah, and Mrs. William Tinsley. Fancy work stall : Mrs. Richard .Fairelough. Mr. George Dakin, Mrs. Manro, and Miss Massy' Toy stall : Mrs. Edwin Woods, Mrs. Venn, and Miss Atkins an. Flower stall : Mrs. and the Misses Broadbent, Refreshment stall : Mrs. James Fairclough, Mrs. Harrison, Mrs. Dakin, and Mrs. William Pierpoint. During the afternoon and evening concerts were given and much appreciated. The following took Dirt in them : Miss Fairctaigh, pianoforte solo; Mr. E. Fairclough, song ; Mr. Williams, violin solo ; Mh3,3 Briggs, recitation ; Mrs. Griffiths, song ; and Miss Norah Fairclaugh and. Mr. Williams, violin doe. The proceeds amounted to 41.'21. 134. 1(1. Warrington Class Teachers' Association The annual 'meeting of the 'Warrington Certificated Class Teachers' Association was held in the Wycliffe Memorial Building on Saturday morning, Mr. F. tong- shaw presiding over an excellent attendance. Mr. Jeffery, the secretary, presented an eacouraging report of the year's working. The balance sheet submitted, by Mr. T.Edden, showed that the association was in a sound financial position. The election of officers for the ensuing ye tr resulted as follows : —President, Mr. F. J. Emsoa ; Vice• President, qr. James Armstrong; Committee, Misses Brookes McGrath, Bailey; Messrs. I. Bowen, W. Cutland, awl J. E. Morgan; Treasurer, Mr. T. Edden ; ficretary, :qr. S. W. Jeffery. An excellent address was delivered by the newly elected president, who instanced the additional duties which had of recent years devolved upon the class teachers, largely in the direction of clerical work. He strongly advocated a reduction in the size of the classes paint- ing out that the average number in England was 71 whereas on the continent the highest number per class was 50. To teachers, in elementary schools, he said, the gliestion of largo and unwieldy classes was a pressing one and deserving of the attention of all those engaged in educational work with a view to its being remedied. However, mach good work had been done by their parent association—the National Federa- tion of Assistant Teachers—and if the certificated class teachers would continue to increase in the bond of unity. and always endeavour to carry out all thei i r duties n a conscientious manner, they had every confidence that their social position would still con- ( tinne to be on the upward grade, that they would inspire and merit more confidence than they did at present, and that their most pressing difficulties would gradually disappear. (Applause.) A hearty vote of thanks, proposed by Mr. F. Longahaw,secondeil by Mr. I. Bowen, and su pp irted lw Mr. S. Jeffrey, was accorded to Mr. Emeon for his address.</p>
        <p>Christina Presentß, Splendid New Stock.—. 7. S. GARLAND, 51 to 55, Bridge et., %Warrington.— Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-12-02_WidnesExaminer_art0064" n="art0064">
        <head>Up-to-date Fire Places.—J. S. GARLIND, 51 to 55, Bridge et., Warrington.—Advt. Funeral of the Late Sergeant-Major Nunnerley. . .</head>
        <p>Up-to-date Fire Places.—J. S. GARLIND, 51 to 55, Bridge et., Warrington.—Advt. Funeral of the Late Sergeant-Major Nunnerley. . .</p>
        <p>The funeral of the late Sergeant-Major Nunnerley took place, with military honours, at the Ormskirk Parish Church on Saturday, in the presence of a very large gathering. The procession was headed by a firing party of twelve from the Ormskirk detachment of the 3rd V. 8., under Colour-Sergeant Clayton, followed by the band of the detachment. Then came a gun carriage conveying the corpse, drawn by a full team of six horses and drivers from the Royal Garrison Artillery, Seaforth. The coffin was covered with the Union *Jack, on which were pinned the veteran's medals add resting on the top of the coffin were the helmet, sword and spurs which he wore in the famous charge at Balaclava. After the chit,' mourners I came the Volunteers, about seventy in number, in command of Major Bentham ; about sixty of the Lancashire Imperial Ytoinanry, about twenty of the Army Ordnance Stores (Burseough), and sixteen ; sergeants of the Royal Garrison Artillery (Seaforth). Besides the chief mourners there were present the lEarl of Latham, Mr. J. C. Wood, Mr. W. Sewell, ; Mossley-hill, Liverpool (13th Light Dragoons), and IMr. Ed. Hindley, Smithdown-road, Liverpool (13th* Light Dragoons), both of whom took part in the ; Balaclava charge. The funeral service was conducted by the Vicar (the Rev. J. E ;Woodrow). The coffin was borne to the grave on the shoulders of men of the 3rd V.B. Lancashire Hussars and Royal Garrison Artillery, Seaforth. After the service the firing party fired three volleys over the grave, followed by the rolling of the drums and a sounding of the " Last Post.' ASA for CUNNINGHAM'S HIG 11-CL ASS MINIM AL WATERS.—Advt. Humorous Competition. Messrs. Kilduff and Hardiman held their ninth annual humorous competition at the Parr Hall on Monday evening.. The programme differed from those submitted in past years and was more attractive by reason of the fact that greater variety was introduced. Despite the unfavourable weather a large audience assembled. There were ;24 entries, but only 14 put in an appearence. The n Onalotttition waa a very keen one, and the judge(Mr. A. Grdunds)had great difficulty in making his awards. The f illowing were the prize winners : —l, Alf. B. Millard, Manchester. "At a music hall" ; 2, Revill Hall, Manchester, " The Hamilton Diorama " ; 3. Frank Laurie, Stockport, " Amateur Orchestra"; 4, Leo Groin°, Seaeombe, " C trtooas of celebrities " ; and 5, J. Harwood, Bolton. "I followed 'ern." Mr. Matt Kilduff contributed several humorous items, for which he was de&amp;trvedly applauded,and Mr. William Kilduff was responsible for an excellent recitation. The duties of docompanist were carried out in a praiseworthy manlier by Mr. Albert Holt, and the stage manager was Mr. William Kilduff. Potroleum Oil in barrels and drums delivered free.—J. S. GARLAND, 51 to 53, bridge St., Warring- ton.—Advt.</p>
        <p>Distribution of Bread at Warrington. An experiment in which Warrington unemployed I participated was made at Warrington on Tuesday. ,• Having taken over the Wilderspool steam bakery Mr. I James Horton tested the capacity of his new ovens I early in the morning, produemg some 1,500 loaves. These he later in the day distributed amongst the , unemployed in the t ova at a gathering which was I held in the Parr Hall. Long before the advertised I time for the cotnmencernent of the meeting a large , crowd assembled in the building---a motley pile-faced crowd whose cauntenances were brightened by the anticipation of what lay in store. Cheers greeted Mr. • Horton as he rose to address the expectant unemployed and the fair sprinkling of,,the general public who were accomiLotlated in the gallery. Mr. Horton's remarks were brief. He touched upon the unemployed question and advocated as a solution the establish- ment of an institution for the provision of cheap meals —an institution where a breakfast of porridge,a cup of coffee and Ilb. of bread for lid. and a dinner of soup and bread for 21d. could be ob- tained. This he said could be done for :tso,and more- over, be added, the enterprise could be run at a profit. He also spoke upon the fiscal question, as he said from a non-poiltical standpoint, advocating a continu- ance of Free Trade on the ground that any departure from that policy on the lines at present suggested, would result in an increase in the cost of living. His speech concluded, ' the distribution of loaves com- menced, the number being gradually diminished until none were left, and 1,500 hungry people had been provided with the means of making a hearty meal. A number of excellent humorous selections were given by Mr. Gifford, Manchester. LADIES' BEST GLACNI BOOTS AND SHOES, UNDER VIE CLOCK, BRIDGE-STR.EET.—Advt. P.S.A. Meetings.</p>
        <p>An open meeting of the Buckley-street P.S.A. Society was held on Sunday at which the Legh-street Primitive Methodist choir sang the sacred cantata " Esther, the beautiful queen," the choruses and solos being rendered in an excellent manner. Mr. F. -Podmore accompanied. Mr. H. H. Taylor occupied the chair in the absence of his father, Mr. J. Taylor, and ; gave an encouraging address. Mrs. HaMpson supplied ; the connective teadings.-11r.H. Stone presided at the Warrington P.S.A. meeting, when there was an excellent attendance: The President (Mr. E. W. ; Jones) gave the address, and reminded his hearers ; that that was Temperance Sunday. In thousands ; of churches. and P.S.A. Secielies he said attention would that day be drawn to tho great need there was for some measure of , reform. Amongst all the grave questions pressing for attention in State and Church there, was not one for ; which a plea of ,urgency could be advanced with greater force than for this question of the drink traffic. 'rho tendency now amongst temperance ' workers was to fall into line, and accept any and every instalment of reform, and to help forward every effort that was made to cripple the power of the drink interests and to ensurea more thorough administration of the licensing laws ; so there was now a good chance of something being done. They must throw all their strength and energy into every forward movement,and take advantage of every practical attempt to deal with the drink evil. They inuat proceed step by step,doing what they could to educate public opinion on this question, and when the moral sense of the people had been awakened they would expel the evil from their midst. Miss L. Hewitt rendered two solos " Come unto Me ye weary " and The beautiful prayer." Mr. B. Wadsworth was the accompanist. Councillor IV. Clegg read the Scripture lesson and Mr. 11. Nelson also took part in the meeting.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-12-02_WidnesExaminer_art0065" n="art0065">
        <head>LOCAL NEWS.</head>
        <p>LOCAL NEWS.</p>
        <p>Notice. The Warrington " Examiner " is now published on Friday afternoon, price one penny, and is on sale at the various newsagents at about 4 o'clock. Should any difficulty be experienced in getting the paper the proprietors will undertake to deliver a copy at any address in Warrington by 5 o'clock on Friday after- noon without extra charge. Address all communica, tions to" Examiner Office," Bewsey-street Warrington H. M. ASIITON'S LIFE TONIC takes away that tired feeling.—(Advt.) Index to Inside Pages.</p>
        <p>PAGE: 9. Women's Chat and Fashions. Guardians and the cost of Vaccination. Warrington Distress Committee. , Billiard Matches. 3. Industial Betterment at Messrs. Crosficlds. Works. " The White Rose." 6. " The Interest of Tears." Short story. Christmas Magazines. Major Seely at Widnes. The Warrington Sensation. 7. Liberal Meeting at Frodsham. Children's Corner. Runcorn Rural oouncil. , UNDER THE CLOCK for Good BOOTS.—Advt. Local Will. The will has been proved of the late Major John Cavendish Orred (61), of Traninere, Runcorn, and : Weston Cheshire, at £276,307. H. M. ASHTON'S LIFE TONIC takes away that tired feeling.—(Advt.) Concert at Ebenezer Church: There was a large attendance at Ebenezer Church, on Monday night an the occasion of a very successful concert which was given by Miss Thornton's orchestra. The programme consisted of selections by the orchestra violin sole, " Tremola," Miss Gracie Hudson ; songs, Miss M, Gandy, " Three beggars," Mr. A. Griffiths ; quartet, minuet, and trio," Miss E, Jones, Miss A. Thornton, Miss U. Thornton, and Miss M. Bouchier ; violin obligate; Miss M, Thornton ;. violin duet, Gondalera," Miss Ada Middleton and Miss Edith !Chadwick ; selection, " Reminiscences of England." F. Gedfry orchestra ; violin sob, " La Danza, ' Miss I Ada Thornton; quintett, "Scherzo," Miss A. Thornton Miss E. Hodgkinson, Miss M. Thornton and Miss M. Bouchier. The progranune was well sustainedthrougli- out the various items being heartily applauded. The proceeds were in aid of church work.</p>
      </div>
