        <p>ST. ANDREW'S DAY CELEBRATIONS. WOMEN'S LIBERAL ASSOCIATION. Annual Dinner of the Warrington Sale of Work and Concerts. Caledonian Association. ADDRESS BY MR. A. H. CROSFIELD. THE MAYOR ON MUNICIPAL MATTERS. ---- i The Women's Liberal Asseciation is one of the most useful and active of the party organizations in Warring- ton and the great progress it has made during the last twelve months has been a source of considerable gratification. It will he remembered that in October of last year the Association played a prominent part in the promotion of the successful bazaar which was held in the Parr Hall. The project WAS entered into with enthusiasm and was the Mills of 'seising much-needed funds for party purposes. All the goods displayed, however, were not sold and with the object of disposing of the remnants a sale of work was arranged by the Association and held at the Liberal ; Club on Monday afternoon and evening, Opportunity was also taken to hold the annual tfa meeting and concert, and the functions proved most enjoyable. The ; sale was opened by Mrs. Armitage, president of the Association, in the presence of a large gathering. Mr. A. Ff. Crosfield had been announced to preside, but was unable to he present until the evening gathering. The chair Was taken by Miss thI.aSFLELD, Who Said that sales of work and bazaars were now one of thechief methods employed by both the Liberal and Conservative part is to forward their causes. She supposed the reason was really that at such functions the workers were all brought together, and enthusiasm was kept alive. Just now, of all times in the history of the country, they oegit to keep their enthusisam as bright as possible. When they came to think of what work stood before the new Government, it Was most important that they- should be keen on the subject. She did not pretend to sketch out a programme for the Government to carry out when it came into office, but she would mention few of the chief points. The housing of the working classes was one of the most vital questions for England, At present, as one of our politicians slimmed it up "the rich man lived on cheap ground out in the country, while the poor man often had to lice on dear ground in the town." That was a manifest injus- tice and legislation should he brought to bear upon it Then, if we got a graduated income tav, it would make a vast difference in the conditions of life of a great many people. There was also the temperance agitation, and everyone who loved England at all must feel that that would be one of the greatest factors for good in the future if it were run on proper lines. She would also like to put in wertran's snffrege—(hear, bear) —because,whea they came to think,it was only common- sense and justice that those who paid rates and taxes should be allowed tn have a vote. If she had anything to do with the drawing up of the programme she would also go in for the abatement of the smoke nuisance as the advantage of such a msesure, especially throughout Lancashire, could not be told. It was, said Miss Gros- field in cencinsion, a very e.loi thing that the members of the Association could be brought together at this juneture when they had an election looming before them. (Applause.) Mrs. ARNI A.Gr., in ft brief speech. declared the sale open end wished it every success. alr.C. E. PIRKER moved a vote of thanim to Mrs. Armitage and Miss Crosfield for their services. flo said that Miss Crosaeld had been bold enough to map out a Liberal programme—a thing that most of the leaders of the Liberal party seemed somewhat frightened of doing. The subjects she had mentioned would certainly give a text for a lengthy address. They were all, of coarse, very much interested in the question of Free Trada and Protection hut he ventured to think that me subject, to which Miss Crosaeld had called attention was even more important. When they considered that, taking the whole of the working claseas and striking an average, the working classes spent one-fifth of all they received in intoxicating drink, they would see that that was a vital question affecting the morel welfare and the economic prosperity of the country. Although he was very strongly attached to Free Trade and thorourzhly believed in it, if` be had to choose between a Protectionist and a sober nation and a Free Trade and a drimken n Ition, he would " b ink " the former. He would not at that gathering enter into the question of land values, but all the points raised by Mbar Crosfield were very important find vital ones. The obligation that the 'Liberal party in Warrington owed to the members of the Crosfield family was very great. They were elweys in the right place and always willine to do whet they could for Liberalism Wha he had said about the name of Crosfield be could (+Tully say about the risme of Armitage. (Applause.) They all respected and how-lured Mrs. Armitage and they were pleased she haul kindly oeened the ails. (Applause). Mr. C. JacesoN FrOGNIES wonder' the vote of thanks, which WM heartily ace:lorded, and the business Of the sale commence-I. NThere was a good display of useful and fancy articles,. the following ladies presidine at the stalls :—Mrs. -Arrnitege, Mrs. Osborne, Mrs. Holmes, Mrs. Plinston, Mrs. Dutton, Miss Short and Miss Howard. There was a lame gathering at the toe meeting in the evening. The following members of the committee assisted at the tables :—Mesdames Clay. Wyllie, Osborne, Clove Dunn. O'Brien, Mounfield, Ewart and Simosoa, and Miss Crosfield. Afterwards three highly enjoyable concerts were held in the reading room, awl for each the room was crowded. The artistes who contributee to the Pro- eramuies were Miss Marie Houghton (soprano), Min Fairhuat (contralto), Mrs. IL 'Lavine,- (solo violin) Mr. P. J. George (baritone), the Lyric taeartette Party tirti Mr. E. A. Senders (humorist). Mrs. C. 'leeks= Holmes end Mr. A. Trow acted as accompanists. SPEECH BY MR. A. H. CROSFIELD. Mr. A. H. Caosareme presided at the first concert and delivered a short, address. He said it had been rather a hippy custom at that club. us he had remarked on a previous occasion, to combine pleasant musical entertainments with a little political disquisition, just as the ancient Greeks used to consider music and athletics were the most important part, of education. He would not venture for that reason' to detain them for more than a few minutes and enether reason Was that ho found he had been forestalled Already, mach te his surprise, by his sister. (Laughter.) He was quite sure he had dealt much more adequately than he could with the important causes that they were there to defend and advance. The great issue which they put in the fore- front of their political platform and which was the chief object of their endeavoure was the great and all- embracing issue of Free Trade. They would know that it had been said many a time, not as a mere platform plati- tude hut as an honest truth, that it was a cause which concerned the women of this country even more than it concerned the men. (Hear, hear.) If that were true of England es a whole, it was true of Lancashire more than any other county from John-0- Groat's to Land's End, because they knew how many millions of peep) were depeadent for their daily bread and for the standard of their living upon our great cotton industry, the products of which found their ways to distant. parts of the world. It would be an irreparable injury if we were to introduce the mischievous and mistaken policy of Protection. ' They might say that it WAS a dry subject to deal with, but it was an issue which had a bearing on every humble household in the county. He dare say it was partly becanse of that and partly because the Liberal women of Warrington recognized how the subject of providing sound and adequate education for the chil- dren came home to them, that there had been such a splendid increase in the membership of the Warrington Wornen's Liberal Association and that. they saw a greater gathering there that night than they had ever had before. (Applause.) They had broken the record—(applanse)—and he hoped they would break it again next time they had a meeting there. (Applause.) He Was considered to be a very indifferent hand at canvassing. He did not think it was his .fortr and he had not felt it was his place so far, because as they knew heewas not the candidate but only the prospective candidate. (A voice : " But we hope. you will be "and "We will do our best.") It was very kind of them to say so, and it would be pure affection. on his part to ignore the fact that he might have that greet responsibility placed on his shoulders in a very few weeks' time. If that be so,he thanked his lady friend who said she was going to do her best for him. (Applause). He sincerely hoped that everyone else would do the anion, because he was quite certain that if he had the good favour and influence of the ladies it. would count for a great deal more than the influence of the men. (Laughter and applause.) He had spoken of the two great questions which they had to put in the forefront of their programme, and to whieh they had in the first instance to devote their attention—Free Trade and education. If he might make a suggestion for their husband's and their men- friends, it was that they should he careful not to let any red herring trail drawn across the scent distract them and divide their attention. Men might be caught by chaff of that kind— they might be deceived—but he was sure the ladies present were far too clever to let anyone divide their attention and diminish their energies by any little plan of that kind (Hear, hear and 'applause). They had got this great work to do. Ti would not cure every- thing for them—they had no short cut, to Utopia to offer end no false promises to make. But they had a , very fine and noble cause to support and it was a cause which all women could work for as heartily and sincere- -ly as the men. By doing so, they would be helping to some extent the cause of human progress which was a matter he felt very deeply. Afterwards they and friends of their's might differ on otherlquestions that arose—awl there were many other questions they had to deal with— hut they were all united on One great issue. They would face other questions in their turn, but theylwere not going to be baulked of their purpose or to he misled or deluded by any astute electioneerer, however clever he might be sad however often the public had been deceived before. Now they were dealing with a cause that I touched every household in the country, with questions that went home to the people and upon which all English people could judge soberly and dispassionately. Be for one had faith in the good senseand the common- sense of his fellow-countrymen and he believed they would come to a wise conclusion on the whole matter. He only hoped that in the great victory that was going to be won throughout England, Warrington would take an honourable part. (Applause.) He would most sincerely an I gratefully thank every lady in that room for any help she felt inclined to give him on that occasion. (Applause.)</p>
        <p>Loyal Scots in Warrington, in common with -their kinsmen throughout the world, joined on Thursday in celebrating the feast of St. Andrew, their patron saint. The seveuteeuth annual dinner of the Warrington Caledonian Association was held at the Lion Hotel, and proved one Of the most successful and enjoyable funotiOns in the history of the Association. It was carried out. with typical Soottish cerem any, the guests assembling to the strains of the bagpipes, indulging in favourite Scottish dishes, and honour- ing toasts in the accepted Highland ,fashion. The room was very tastefully decorated, the flag of St. Andrew being conspicuous. The gathering was presided over by Mr. J. Mmro (president of the Association) an enthusiastic Scot to whose worthy efforts the success of the organization is largely due. The chairman had on his right the Mayor (Alder- man Bolton) and on his left Colonel W. Hall Walker, a life • member of the Association. The vice- chairs ware Occupied by Mr.W: Skinner (vice-president); and Messrs. J. A. McGregor, ana B. Shearer ex-presi- dents. There were also present Mr. Reginald Mortimer, (London), Captain Appleton, Councillors Efulse,Fulton- Smith, and Dr. Joseph and local Scots and their friends to the number of between seveoty and eighty. An excellent dinner was servel by Mr. L 33 and included such dishes as haggis, " cockie-leekie," haunch o'red deer, and St. Andrew's. padding. As usual the haggis was " playel in by a stalwart Highland piper in full national costum 3, Sergeant Donald Murray, of the Liverpool City Police. The president announced the receipt of apologies for non-attendance from Sir Gilbert Greenall, Bart., Mr. R. Pierpoint, M.P. Mr. J. Lyon Whittle. and Mr. Linke Talbot. During the evening the following telegram was despatched to the Liverpool St. Andrew's Society: "Thine be ilk joy and treasure, peace, enjoyment, life and pleasure. Here's ta ye a' from Grittier Scots, Warrington." The reply received was: "Heartiest greetings from Liverpool Scots." The toast of "The King" was proposed by the presi- dent who said that during • the year his Nlajesty had intensified his interest in the welfare of his subjects ! while he had most succesfullv endeavoured to promote the peace of the world. Mr. Monro expressed the gratification of Scotsmen at the recent visit of the King to Scotland when there wera striking demonstrations of loyalty., (Applause.)</p>
        <p>The toast 'MILS drunk with Highland honours. The toasts of "The Q teen ' and " Toe Duke and Duchess of Rothesay and members at the Royal family" were also honoured on the proposition of the President. Mr. W: SKIVCIEIt proposed The Navy, Array and reserve forces," and Captain Appleton responded. Mr. REGINALD hiCtIttENIER elocrieritly proposed the toast of the evening—" The land 0' Cakes.' Alluding to ancient Scottish history Mr. Mortimer trae,ed the history of the famous Coronation stone of Scone—one of the most treasured links with the past. Continuing he said his historian told him that the true Scoteh °ha rooter was Trioolied upon the lines of true religion—that Christianity was the prime factor in the development of the race. It was interwoven with their Government, their &amp;initiation and their people, and its influence operated from the cradle to the grave. The speaker then reminded his hearers of the days of valour—of Wilkes, Bruce and other Scotchmen. So far as England was concerned the one great blot on the pages of her history was the cruel persecution and death of Mary Queen of Scots. flaying referred to the Union of Englanu and Scotland land to the persecutions of that time, Mr. Mortimer said that now instead of two kingdoms ever at variance with one anoter and the prey of every foe, we had a great and mighty nation which by its own efforts had forced itself into the forefront of the nations of the world. (Applause). Scotland had been the means of giving to England and the world great statesmen, artists, poets, orators,soldiers.sailors and men,and his only hope was that the great traditions which those in the past held would be handed down from generation to generation(Apn!ause) The toast was heartily honoured in Highland fashion. I Colonel W. HILL Wa.Licruti M.P., proposed the toast. of " The town and trade of Warrington. He said that thanks to the cosmopolitan nature of IVarrington's industries he did not think they could very well be dis- appointed with the present state of trade in toe town in comparison with other parts of England. They had good reason to hope that the prosper!ty -of the town , would continue, as, for one thing, it was exceptionally Well situated for commercial purposes. It might become , a very serious matter in the future as to the growth of the rates in towns which were devoted to commerce. Although they were all anxious to see the town unproved and the latest luxuries introduced for the people, he hoped tha Corporation would think seriously first of all of the industries upon which the town was dependent awl which might be hampered in competition by the burden of rates. Colonel Flail Walker proceeded to refer to varioaks phases of the history of Lancashire—to periods of depression and of prosoerity—and to the part which IVarrington played in them. At thepresent timo they heard a great deal ahont the unemployed. but he had made iraqujries itt this district and heard on all sides that trade was prosper- ing fairly well. It scpinert uinaccuont&amp;sls that so many should ha unemployed and he sometimes thought the , increase among female labour might be one cause, but ' he hoped not.</p>
        <p>In respon ling the Mayor said that every man who was seat as a representative on the Town Council ought to go there with the determination whether he was sent on particular lines, whather he came to serve his own purpose or someone else's, to look at the matter collec- tively—in the sense that the Corporation ought to work in the interests of the wholecomumnity. He was told to be merciful to the Town Councillors—(laughter)—why, he did wit know. Lie, however thought the ratentyers ought to know bow the increases of rates affected them. Of course, if they wanted to do things on the " agitation business " then let them agitate, but they must understand thatan agitator never used his own money en] never spent his own money. (Laughter.) He used his own brains for the benefit of his own pocket, and that was his living. (Laughter.) The biggest share of the agitators who came into the town came for a ptrticular purpose. They clue to serve their own interests, an I those of the section who sent them. He was trying to bs merciful. (Laughter.) When a man went into the Council who knew i more than anyone else, and thought he was too clever to learn, it v-as a dangerous thing to try amide anything with him. (Laughter.) The best thing was to let him talk and "carry on" and simply ignore the ideas he was trying to bring through. Money was frittered away sometimes besanse they sent in a business man who gave time and abilities which in a firm would probably earn him £2OO or £3ooal year,—and did so for the general good of the conamunity,—and he was so annoyed and disgusted by certain members of a committee that he retired from the Council and left them with the tail end of the committee to work out the concern. He (the Mayor) was getting quite disgusted with the way things were going on as far as the Corporation %US concerned. The Distress Committee, he continued, would do wonders. They had id. in the £ to play on. (Laughter.) The Education Committee began on much the s ame lines—they were going to do wonders. They pnt a shilling on the rates and we had got nothing for it but an e licient staff and the teachers' salaries doubled. (Laughter.) In conclusion the AI tyor stid they wortld find it was not as much the waste of the Corporation as the waste of Acts of Parliament and the manner in which they were carried out that helped to drain the ratepayers' pockets. Mr. P. MONKS proposed the toast of" The Warring- , ton Caledonian Association." He commenced by corn- cleating npan what he termed the sensible speech of the Mayor with reference to the government of the town. On looking through the figures they found that the large firms in the town oontributed consider- ably more than one third of the rates of the 1 town, but yet they had not a single vote. They were represented in the Council not by trained business capacity but by men a.ho, as the Mayor had slid, week, think they were doing well if they were getting 303. a week. It only required a Scotch. man to use his brains aid to reflect to see whether he had the right to expect good government and the economical management of affairs from such a repre- sentation. Mr. Monks proceeded to refer to the obligation which Scotland owed to John Knos as the founder of the educational system which placed the universities within the reach of even the poor people. Ho conch-tie Iby pointing out that Scotchruen had invaded England to an extent that was never dreamed of. and to them we owed a great deal of the sucoess of our banking system. (Applause.) Mr. B. Slum= responded to the' toast, refer- ring to the advantages which membership of the Association offered, and urging local Ssotchraen to avail thetwielves of them. He thought the membership of the Association, which now stood at 9 honorary members, 23 life members, and 4V ordinary members, oueht to he considerably increased. (Applause). The toast of the health of" Our Patron " (Sir Peter Carlaw Walker, BIM) wts honoured on the proposition of Mr. ARVIUR BROWNE. The toast of "Our Guests" was proposed by Councillor FULTON SMITE!, who amusingly referred to the wrong impressions entertained of otohmen and their characteristics. Councillor Dr. JOSEPH responded.</p>
        <p>Air. J. L. DAVE 71'ORT proposed the health of" Onr President " and spoke in highly eulogistic terms of Mr, Monro's services to the Association. He also congratu- lated him upon his recovery from his recent illness. (Applause.) /kin Mono briefly responded. Daring the evening songs Were rendered by Mr. J. L, Davenport, Mr. McClure, and Mr. Wescott, and Mr. Williams, of ‘Volverhamnton gavea violin solo in highly creditable style. Mr. W. H. Payto Mits. Bac. accom- panied.</p>
      </div>
