      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-12-02_WidnesExaminer_art0072" n="art0072">
        <head>CRIPPLED BY RHEUMATISM!</head>
        <p>CRIPPLED BY RHEUMATISM!</p>
        <p>Astounding Cure by Bile Beans.</p>
        <p>Rheumatism is one of winter's most painful It cripples thousands, makes life a torture, and opens the gates of death. Bile Beans, by supplying the body with new rich blood and expelling the poisonous acid which is the caue of rheumatism, attack this dread disease in a natural way. Proof of the value of Bile Beans is given in the story of the cure of Mr. Samuel Milne, of ISO, Coa!shwa Green Road, Hollinwood, Oldham. He says : —" Twelve years ago I was in the infirmary suffering from lead poisoning. Then I began to suffer from muscular rheumatism, eausca' by indiges- tion and liver disorder. So bad had I become that I used to fall off my bed in fits. I kept fighting with my illness, having a wife and family to support, hut at the finish it got me down. My left a-m became practically useless. I was treated by more than one local doctor, and managed to get to my work at intervals, hobbling atoni on hro sticks. I could scarcely get home at night. Two doctors told me that unless I got out of the country I should be a con- firmed cripple in a year or so. "I tried medicine after medicine but got no reltef. I had got so low-spirited that didn't really care whether or not I was ever cured. I bad on spell of ten months in bed, and anotker of twelve months. One day friend persuaded me to try Bile Berms. When I had finished the first box I felt a little better, and by the time I had taken a short course I was able to put my sticks aside, and soon after that I one day walked seven miles. My friends were astonished, and conld scarcely believe I was the same person. My digestive troubles and liver disorder were also cursd, and hit by bit the rheumatism vemed to be ariren from my bodk. Bile Beans have now cured me entirely. I am now back at work, and my employer says he never saw such an alteration in anybody as Bile Beans have made in me." Bile Beans are an excellent specific against rhen- 1 rastism. lumbago. neuralgia. influenza, colds and chills, and are invaluable for biliousness, indigestion, consti- pation, piles, anmmia. female ailments. weakness, dizzi- ness, imomnia, and liver tronbles. Of all chemists, or Post free from the Bile Bean M fg. Co.. Red Cross-street. London, E.C., for is. 11:1. or 2s. Dd. (three times the quantity). Beware of substitutes and imitations. -</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-12-02_WidnesExaminer_art0073" n="art0073">
        <head>LIBERAL MASS MEETING AT WIDNES. Major Seely on Free Trade.</head>
        <p>LIBERAL MASS MEETING AT WIDNES. Major Seely on Free Trade.</p>
        <p>A large demonstration of Widnes Liberals was held in the Drill Hall, Widnes. on Tuesday evening, presided over by Mr. H. Wade Deacon, J.P., C.A. The interest evinced in the meeting again tended to show the strong revival in the cause of Liberalism in the town. The hall was crowded. The Liberal leader (Mr. Deacon) was snpported on the platform by the princip•! speaker (Major Seely, M.P.), Sir John Swinburn (the local Liberal candidate). Mr. Max Mospratt, I\lr. J. B. Aitken Mr. Enstace Carey, Mr. F. J. Pools, Mr. A. E. Jacobs and others.- _ _</p>
        <p>Letters of apology were read from Mr. T. Sutton Timmis, Mr. Stuart Dancon, and Mr. H. Brunner. Major Seely, who had an enthusiastic reception, said that the general election would not be long deleyed. The greatest issue before them was Free Trade. There were few men in Widnes who could gain by ?retention; but he would not argue the subject from that point of view. He would rather Ion!: at it from the point of view of the whole United Kingdom. From the first he had opposed Mr. Chamberlain's proposals root and branch, and he would oppose them to the end (applause). The first effect of resteicting external trade would be to damage the persons concerned with carrying thins as opposed to persons engaged in making things. It was a most ridiculous fallacy of Mr. Chamberlain and his followers that foreign exchange between nations meant national loss. If there was one scintilla of truth in the theory that checking imports would increase the wealth of the nation, which would be the wealthiest nation of the world? Thihet, that had no imports at all (laughter and applause). The more they examined the Free Tradn cause, not in the ordinary clap-trap of both sides, but in the pure light of reason, the more they would see that to check imports meant diminished weahh. Encourage imports. enconrge exchange, and fie', would increase the wealth not only of the nation, but of the whole , world (applause). Fr fifty years foreign Eur-pean nations had been endeavouring to tax England. If there was onepaeticle of truth in the theory that they could tax the foreigner what would have been the state of England after fifty years of trade, amounting on the average to hundreds of millions of pnnnds ? We should have bean sunk into the most abject poverty. and foreign c,eintries that imposed the highest tariffs should be full of unbounded wealth ; whereas the fact was that in these countries there was more poverty and more enemploy- went than in England. Hyatt means let the noemployed seek for a remedy, but do not let them trtk for this clap-trap fraud Protection of making the country rich by taxes, which at the end of 6,000 Team of trial had ended in nothing hnt misery for those who lied attempted it. (Applause.) England stood, as MP. Balfour had said, in a position superior to that of all other European countries not only in material wealth, but the diffused well-being of our population was greater than it had ever been. (Applause.) As a progressive politician there -vere many things he wished to see done. He wished to sce the land dealt with (applause'; but let the people aliandon the idea that by " taxing the foreigner " they would cure their woes. It had never been done, and never would he done. Experience had proved it, logic had proved it, and he stood there to denounce it as a sham (applause). If we ha Protection we must have a tax upon food. We needed a change in the direction of taxation, but not in the direction of putting more into the-pockets of the landlords, not in the direction of making great trusts, but in the more equitable distribu- tion 'or taxation, which should fall more equitably than it did at present upon those who held the urban land of this country. (Applause). After dealing with the snb- r7ect of Chinese labour, Major Seely touched upon the Irish qnestion and said tho whole matter rested upon the cutestion—" Will you or will yon not trust the poople " (applause). It had hithe to been believed that they could not trust the Irish p,ople. He did not believe it. The first step towards trust was taken when the Tory Government gave self-government to local assemblies in Ireland, and he believed, when they trusted the people of Ireland. all ideas of separation between England and Ireland would cease. (Applause). Sir John Swinfatrne, who was also received with pro- longed cheers, said the large company present augured well for the future of lEiberaliam. Dealing with the land question, the speaker contended that the system for the transfer ofdand required to be much more simple and much less expensive. The land lay at the root of ull our national prosperity, and the more free it was from the shackles that now enthralled it the greater would be the prosperity of the country (loud cheers) Mr. Max Mnspratt also spoke at some length, as well as Eeveral others.</p>
        <p>The use of Mustard at</p>
        <p>the table sharpens the appetite, makes the food more palatable, and aids digestion.</p>
        <p>Pro'ably nine persons out of ten who ta.e Mustard regularly do so because they like it. They are " building better than they know." 'The " Lancet " says that "moderate indulgence promotes digestion," for it 'improves the flavour," and "flavour is undcubtedly a great promoter of dizesuon success."</p>
