      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-12-02_WidnesExaminer_art0076" n="art0076">
        <head>INTEREST OF TEARS.</head>
        <p>THE INTEREST OF TEARS.</p>
        <p>But who shall so forecast the years And find in loss a gain to match? Or reach a hand through time to catch</p>
        <p>The far off *interest oiteara ?</p>
        <p>I How a high 'spirited girl like Evie Wright could ever come to let herself be engaged to a dissipated I little beast like Yorke neither I nor anybody else I could understand You had only to see the two of , I them together to recognise the absurdity of the whole I thing. She was tall and stately whii pride and breed- ing written all ovet her face, and he t mean shifty little wretch that could not lo k a dog in the face. It was easy to be seen that they were about as unsuited to each other as darkness to light. Of course I know that I am perhaps not regarded as an impartial critic, it being a matter of wonder among my friends that j all- I seemed to care about was my profession and 'j making money. How should they know? How i should anybody know except myself —and a photo- ' , graph that smiled down on me from the wall of my sitting-room every evening as I smoked ray final pipe. There was nothing especially attractive about the picture to a stranger. It was merely that of a young ; girl with large wistful eyes, and bright sunny hair 1 which hung about her neck and shoulders, yet it knew, it could have told enquiring friends why it was that I was toiling and struggling to make a name, but it ; kept its secret. It is ever the way of the world,—one man shall sow, but another shall do the reaping. So those of us who ; have begun our toil with some definite end in view, who little by little and after much struggling and many days of waiting begin to see our reward in sight Perhaps we are just about to stretch out our hands j and grasp the long coveted much sought after prize ' I when lo ! suddenly up comes soind hop-of-my-thumb I dancing along and snatches it away from our very ' eyes. Then the darkness comes upon us, and the iron , enters our soul and we remember how in our early . school days we used to copy out in a large smug hand that virtue is its own reward ;" and it comforts us vastly. There was a party of us all spending our holidays in the Kyles of Bute, and I first saw that there was trouble brewing when that drunken little brute Yorke j would persist in trying to say Auchenlochan. Now Anchenlochan is no word for an Englishman to pro- nounce, even when sober,and Yorke was far from that. He and some companions had been spending the after- noon at Rothe,,ay neither wisely nor well, and when he landed at the pier, he should if he had possessed a grain of sense gone straight up to the hotel. Instead !of that the young ass must needs go along to where ; the girls were sitting, and start telling them about ! what he had been doiag at Rothesay. They well knew his weakness and so far from being shocked at it encouraged him in his capers, and it was just in the middle of a fierce argument as to the pronunciation of I the place I have mentioned that Miss Wright walked ; up. She gave one glance at her wretched fiancee and another at his audience then turned to where I was j standing. " Mr. Platt I should so much like to have a pull across to the Bute shore before dinner. Have we time , do yon think ?"</p>
        <p>‘ioi,I.;;;;)' I answered delightedly.' Surely I must be dreaming, or my ears had deceived me. It could not be true, but there she was waiting f ir me to get the boat ready with a proud hart look on her face. If I had not been so carried away by the idea of having her in my company, p.orhaps I might have paid more attention to the expression of those beautiful eyes of hers and been warned ; but I noticed nothing and accordingly we set out. I thanking Heaven for the little hit Of leaven that had been vouchsafed to me and little dreaming what the trip would bring forth. Every dog has its day, or a portion of a day any- way, and sitting in the stern of the boat watching my . lovely companion as she sculled us across was my particular period. I was enjoying it to the full and giving grateful thanks accordingly, when Evie stopped ; rowing for a moment, and leaning forward on her oars : and eyeing me the while very steadily said " Mr. Platt I suppose you are wondering why I asked you ; to come out with me "?.</p>
        <p>The; -;a's.S.O.nie-iiing-tibout the tone of her voice - and the look on her face as she asked the question that made me wonder if our little trip was going to pan out quite as I expectA it. However I made answer boldly " Not at all Miss Wright, it was merely t your goodness of heart, which prompted you to take I pity on my loneliness." " M 7. Platt "said she with her eyes on my face "you are an honest man I think." From her tone she evidently expected &amp;in answer "Oh ! more or less " I answered with a flippancy I was far I from feeling " that is for a mere man you know." She I shot a glance at me from under her heavily frineed I eye lashes. "Mr. Platt "she went on softly "How !long have I known you." " Ever since I hashed up Ithe butcher's boy for lauehing at you when you tumbled off the slide." "Oh yes I remember, and how mother laughed when I went home and told her that a fat lad with red cheeks and horrid red hands ! had knocked down our butcher's sou Dick, just because he was watching us girls slidinu." " Oh ! alright," I answered severely,"if that was all he was doing, I am very sorry that I interfered, tut I I distinctly saw when you fell how"— -</p>
        <p>" And during all that time you have told me the truth, have y,u not, Chris"? "Always" I answered promptly. Dear goodness the way I walked into the trap like a lamb to the slaughter was ridiculous. The glamour of her eyes and the fact that she had used my chri.slian name had got into my bead. However it was too late. The word was scarcely out c my mouth before she asked. "Was Gi!bert, M. Y, rke I, mean, very intoxicated this afternoon " ? _ _ _ .</p>
        <p>I felt myself growing hot. I saw her eyes searching my Imo. I turned my head uneaAily to the right and gazed down the Kyles towards the small green island of Inchmarnock and beyond to the grey mist Clad peaks of Arran. I looked up the bay towards Tignabruaich and the " narrows " to Where the great heathery hills tome down to the waters edge. Again my eyes sought her face; her glance held mine, "Mr. Matt I am waiting." • "Yes Miss Wiight he was, I am afraid." "Was he —I mean has he often been like —like what he was this afternoon "?</p>
        <p>Oh ! %thy did not the " Columba " or the " Lord of the Isles " come along and swamp us in their backwash. Death would bate been sweet at the moment. "I don't think it is quite fair to ask me these questions Irsa Wright ; you don't expect me to answer them surely, besides you ought to know more about him than any of us." . _</p>
        <p>" Perhaps I ought, at any rate I no doubt will before I have finished " she retorted bitterly, " hat I have not brought you out to ask questions. Do you know, Christopher, of all the people I know, and of all my friends there is but one I can trust," hero she flashed a look at me "Because of that, and because I want your help, and for another reason which—which perhaps does not matter I am going to tell you my story. Nobody knows it, nobody must ever inow it.. Take the oars -Chris." I did as she bade me: Despite the knowledge that she could never he mine, there was a., strange elation at my heart. Something told me she shared my secret. Anyway she trusted me and had need of me. That was enough for the present. For a few moments I sculled slowly and in silence. Then my eomoanion commenced,speaking very slowly and very quietly—so quietly that at times I had to rest on my oars and catch what she was saying. " I sappose you in common' with everybody else wonder why I became engaged to a man like Gilbert Yorke." She paused." I don't love him ,and never shall " she went on proudly. A thrill went through me. I bad not been deceived: hopes which for months bad lain dormant rose up again with renewed force. The next second I felt the chill of ice cold water trickling down my back.—" But I am going to marry him. It is a queer story Chris, which shwa how with- out being consulted our destinies are arranged for us." We were close into the Bute shore by this time. Twilight was creeping on, but the air was calm and still, with hardly a rippl&amp; on the water. " Years, oh years and years ago, before mother married father she was engaged to a young curate in Cheshire, hut the night before they wete to have been married she jilted him, and for some unaccountable reason refused to have anything more to do with him. From what I have heard, he. must have ben very much in love with mother, and the shock turned his bead. He gave up the Church and fell into had ways, and disappeared from among his friends, who for a time beard nothing of hini. Eventually they found him, only to discover that he was driLking himself to death."</p>
        <p>, Iliad given up all pretence of rowing now, and the boat was drifting where it lid. The evening was closed on fast; but my eyes were fixed passionately on the white clad figure in the stern. "During his wanderings up and down lie had met and married an actress who treated him very badly. Just before lie died he sent for mother, and askeddier if she would take charge of his only child, a boy, as</p>
        <p>IHE CHILDREN'S BREAKFAST</p>
        <p>Cooked in</p>
        <p>there was nobody else who would look after it. Of course, poor mother promised she would, and ever since then that child has been brought up oby my grandparents." . "Ills name ?" I whispered. I "Gilbert Yorke, the man lam going to marry." I I took hold of the oars and commenced sculling . vicimisly. The cat was out at last, and this proud, high-spirited girl, who I loved with my whole soul, was sacrificing herself deliberately on the mistaken altar of duty. "You have no right---" I began hotly. "You have not heard all" she went on, "Perhaps you remember Chris, what a fancy mother always had for Gilbert, and how she tried to look after him. His wild weak ways grieved her sorely, and she saw with sorrow that he was going the way his father went before him—the blame for which she took all on her- self. They say Chris, that the evil that men do lives after them, but in mother's case it lived with her—all her life. She never got over it, and before she died she told me the whole pitiful story. Excepting your- . ' self, I am the only one who had any control or I rran influence over him, and he—he the pit RESULTS OF SATURDAY'S GAMES. iful coward, I I told mother that he would go the way his father had *Wi G. T. P. G. T. P. • b 4-6 26 Widnes -2 8 gone, if I did not promise to marry him." I *Runcorn 2-3 13 Morecambe . 0-1 3 I " Well " I asked sharply "what more"? , I *Oldham l-2 8 Wakefield T l-1 5 I "Shortly aftr that mother lay dying, and I *Leigh 3-4 18 Millom 2-2 10 I promised."" You promised"! i *Bradford -6 26 Batley O-0 0 I She put her band before her face and bowed her I Broughton R. 2-3 13 *Roll l-0 2 3—2 12 Warrington -1 3 ' bead, and whispered, "Yes, God help me, I promised." i *Swinton I *Normanton -0 0 Rochdale II O-0 0 We had landed and were walking up the shingly. . 0-1 3 *York '—o 2 I Pontefract ' beach'. The youn7 moon was rising gaud *Dewbury y behind us, I 0-1 3 Hull M. Rovers 0-1 3 i over the low lying'Bute hills. The lights of the hotel *Salfo