      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-12-02_WidnesExaminer_art0077" n="art0077">
        <head>IS YOUR SKIN " WELL-DRAINED ? "</head>
        <p>IS YOUR SKIN " WELL-DRAINED ? "</p>
        <p>Nature's Wonderful System—Care Necessary in the Winter.</p>
        <p>(Specially Contributed to the Examiner.)</p>
        <p>The health of a town depends very largely upon efficient drainage and ventilation. Modern systems of drainage in our up-to-date cities are wonderful enough, but what could be more wonderful than the intricate system of skin-drainage cat rierl out by means of the millions of pores in your skin ? There are thousands of pores in a square inch of skin surface, yet Nature has fitted each one with its own automatic trap-door at the top, its pipe or tube throegh the tissue, and its sac or bag for collecting the refuse, which is expelled throlgh the " trap doors " to the extent of two pounds per persol per day. If one " drain " gets choked—if a single pore becomes stopped up—you notice first a blackhowl and then a pimple- while if several fail to do their duty, more serious skin ' disease is sure to result. Other signs of badly-drained tissue are sallowness, blotches, excessive oilness or dry, , fleas, and irritation.' Skin diseases are more prevalent RP. winter because the pores lack ventilation, and are not so frequently cleansed by perspiration as they are in summer. People are confined more indoors, and even when one does get into the open air, the ventilation of the pores is obstructed by extra clothing. Little wonder that the delicate siructures get out of order, and little wonder, too, that the use of coarse ointments makes matters worse. What is really needed is the application of Zam-buk, a refined combination of vegetable juices which constitutes the natural treatment for skin disease. Most otdinary preparations contain rancid animal fats and mineral products an harmful nature. If the skin absorbed them they would do infinitely more hartu than good, and they accomplish nearly as much harm by staying outside and clogging up the pores. The first sign of skin disease should he met by the application of Zaro-buk, both because of its remarkable purity and acfnowledged efficacy. Its valuable ingredi- ents. and the manner of their combination, are the result of years of study ind expes imentation conducted on scientific lines. Zaru-buk contains no harmful sub- stances, but is a combination of rich vegetable saps and juices beautifully refined and concentrated. The use of this balm regulates perspiration, and ensures a healthy action of the pores under hygienic conditions. If skin-trouble has already developed, Zarn-Buk first soothes the pain, allays irritation, and reduces inflammation by killing off all harmful germs and nullifying the effects of dust end other poisonous matters which may have got into the affected part. The refined nature of the balm enables it to sink through the pores deep into the tissue, searching out .ind expelling the causes of the trouble in away no ordinary preparation can possibly do. Zain-buk's work is thorough and it. will overcome the most obstinate skin disease as Iwell as the simplest injury or disorder. Finally Z tm- Buk actually promotes the growth of new, healthy skin and tissue. and thus has earned its right to the title "the ideal Skin cure."</p>
        <p>.. . - No preparation is so pleasant to use, or so soothing and healing for chaps, chafings, cold-sorea, chilblains, and the c3mrnon winter disorders directly due to the cold weather, as 7,ani-huk. Not only every home, but every member of the family, should have a one-and- three-hnl f-peuny or two-and-nine Los for his or her own special use.</p>
        <p>Warrington Landlady Wounded. At the Liverpool Assizes on Wednesday, before Mr. Justice Ridley, Arthur Pratt (25), butcher, pleaded not guilty to having, at Warrington, on the 26th July, unlawfully wounded DorAhy Caroline 13Ieasdale, a widow. The charge was originally one of feloniously and maliciously wounding with intent, and to this the prisoner at first pleaded guilty, but afterwards, when the prosecution reduced the chruge to the Miner one of unlawfully wuunding, prisoner pleaded not guilty. Mr. Ford, who prosecuted, said the wounds were inflicted on the prosecutrix by pris tier with a razor, but the injuries were not serious. Prisoner was one of three male lodgets at the house of the prosecutrix. Mts. Bleasdale's story was that on the 26th July she was sitting in her perlour writing a letter, when the prisoner came behind her and cut her with an old razor on the face and neck. Counsel stated than the prisoner seemed to think ho had some cause for jealousy because of the.way Mrs. Bleasdale had acted towards another lodger. The Prosecntrix, a well- deserved, middle-aged woman, in her evidence denied that there was any truth in the prisoner's allegation that. she had misconducted herself. " You are a bad, wicked man," she said to prisoner; " I have been a mother to you and fed you when you were starving and this is my reward." Thelury found the piisoner guilty of unlawful wounding, and he was sentenced to six months' imprisomnent.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-12-02_WidnesExaminer_art0078" n="art0078">
        <head>CHEERFUL MILLIONAIRE.</head>
        <p>THE CHEERFUL MILLIONAIRE.</p>
        <p>"The discussion of an old-age pension is unnecessary, for it is too far to look ahead." said Mr. Carnegie to an interviewer on landing in New York. "The young man now who doesn't drink and who doesn't smoke will make his own pension. In spite of combinations of capital and commercial interests, I think the young man of to-day has as much chance to get ahead as in yearg gone by." •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-12-02_WidnesExaminer_art0079" n="art0079">
        <head>NORTHERN RUG3Y LEAGUII.</head>
        <p>NORTHERN RUG3Y LEAGUII.</p>
        <p>LANCASHIRE COMBINATION.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON " A" v. CHAD DE RTON.</p>
        <p>Played at Warrington on Saturd tv. Warrington opened well, and Foster almost forced his way over ' before the game was ten minntes old. Lloyd got cleverly away, and threw across to Kenyon, who fall a victim to superior numbers. The visitors claimed the advantage when premature work by Jordan was responsible for the home men being penalized, and the subsequent kick gave the Chadderton msn a footing inside Warrington quarters. Ashton upset a rather smart movement of the visiting front rank. Garsido got in, but the point was not allowed. Chadderton rallied slightly, and, taking the gams direct to the home quarters, were not ousted from their position until one of their half-backs cleverly drooped a goal, this being the only tangible record at half time. Turn- ing round, Warrington forced the pace cleverly, and after manccuvring at centre Lloyd got splendidly away, and a brilliant run Was followed by a timely pass to Knight, who got in at the corner. Toe scorer also added a goal. With the lead in their favour Warring- ton played confidently, and the visitors being penalized Knight added a goal. Poster and Stubbs front the respective teams were awn' marching orders, Lloyd again effected a smart run, which ended in Kenyon scoring a try. Result : Warrington, two tries (ten points) Chadderton, 0113 goal (two pJints).</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON JUNIOR LE IGUE. The results of S,rtnrd ry's matches were : Orford, 1 gonl, 2 tries, 8 points ; Woolston, nil. Orford Hornets, 2 tries, 6 points; Warrington Rovers, nil. Victoria Park, 5 goals; 11 tries, 43 points ; Warring- ton Rangers, nil. All matches for to-day Ire cancelled. For Agst.</p>
