      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-12-02_WidnesExaminer_art0083" n="art0083">
        <head>INDIGESTION CURED. A Walthamstow Woman's Recognition</head>
        <p>INDIGESTION CURED. A Walthamstow Woman's Recognition</p>
        <p>of Benefit Derived from Mother Seigel's Syrup.</p>
        <p>Mrs. Greenwood, of IGa, Ringwood Road, Waltham. stow, relates an experience of suffering which is all too common, and would be almost universal but for the good work done by Mother Seigel's Syrup. Since 1868 (thirty-seven years ago !) this worlcbrenowned remedy has been known as the greatest cure for indigestion and biliousness, and for the numerous complaints which arise from those two ailments when neglected. "I can- not refrain from writing to tell you of the benefit I have derived from taking Mother Seigel's Syrup," says Mrs. Greenwood in a letter dated April 12, 1935. "For quite a long time I suffered from indigestion. Mv appetite was extremely poor ; and though sometimes felt as if I could eat a little, no sooner was food placed before me than the desire pissed, and I would turn away, leaving it untouched. When indeed I did eat anything, I was .always punished by great pain in the stomach ra.,A a sensation of pressure at the chest immediately afterwards. Sometimes there was also pain at the back between the shoulders.</p>
        <p>These are the ordinary ayi-ni;;;;;To—f.indigestion, and they constitute an illness in themselves. But, unfortu- nately, there are others. Nlrs. Greenwood continues : The headaches I endured were agonising. They were not like ordinary headaches, but infinitely more severe, and I was hardly ever free from them. Another of my troubles was flatulence.</p>
        <p>I tried a great number of proprietary medicines, and when all these failed, placed myself under the care of a doctor. But I derived no benefit whatever from either one or the other. On the contrary, my condition steadily became worse until the day I thought of V*lting Mother Soigel's Curative Syrup. Then, for the first time, I began to mend. The headaches became less frequent an 4 violent, and soon after ceased altogether; my appetite and strength returned, and in a short time I was my old self again, fully restored to health.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-12-02_WidnesExaminer_art0084" n="art0084">
        <head>WARRINGTON SENSATION. PRISONERS COMMITTED FOR TRIAL.</head>
        <p>THE WARRINGTON SENSATION. PRISONERS COMMITTED FOR TRIAL.</p>
        <p>The circumstances attending the discovery of the rnmains of a child buried under the pantry floor at 63, Grafton-street, were further enquired into by the War- rington magistrates on Tuesday when 13eatrice Water- worth (32),single woman, and Alicia Drury (35), widow, both of 10, Russell-street, were charged with the wilful murder of the infant child of the prisoner Waterworth and also with the concealment of its birth. Mr. Halloway, deputy town clerk, appeared to prosecute on behslf of the Director of Public Prosecutions and Mr. T. S. Steel defended the prisoners. Mrs. Drury, wife of John Drury, fish dealer, 69, Lairs; street, said the prisoner Drury, who was her daughter-in-law, some seven ruontha ii4o said she " had committed murder for them." Prisoner also said that the baby was born alive and she" took it in the other room and strangled it." Witness said to her" It would have been better to have let it live, and not have had that on your conscience." She then said her husband cursed her when she had done it and helped her to bury it. Witness cross-examined said that she received .C 26 inturance money on the death of her son, and her daughter-in-law also received as few pounds. They did have a dispute about the payment by the prisoner of part of the funeral expenses. It was after her daughter- in-law had left her house that she made a statement to the police. .</p>
        <p>Annie Atherton 11, Akin-street, said she formerly lived at 65, arafton.street, after the birth of the child she saw Waterworth in bed and asked her where it was. Mrs. Drury, who was in the room at the time said it was born dead and she had burnt it.</p>
        <p>Dr. Reginald Smith said he was OR° unable to say whether thechild had lived or not.</p>
        <p>Detective Meaco—cli WpOL—tO-I;isiting 63, Grafton- street, and there finding the bones of a child buried in quick lime under the pantry floor. Wnen before the Chief Constable prisoners made statements. Waterworth said:—"E did not touch the child." Alf and Mrs. Drury came upstairs and I said : What are you doing?' and he said : Nothing.' I said : 'What have you done with it, Mr. Drury?' and be said : • I have buried it in the pantry.' He said : I never he ird it cry.' Mrs. Drury wts not in the room when he said he bad buried it.</p>
        <p>Mrs. Drury said :—" That child was never born alive. I wrapped the baby up and my husband took it and put it in the front bedroom. I went out between seven and night o'clock and left my husband in downstairs and Beatrice upstairs. I missed the child end my husband said : I have taken it out of the house' but he would not say where. I saw lime in a piece of paper in de back kitchen. I suspected what bad happened and I asked him if he had buried it in the pantry and he said' I have done it for the best, it was still-born.' Witness added that the prisoners were given the opportunity of `withdrawing the statements if they chose to do so. Both said they wished them to go for- ward. THE DEFENCE.</p>
        <p>Mr. Steel slid he thought the Bench would agree with him when he said there was not the slightest evidence to sustain the chargo of murder. With respect to the other charge he suggested that before prisoners could bp committed for trial it must be °hewn that they actively participated in the coneealment. The evidence before them was that Mrs. Drury's husband was the ons who disposed of the child's body, and if the husband concealed the birth he would have been the one to stand charged, but fortunately for him, he was not hear. But had there been any concealment at all ? They knew that Mrs. Drury Wll9 told about the matter, and they knew that Mrs. Appleton was told about it by Drury in Waterworth's presence, therefore, he asked whether the evidence before them was uili- cient for any reasonable jury to convict these two women of concealrneut. Another point for them to consider was the mental condition of the woman Water- worth, who, he thought it was apparent, was not quite responsible for all her actions. He Would leave the matter there without putting the prisoners in the box. because they could only repeat the state:oents made to the Chief Constable. _</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-12-02_WidnesExaminer_art0085" n="art0085">
        <head>'THE VERDICT.</head>
        <p>'THE VERDICT.</p>
        <p>The magistrates retired, returning after absence of a few minutes. Mr. Roberts said they had decided that there was not sufficiect evidence to justify a commit- ment on a charge of murder, but they thought there was sufficient evidence to jnstify it on the charge of coucealraent. The first charge ivould there- fore be dismissed, and on the second the prisoners would take their trial at the next Liverpool Assizes. Both prisoners then pleaded not guilty, and reserved their defence.</p>
        <p>Mr. Steel applied for bail, and the Bench and the Chief Constable agreed to the same sureties as before— prisoners in £2O each and two other sureties of £2O. Mr. Steel applied ,ot behalf of the pri3oners for assistance under the For Prisoners Defence Act, 1903. but application was refused.</p>
        <p>/lIIMMEII</p>
        <p>CHRISTMAS MAGAZINES.</p>
        <p>TWO KINDS OF MONUMENT.</p>
        <p>The moment you pass the picturesque village of Grivitza you see monuments to the fallen everywhere, from the large, pretentious chapel of the ,Rounianians on the mama of the Glrivitza heights, to the plain white obelisk erected by some Russian regiment. There are over 300 of these monuments in the imturdiate sur- roundings of Plevna. Some are ereoted on giant graves The regiment Pensa lost, in the second of the four battles, 2,2J0 men out of 3,CC3 in an attack lasting twenty minutes, and one of the four inscriptions on the simple monument says" Sacred to the memory of the regiment Pease-, which lies buried here." At Gorni Duhnik, on the southern high-road, where a desperate fight occurred between a Turkish outwork and a Russian assailing column, a tent-tcle of the evergrowing invest- ing octopus, ono memorial crowns a gigantic grave of three thou id bodies. The monuments are all consecrated to the dead Russians and the dead Roumanians, never to the dead Turks, who know not stone memorials, for their im- pecuniosity has uttered a peremptory veto, and there is also a religious objection to them. History is their indelible and supreme monument. "To the Greek is wealth, to the Circassian beauty, to the Frank learning; but to the Osmanli is majesty:" says a Turkish proverb. Here in Plevna one realises the glorious dignity of silence, as practised by the Turk. The credit of the most famous, of the most sublimely heroic campaign of modern times is his ' • yet he never mentions it, either in writing or in speaking- eithet• publicly or privately ; and his dead heroes rest in forgotten graves. The Bulgarians talk glibly of war with Turkey. Those who reside in Plevna, or who have visited Plevnv, are not quite so fluent or quite so loud. Turkey is silent, but Plevrte,444,as_and, speaks for her, whose power of recuperation, whose latent might and majesty, whose blows, hard, swift, sure and cruel, have filled all think- ing men of Western civilisation with admiration,elmost with lawe, for live centuries past.—From " Plevna Revisited," in the" Cornhill Magazine " for December. CHRISTMAS GIFTS THAT GO ASTRAY.</p>
        <p>In the Christmas number Of the " Pall Mall Magazine "there is a vivid illustrated description of the way in which the Post Office deals with the flood of parcels at Christmas, and the way in which some of hese (often of the quaintest hind) miss their destina- ion.</p>
        <p>In this great whirl, many tokens of goodwill from various causes may fail to reach their destinations and find refuge in the limbo of tho lost. The contents of some derelicts are striking. In a tin mould was covered -lira sound condition a plum pudding which lad been sent to Australia and had found its way blick owing to the impossibility of tracing the lad to whom it was addressed. The contents of another parcel com- prised two petticoats, is pair of stays, a leg of mutton, and a parcel of tobacco. This motley collection was, however, eclipsed by a third, with the following con- tents—a large grey rabbit (dead, of course), containing in its inside two tobacco pipes, tobac?o, a doll, and a piece of bacon, the whole being wrapped up in a lady's jacket ! High sausages from Germloy and malodorous cheeses from Italy have perforce to be removed to places of safety ; and if Irishmen would devise some better covering than diaphanous muslin for greasy trussed geese and fresh butter, the coverings of the genteeler consignments they encounter would greatly benefit.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-12-02_WidnesExaminer_art0086" n="art0086">
        <head>NO HONOUK IN HIS OWN COUNTRY.</head>
        <p>NO HONOUK IN HIS OWN COUNTRY.</p>
        <p>The Christmas " ‘Vindsar " contains a very interest- ing article on "The Art of Mr. W. Deady Sadler," from which we quote the -following : " Mister, how much may you be gettin' for this 'ere pitchure ?' asked mine host of one of the inns where Mr. Sadler was at work. I Mr. Sadler, having nothing to conceal, and being in a ! gregarious mood, named the sum as fonr hundred pounds. The landlord shook his head, evidently considering the artist enormously overpaid. " Four hundrid pounds for that !' (he pointed a! finger of scorn at the canvas) 'Why, I couldn't get so , much for the old inn !' " "Another time one of the frequenters of the public- house in which Mr. Sadler had established hi meet and 1 the impediments of his work, offered him a sovereign to paint his portrait, stipulating, however, that it ' should be painted from an early photograph of him as , a smart young soldier, rather than from the burly I figure and aleoholie countenance to which he bad I attained. Seven-and-sixpence is a sum that has been I frequently offered as purchase-money when some local I connoisseur in art has desired to become the possessor of one of Mr. Sadler's pictures, an appraLement of value proving once again the truth of the proverb that a prophet has no honour in his own country." The Christmas numbar •of the "Windsor Magazine " is an amazing double issue, containing complete storks and articles by Rudyard Kiplin?, Miss Ellen Terry, Egerton Castle, S.R. Crockett, Eden Phillpotts, Frank Richardson lan Maclaren, Baroness Von Hutten and leading writers. A large opening instalment is also given of Anthony Dope's new serial story, " Sophy of Kravotia " in which the author makes a brilliant re appearance in his own field of " Zenda " romance. Another new series is inaugurated by Justus Miles Forman, and the articles of the number include the first of a series in which will be reprinted in the original colours all the cartoons of celebrities which have appeared in" Vanity Fair." The first thirty appear in this issue. The whole number is superbly illustrated. MESSRS. RAPHAEL TUCK AND SONS CHRISTMAS CARDS.</p>
        <p>So familiarised, we should think, must the general public be now with Messrs. Tuck's trademark the easel and palette " on Christmas cards, that it will be almost the first thing they look for when making their annual purchase of seasonable art novelties. Tuck's trade-mark stands for excellence, and having found it there is no need to look further. The firm is never behindhand, and of this season's Christmas goods, from Raphael House we can say that in everything that goes • to make a perfect collection—originality of style and design, beauty and finish of execution, range of subject, and artistic merit—Messrs. Tuck's latest contributions to the world of miniature art are first and foremost. Of Christmas and New Year cards there are 1,700 entirely new sets, and among a host of novel creations come the" °Roue art pendant " and " platioo art pend- ant series,which areladaptable for either wall or table.". A feature of the 150 assortments of boxed earth is their choice design and remarkable value, while a special</p>
        <p>NEM=lll%k</p>
        <p>art'cle would be needed to properly describe the iutiumerable new lines in picture post cards. Almost more so than in the Christmas card wortd are Messrs. Tack pre-eminent as publishers of art calendars and toy books. To-day, the public has the opportunity of 'selecting from a huge stock 300 Tuck calendars, each of which is endowed with a literary and artistic individu- ality of its own. "Father "Tuck has been vely busy, too, with his toy books and has covered miles of canvas with beautiful pictures for his little people, and acres of pages with charming stories, which will not only interest but educate. We would earnestly advise our readers to look at Messrs. Tuck's dainties first when on their Christmas shopping round, for they will probably not need to go any further to secure complete satisfactioo.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-12-02_WidnesExaminer_art0087" n="art0087">
        <head>"HOLLY LEAVES."</head>
        <p>"HOLLY LEAVES."</p>
        <p>There is no more seasaticible publication than "Holly Le'lves," the Christmas number of the " Illustrated Sporting and Diaz-natio News." It is alwa)s replete with the best Christmas literary and pictorial fare—in fact we do not know a magazine so splendidly illu,str,ited. Among the conHbutors this year of excellent short. stories are : Eden Philpotts, idoeley Robers, W. Pett. Ridge, Katherine Tyndall. al. Ccoker, Florence Warden, Arthur Clements, tt.c.. while the illustrators include almost all the best known knights of the pencil. As usual, too, there is a special coloured supplement—this time an exact reproduction of the historical paint- ing by Fred Roe, entitled" flood-bye, my Lads"! and depicts Nelson leaving Portsmouth for his last fight at sea. It is one of the best samples of reproduction in colours we have seen. "THE QUEEN."</p>
        <p>First and foremost among ladies' journals is "The Queen,"which comes out this mouth with its Christuma double number well-stocked with most interesting read- ing matter from the pens of titled ladies well-known in Society. Even the adverNernents form an interesting feature of this splendid magazine, and from cover to cover there is not a page without some attractive item. Each Christmas number is greatly enhanced in elle too by a high-class presentation plate in the forai of a Rembrandt Gravure reproduction, 30 by 22 inches, of the original painting by the well-kno artist, J. W. West, It.W.E., entitled " A lung story." This charming picture of the Georgian period was exhibited in the Royal Academy last yrsr and mo:t. favourably noticedat the time. The picture is artisti, - ally produced on proof paper, and as a real work of art is worth many timcs the sum charged for the Magazine.</p>
        <p>BLOOD AND SKIN DISEASES. BLOOD IS LIFE, LET IT BE PURE. Blotches' pimples on the face and body, scrofula, sores, itching and redness of the skin, letter and eczema, are symptoms which indicate impure blood and skin diseases, if your blood is pure your skin will be clear and healthy looking ; the direct cause of skin eruption is found in the blood. Purify the blood and you purify and clear the skin. The purifying and healing properties of VENo's WEED Tosac do this admirably. Treatment for a week or two will be so convincing us to place a cure even in the worst cases beyond doubt. The old way was to take sulphur, sarsaparilla, etc., the new way is to take SEAWEED, which is infinitely superior. VEND'S SEA- WEED TONIC not only purifies but enriches the blood. It acts constitutionally by strengthening the digestion and increasing assimilation and nutrition. Ask for VENO'S SEA WEED TON IC. Price Is. L'Al. and 2s. 9d. st Chemists everywhere.</p>
