      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-12-02_WidnesExaminer_art0088" n="art0088">
        <head>WIDNES SIIOPDREAKERS CA.REEA OV CRIME.</head>
        <p>WIDNES SIIOPDREAKERS CA.REEA OV CRIME.</p>
        <p>At Liverpool Assizes on Wednesday before Afr. Justice Ridley, John O'Brien (35),.labonier. was charged with having, at Widnes, on November 3, oroken and mitered the shop of Edwin Jolly, and stolen therefrom some tobacco, bacon, and other goods. He was also charged with having, at Ditton, on November 40, broken and entered the premises of the Ditton Catholic Young men's Society and stolen a number of billiard balls and chessmen.—He was found guilty on the first charge.—His Lordship said, prisoner commencsd his career of crime at the age of fifteen. He rust go to prison for twelve months.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-12-02_WidnesExaminer_art0089" n="art0089">
        <head>HOW LONDON STRUCK HER.</head>
        <p>HOW LONDON STRUCK HER.</p>
        <p>A girl of seven, • who had never left the country befoi e, was brought up to London for a day by a corres- pondent of the "County Gentleman." "I took her irk cab, in train' in omnibus ; we finished with an elephant ride at the Zoo," he says. "I expected raptures of admiration at the glories of tho city. tier verdict was, however, simplicity itself. 'How do you like London? I asked. My!' don't it stink?' It was her only reply."</p>
        <p>A Fora PER CENT. SectuaTr.—lnrestors on the look out for a safe 4 pez cent. Stock clientit do better than tub their attention to the sew Issue of £500,,C001 Swed.,ea Harbour Four pee Cent. "A" Stock (repro- Renting part of £2,000,000 authorkci by the Swansea Harbour Act of 1901). When the enlarged dock at Swansea is completed there will be accommodation for the largest ships afloat. It will t 3 remembered that H.M. the King cut the first sod of the new dock in July 1901, and the extensiors which are nearing com- pletion in this direction are amply justified by 'the wonderful figuras in the trade of Swansel. The reihrns shew that in 1901 the exports and imports reached tho very substantial figurt of 1,313,910 tons, Rad a very considerable increase will be snewo for the current year. The Issue will be made under the arsik rs of Messrs. F. J. Henson Rodeo.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-12-02_WidnesExaminer_art0090" n="art0090">
        <head>COMBINED RAIL kt; DINNER TICKETS.</head>
        <p>COMBINED RAIL kt; DINNER TICKETS.</p>
        <p>A rather novel ennouncement for this part of the country is made by the Cheshire Lines in the shape of the issue of daily combined rail and dinner tickets between Liverpool and Manchester Central. For 7r. 6d. third class and 108. Gd. first class a return ticket can be obtained, this entitling the holder to dine at the Grand Dining-room. Midland Hotel, adjoining the Central Station, Manchester. Several lines in the South of England issue these combination tickets, but the present announcement seems to mark a new departure for the northern counties.</p>
        <p>SIY• DEAR CHILDREN,— I hope you are all very busy preparing your essays for the Christmas competitions. I have not given any puzzles this week; as I feel sure it will be as much as you can do to get the essays finished by December 4th. I expect you all have Christmas holidays, so I propose offer- ing three nice prizes to my nieces for the ones who send me in the best-made garments that will be suitablewto give away to the poor. You need not make expensive things. I would sug- gest such articles as wdollen cuffs, scarves, baby's socks, or any such garments. S'o you can com- mence your work as soon as you like, but the articles must be sent to the office of this paper, addressed to Uncle Ned, not later than January 41-11.' I will offer a competition for the buys a little later.</p>
        <p>I have awarded prizes this week to the senders of the essays printed below;' also a post-card album to May Avery for a list of new. me)inbers, and a box of chocolates to Nellie Robetts for correet solutions to puzzles. I .must thank all those who have wiitten me letters, and sent in contributions. I shall pub- lish some of these latter When I. have space to spare,</p>
        <p>With all good wishes, from UNCLE NED.</p>
        <p>My Dear Uncle,—l was delighted to have a letter from you this morning. I shall like to try and solve the puzzle, as I am very fond of puzzle-finding. I am very pleased that siome of our members have succeeded ' in getting two dozen members for our League. Dear Uncle, I will take more interest irr•the Children's Corner than I have clone in the past. Well, dear Uncle, I have succeeded in getting one dozen names for our League. I have done my very best, and can only get one dozen, and they all seem very anxious to start another week. I am sure I have been trying very hard to get twenty-four, but could not succeed. One of them is my sister. We are twins, and have both left school. Francis is my youngest brother. We have such a dear little kitten, and we call him Snip. He is black, with a white breast. We also have a large cat. She is the mother of the kitten, but she does not like it a bit, and swears at it. I will try my very hardest to solve the puzzle.—l remain, Your affectionate</p>
        <p>NIECE MAY.</p>
        <p>A PRIZE ESSAY. POOI,E. Poole is not a very larke town, though it is a port, which has a harbour, where all ships come and go, as it has not a pier. It is an important manufacturing town. It has twine fac- tories, clay pits, and boat-building yards. The -wood industry in Poale is the largest in Dorset. Poole is at the east of Dorset. It has a Public .Free Library. I think Poole is a very nice town. Near' 'upper Park Stone is Constitution Hill, from the summit: of which you can get a view of Poole and Lower .Parkstone, Sandbanks, Brownsea Island, and Purbech ; 4so a portion of . Poole Park. Poole has a very large park, which has three lakes. One of them is a salt water lake, and as sixty acres of water, the other two being fresh water. The salt lake looks beautiful on a fine surnmer morning when the sun shines, and makes it like a sheet of glass. This lake is used for boating in the summer months, and boats Can be hired at moderate charges. Private boats are also kept on this lake. It also affords a great pleasure to the children of Poole, where they paddle in</p>
        <p>7." the summer. . There is a model yacht club-house, where the yachts are kept. These yachts race across the lake, and sometimes the owner of the , winner • gets a prize. Several swans swim on the lakes. One of the fresh water lakes is very picturesque. It has an island in the centre, where peacocks, ducks, swans, and pigeons live at the side. of the lake. This lake has a rustic bridge, and the water flows under it into another portion, at the end of which are the cricket grounds, which are one-third of a mile round. The fresh water lake has eight acres of water. The other lake is not quite so old as the other two, and is only about two or three years old:. It has also an island, but not so large as the .‘ other. Shrubs are growing , over this island, which are still young. The tram-ears run along the outside of tips park to Poole Station. GLADYS M. KENDALL.</p>
        <p>A PRIZE ESSAY. WRITTEN IN THE KING'S NAME. Frederick the Great was about to build Sans- Souci. Right in the centre of the ground pro- posed for its beautiful garden stood an old mill. Wishing to have it removed, the Emperor sent for the miller. "My good man," he said, " I want to buy your mill." will not sell it, . please, your Majesty." " I will give you double its value." "I thank your Majesty, but I will not sell it for any price whatever." " Why? " "Because for generations it has been in my father's family, and I mean it to pass down to</p>
        <p>But do .you not know. that I have</p>
        <p>iwer to take it? Aye, without paying you a single farthing ! " " Yes, sire," replied the miller, most respectfully, "were it not for the Chamber of Laws in Berliti which contains our country's laws, among them the special law of inheritance, written in your Majesty's name, and sealed with your Majesty's seal, which none, dare reverse. You are too just, a sovereign by far to take my ancestors' mill." "You have won your case, my friend," exclaimed Frederick the Great, charmed with the compliment, .and shaking with much warmth his neighbour's hand. "By all means pass the mill down to your childrqn's children. I will change my garden ground." To-day, were you to visit ‘Sans-Souci, I believe you could still see the old mill.</p>
        <p>ERIC PARKINSON.</p>
