      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-12-02_WidnesExaminer_art0091" n="art0091">
        <head>LIST OF NEW MEMBERS.</head>
        <p>LIST OF NEW MEMBERS.</p>
        <p>Gladys M. Kendall, Ella Kendall, Ida B. Evans, Emily L. Guest, 0. King,clon, Mary Wilde, Fred Brown, Florrie Timins, Alice Dowell, john Wilson, C. Saunders, Freda Wellbourne, Fred Martin, Edie Thompson, Walter Rodgers, Noel Clifton, Jessie E. Davis, Louie Griffin, Dorothy Cross, Lucy, Davis, Margaret M. Press; :Walter Rodgers, Dorothx Parker, Mary Avery, Annie Footitt, Francis Avery, May Avery, Nellie Arnold, Lily Arnold, Annie Bullock, Laura Walker, May Walker, Annie Holland, and Lila Broughton.</p>
        <p>THE LETTER CAKE. (A Tiny .Cluistinas Tale.)</p>
        <p>Betty is deaf, and I am blind. Betty is my maid, and we live on the river-bank in a white house—they say it is white—and are as happy together as two bees in a rose. There is this difference between Betty and me—l know I am. blind, but she doesn't know, she is deaf. I bare to ring a very large bell; and half the time she doesn't hear it; and once when it thundered, she said: " Did yon speak, ma'am?"</p>
        <p>I pity Betty, and wouldn't ,for the world have her know how deaf she is, •</p>
        <p>My name is Mrs. Polly Pope; but I am "Aunt Polly " to all, the good chkhlren in toWn. Perhaps the one I hold closest and kiss oftenest is little Lena Paul. I knit 'worsted stockings for half the village, but for Lena I knit nothing but silk. She is very' dear and sweet, and has set me in her prayers, all of her own accord. 'Her mother says that sometimes, • after her little head is on the pillow, she exclaims : "0, I fe-got to bless Aunt Polly ! " 'Then she springs out of bed, knees down again, and says : " Please bless Aunt Polly—knits my stockings—can't see." God has always blessed me, and surely He always will, when a loving child is asking Him. One day—it was the ,day before Christmas— Lena came to my house just as Betty and I were starting for the chapel with a basket of clothes for th.e poor children. I did not quite like to take lier with us; for she is as frisky as a squirrel, and chatters quite as much; but •go she would. -</p>
        <p>When we arrived, She wanted all the little frocks, hoods, and petticoats, and everything else she saw i Mrs. Hay called the poor children to the platform to get some shoes; and Lena whispered : "I want a pair of shoes, Aunt Polly." " Fie ! " said I, " you don't need them any more than a fly need, a pair of spectacles."</p>
        <p>. - . ".My shoes is all wored-ed," said she. We were, glad to get her home, Betty and I. She took my hand, and prattled to me all the way.</p>
        <p>Lena is only three years old, and she was uncommonly full of mischief that day'. _ _</p>
        <p>" What will I do for a pudding?" said Betty, after we had been at home about five rpinuteJ. "I had mixed one, ready to bar.e, and'tte baby has thrown it into the ash-barrel."</p>
        <p>Little rogue! She set the water running in the kitchen, and I had to go but and stop it, for Betty didn't hear. And soon Betty was say- ing:</p>
        <p>Naughty Lena, to pull the needles out of Aunt Polly's knitting-work; when poor Auntie dan't see." .I brought out the coloured picture•books, and then Lena was happy for w few minutes.</p>
        <p>" I know every letter there is in the world ! " she declared; and she began to read some sur- prising stories aloud to me, in a little, high, squealing tone : " Once there was a little boy, and the wind Wowed him, and bime-by it Wowed his hair right off." °lice there was a wee, wee girlie, and she had thou.sands dollies. Couldn't hear, and couldn't see. Cow came, ate 'em all up.' "_</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-12-02_WidnesExaminer_art0092" n="art0092">
        <head>"_There, now, guess_ I'll go out seeßetty."</head>
        <p>"_There, now, guess_ I'll go out seeßetty."</p>
        <p>_ She shut the door behind her so softly that I suspected mischief. So I went .out and told Betty to give the child some soapsuds and let hef blow bubbles, for I wanted to keep her a good while—l knew her mother was busy. "Yes, ma'am," said Betty.</p>
        <p>I went back to the parlour, expecting soon to hear Lena screaming with delight over the bubbles. But Betty made such a clatter, beat- ing eggs ,for d fresh pudding and !lamming the oven 6d'oor, that Lena's little voice was. quite drowned.</p>
        <p>_ "Betty is a noisy woman," thought I. A whole hour passed, and I did not hear a sound from Lena. I rang the bell twice for Betty, and asked what the child was doing. "I thought she was with you, ma'am," replied Betty. (To be Concluded.)</p>
      </div>
