      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-12-02_WidnesExaminer_art0094" n="art0094">
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, December 2, 1905.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-12-02_WidnesExaminer_art0095" n="art0095">
        <head>LIBERAL DEMONSTRATION AT FRODSHAM.</head>
        <p>LIBERAL DEMONSTRATION AT FRODSHAM.</p>
        <p>Mr. Haldane, VI P., and the Next Goverment.</p>
        <p>A great Liberal demenstratitha was held in the Drill Hall, Frodsham, on Saturday evening, the principal speakers being the Right Hon. R. B. Haldane, M.P., Lord Crewe, Sir John Brunner, MT., Mr. S. Moss, M.P., and the Hon. A. Lyulph Stanley, the prospective Liberal candidate for the division. Mr. Herbert Gladstone telegraphed :—" Best wishes for the success of ycur meeting, and of your candidate. Government in a most pitiable condition." Lord Crewe, who presided, said that the Country would not be satisfied now with the mere resignation of the Government. What was demanded was that Parlia. ment should, as early as possible, be dissolved. This immediate crisis had Arisen out of the character of the men most prominent in the recent fiscal agitation. Mr. Chamberlain at least showed that he was con- ducting his campaign on the lines on which he had conducted his previous campaigns— those which were eescribed in a well-known phrase at the time of the French Revolution Mr. Chamberlain was a man who said—" Swear eternal brotherhood with me, or I will break your head." Mr. Balfour had succeeded for an almost impossible time in apparently holding at the same moment diametrically opposite opinions on the most important subject of the day. But be had broken down at last, and that was how the present crisis had arisen. The country would deal presently in a proper way with those who had been so recklessly extravagant. (Applause.) Mr. Haldane said the present situation was a earning to the Liberal party. Why had Mr. Balfour come to grief ? It was not merely that his speeches savoured of Protectionist doctrines ; it was not that he had said anything very serious. It was what he had not said. There was One thing the people of this country would not stand. It was that their chief, their leader—and a Prime Minister was no Prime Minister who was not both chief and leader—should fail to put before the country in deficite, unmistakable language his policy. At present every tactical advantage was with the Liberal party, and Liberals must see to it that that advantage was not thrown away. There was a great issue before the country—the issue of whether we should abandon our Free Trade policy—and on that ; issue we wanted, rind ought to have, the judgment of I the country. The Liberal party should, not frame its l programme until it knew what the electoral strength behind it would be. These were no days for spinning programmes. But the avoidance of spinning pro- grammes did not in the slightest degree relieve those who contemplated a majority for their party from the obligation to realise clearly th 3 end of the rend along which they were going to tread and the direction lin which 'they wished to go. There was all the difference in the world between a detailed ! programme and a policy which contained broad ! views aftereards to be embodied in a programme. ! Many people talked as if the Liberal party had nothing to do but to get a majority, resolve to do this or that, ' and then set to week to do it. The course was not so easy as that. In almost every quarter of the political bot iron there were signs of trouble. For the moment ! foreien affairs were quiet. But ub statesman could be certain for a week tt a time what foreign troubles he might hake to lace. There were ominous clouds in the; Far Eaet, at Constantinople. which might be the pre- ' cursors of a storm. In South Africa there might be serious difficulties. The Libeeate had work to do there ; which might turn out to he very difficult. He did not ; envy thetposition of the Colonial Secretary after all the , efforts that had been made to create prejudice against Liberal policy. Coining a little nearer home the dile- , culties were greater still. There was a country not far • away that would not 'allow us to leave it out of account and would make great cluinis upon the atten- tion of the next Government. There was no greater ' delusion than that of thinking that the Irish problem I , could be left out of sight. It might very rightly be ' said that it will be impossible to make large or sweeping changes within the purview of the next Parliament. There were, however, no reasons which in the least ! degree conflicted with what ought to be the general Liberal attitude towards the Irish question. His own conviction was unaltered, and in the end the Irish must ! have control cf those domestic affairs which concerned 'them and not us. But what he wished to ernphasise was that it was necessary to educate people to that idea, , and that they could only be so educated by means of ' some co-operation with the Irish leaders themselves. It ens a matter of confidence between the two peoples. : The wise and Liberal course Wes to place responsibility : where power virtually was and to leave the people of Ireland to educate, themselves in the administration of ! their own affaire. There was avast amount of work to he done in Ireland. At present the country was adminis- tered in the interests and under the denomination of a ! small clique. Should the Liberals come into power, the first thing they would have to see to , was that the government of Ireland was a govern- ment" for the people, through the people, by the people." If we could not do this directly, we could do it indirectly. We could see to it that the voice of the majority prevailed in the administration of affairs at Dublin Castle. We could see to it that the large industrial and economic questions which were arising in Ireland %%ere things in which the Government and the people co-operated, so that the people might have ! the feeling that although the Government was not their own Government in the sense of an Irish Parliament, yet it was a government which was in sympathy eith ! them end desired to work for them. Mr. Haldane referred aftereards to education and other problems of' the tuture. Sic John Brunner moved a resolution in , which the meeting accorded its thanks to ;11r. Haldane, expressed its loyalty to the Liberal party, its condem- nation of the reactionary policy of the Governmeneand , its determination to assist in returning a Liberal mm- for Eddisbery. The resolution was seconded by Mr. Samuel Moss, M.P., supported by thecHon. A. 1.3 ulrh Stanley, and carried.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-12-02_WidnesExaminer_art0096" n="art0096">
        <head>RUNCORN RURAL DISTRICT</head>
        <p>RUNCORN RURAL DISTRICT</p>
        <p>COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>, Small-pox at Stockton Heath.</p>
        <p>IThe fortnightly meeting of the Runcorn Rural Dis- trict Council was held at Dutton on Tuesday when 1 there were present—Messrs. S. Woodward (presiding), : C. BoYlance, J. Bolsbaw, H. Eyes, P. Mountield, G. Egerton Warburton, J. Stokes, A.Broom, T.Baixter, U.E. Linaker, J. H. Davies, J. Stnith,T.Wright,H. Maw cisley, ; T. Guest, I. Davies. W. Harrison, S. Horton, J. L. .Jones, J. Fryer, J. Spruce, J. Whitley C. E. Lintiker, jun., W. Weir, J. B. Chadwick, G. 14',2Asiiton (clerk), W. i. Bough (assistant clerk), J. Swinton and W. i niggle (surveyois), and Dr. Adams (medical officer).</p>
        <p>MOORI.: SWING</p>
        <p>he clerk rep-olte'CltVai...iir:ll7(ls-o-niir.eave, engineer to the Manchester Ship Canal Company, .had written with reference to the complaint about the swing bridge noross the canal at Moore being closed for repairs with- out notice, saying that the bridge wasolosed on account of a breakdown. In the event of its having again closed twenty-four hours notice would be given.</p>
        <p>COIINCIL AM) MR. C. E. CIIAPPEL. Mr. C. E. Chappel wrote with regnrcl to certain plans for houses in Warburton-street, which were rejected by _</p>
        <p>the Stockton Heath Parochial Committee, asking that a 1 committee of the district council should be appointed to attend the site for the new houses. Mr. Woodward: I strongly object to being on a committee and attend upon a gentleman whose repre- ' sentati ,es have not carried out their promises with regard to another matter. it INDS decided to refer the matter to the Stockton Heath Parochial Committee.</p>
        <p>KM:TS FORD-ROA D sEwEn.</p>
        <p>A letter was read from Mr. Pilling stating that be approved of the scheme drawn up with respect to the sewer in Knutsforcl-road. As the scheme would be of equal advantage to the residents in Grappenhall as well as in Latchford he thought they should bear at least half the cost.</p>
        <p>The OA ns for' the sewer were approved,and the tender of Mr. nouns Stringer to complete the work for £4O 15s. W. was accepted</p>
        <p>THE MULBERRY TREE HOTEL.</p>
        <p>Plans for the re-constrttction of the Mulberry Tree Hotel were approved.</p>
        <p>SNIA- LL-PON AT STOCKTON HEATH.</p>
        <p>Dr. Adams reported that during the fortnight a case of smallpox had occurred in Old London-road, Stockton Heath. The patient was immediately removed to the hospital at Moore. The man worked at Warrington. No specific Cause of infection could be ascertained. All contacts were being kept under observation. A case of scarlet fever had occurred at Moore. The Medical Officer's report was approved and it was decided to issue instructions for the new isolation hospital to be got ready immediately for the accommo- dation of the patient suffering from scarlet fever, and also to engage a temporary nurse.</p>
      </div>
