      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-12-02_WidnesExaminer_art0126" n="art0126">
        <p>DEATH OF MR. C. H. BROADBENT, J.P.</p>
        <p>The death took place on Thursday at the Hollies, Latchford, Warrington, of Mr. Charles Harold Broa- bent,'J.P., of Upton Heys, Chester, son of the late Mr. Charles Broadbent, Warrington, and son-in-law of Sir .3. Brunner. Deceased was. until retirement recently, a member of the firm of Morris and Broadbent, stock- b rolaen.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-12-02_WidnesExaminer_art0127" n="art0127">
        <head>LETTERS TO THE EDITOR</head>
        <p>LETTERS TO THE EDITOR</p>
        <p>NOTICE TO CORRESPONDENTS. Authors of letters intended for publication must in aii cases send their names and addresses to the Editor as a guarantee of good faith.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-12-02_WidnesExaminer_art0128" n="art0128">
        <head>Secondary School Prize Giving --Why Not For Elementary Also ?</head>
        <p>Secondary School Prize Giving --Why Not For Elementary Also ?</p>
        <p>Sir,—l was present at the prize giving in connection with Secondary Education at the Parr Hall last Wednesday. I was delighted to see the large number of prize winners and the appreciation shown by the large audience present, but the thought came into my mind, as one greatly interested in Elementary as well Secondary Education. Why does the Education Com- mittee allow sums of money to be spent in prizes for Secondary and refuse them for Elementary Education? Under present conditions no prizes whatever are granted to Elementary schools by the central authority, so far as I can find out. If the Education Committee can see its way to give prizes for school work in the Secondary school, then surely it ought to see its way to giveprizes in Elementary schools also. T am assuming that the prizes given last Wednesday will be paid for out of the funds of the borough and not from any private source,except in the cases mentioned daring the prize giving. J. H. G. HOLLINGWORTH.</p>
        <p>December Ist 19n.</p>
        <p>Retaliate with Brains. Sir,—Dr. Markel's letter, read at the meeting of the Warrington Free Trade League, goes with commend- able boldness to the root of one of the chief sources of this unhealthy fiscal agitation. It would certainly be ungracious and unjust to class all the manufacturers who are supporting by money and sympathy the Pro- tectionist movement as inefficient, and to describe their words as out of date. But in every trade the most casual observer can note that the ranks of the Tariff Reform movement are mainly composed of those who desire to find through Protection a refuge—a very insecure one—for incapacity. Dr. Markel tells us that in the last twenty years of Free Trade the firm with which he is associated has increased its wage sheet ten- fold, and foretells further expension in the years that are to come provided always that the country holds to its Free Trade policy. Speaking of another trade that of tanning, we can report a like advance in the 'works with which I am associated. Twenty years ago we tanned about GOO hides per week. To-day at the Penketh works together with the works we have established tesewhere we tan over 6000 hides per week. This industry is often instanced as one of the decaying ones. I can speak freely of this progress, as I claim no special credit for it, being only like one of the cogs in a large wheel. Dr. Markel will doubtless associate himself with me in saying that we much prefer to con- tinue the development of our individual industries in quietness. But the attack that has been made upon one of the main conditions upon which we depend for success makes it imperative that we should break silence and stand on guard to:maintain what, in my opinion, is essential to general prosperity and particularly to the provess of our own industries. But some may complain why should we who believe in Free Trade for our own industries object to those, say the iron trade, getting Protection for their produc- tiop.Such forget that all trades are interdependent,that industry is aglatinated throughout, that whatever trade is protected all others have to pay the benefit this trade derives through the increased price of their production. Let me illustrate this by reference to two of the trades suggested. At first sight it might appear that an increase in the price of iron,the result of protecting this article, would have no effect on an industry so distinct as tanning. But when we remember that the cost of our raw material is largely determined by the freights and carriage which have to be added to the original value, we see how necessary it is to have cheap iron for the construction of ships and other means of transport. The docks and railway charges are affected by the cost of iron in the building of docks, cranes, warehouses, rails, locomotives, wagons, bridges, good stations, itc. These directly affect the transit costs, but when we have figured on these we have by no moans got to the end of the burden on the tanning trade consequent on an increased price of iron. The effect on the purchasing power of the wages we pay has to be taken into account. Our workers will have to pay more for the necessaries of life. The machines that make their clothes, the poker that stirs their fire, the oven that bakes their bread, all have to pay their quota. Dearer iron influ- ences the cost of living in a thousand directions, and this not alone to the direct workers, but to tbe secondary ones as well. All classes would have to receive more (or be worse off) from the doctor who helps us into this world to the parson who helps us into the neat. It will therefore be seen that any form of Protection in any trade cannot he viewed with indifference by any part of the community,and all who realize the advantages of an open market have a right to protest against any curtailment of their right to buy on the best terms all the world has to offer. The best form of retaliation is to retaliate with brains. In our English industrial system the need of the hour is leaders and workers educated, altert, self- reliant and energetic. With these we will progress with or without Protection. But Free Trade gives these human forces their freest expression and richest reward, while Protection hampers their development and paralyses their influence. CHARLES E. PARKBR. Penketh, November 21th, 1905.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-12-02_WidnesExaminer_art0129" n="art0129">
        <head>HOSPITAL. SUNDAY. _ Decrease in the Collections.</head>
        <p>HOSPITAL. SUNDAY. _ Decrease in the Collections.</p>
        <p>The annual meeting of the Warrington Hospital -Sunday Committee was held at the Inflamary this (Friday) afternoon, there being a good attendance. Mr. A. J. JOLLY submitted the annual report, in which he said five places of worship which contributed to the Hospital Sunday collections in 1901 failed to do so in 1905 while seven new churches commenced or arrroaged for contributions. On that head there was a loss on the year of Is. 4d. The total receipts had been £2SS 7s. id., a decrease on those ofnast year of £lO Os. 6d. Of the total receipts £lB9 Ils. 7d. was collected within and £93 15s. Od. outside the borough. The expenses had been £lB 69. tut—including £ll 15s. 6d. for advertising—as compared with £l7 spent in 1904. The report was approved. Mr. A.. 1. Jolley was unanimously re-elected bon. secretary of the committee, and was heartily thanked for his past services. It was decided to hold the next hospital Sunday collection at the latter end of March, 1909.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-12-02_WidnesExaminer_art0130" n="art0130">
        <head>WEEKLY REPORT ON THE HEALTH OF WARRINGTON.</head>
        <p>WEEKLY REPORT ON THE HEALTH OF WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>' •</p>
        <p>-- - WEEK ENDING NOVENIBER 25th 1005. During the week, the following births and deaths were registered:—Warrington Registration District births, 39, deaths, 23. The excess of births over deaths was 11. The birth-rate was equal to 29.3. The death-rate from all causes was equal to 21-0 per 1,000 against 13-0 the average of the previous 6 weeks and against 131 the corresponding period of last year. The death rate from the seven chief zymotic diseases was equal to 2.2 per 1,000, against 2.2 the Average rate of the previous 6 weeks and against nil the corresponding period of 1904. The ages of mortality were :- 0-1 1-5 5-15 15-25 25-65 65—up. Total 8 6 1 11 2 28. The deaths included-1 from •whooping cough, 2 from diarrhcea,2 from tubercular diseases. 2 from cancer, 3 from lung diseases, 3 from heart disease, 3 from suicide, 12 from all other causes. The following cases of infectious diseases have been 'reported during the week :--Scarlet fever 2 cases, erysipelas 1 case, plathisis 4 cases. Total number of patients in hospital, week ending 25th November, 1905, 37 ; Warrington 10; Newton, 14 ; Rural District, 4. METEOROLOGY.—The mean temperature was 39 Fah. ancl therefore 3 degs. higher than the preceding week. The maximum temperature 51 F., was recorded on the 22nd inst., and the minimum temperature 25 F., on the 19th inst. The rainfall measured '43 inch. The general direction was W.S.W. J. GUEST GORNALL. Medical Officer of Health.</p>
        <p>Popular Concerts. There was again a large audience at the Bold-street Wesleyan schoolroom on Saturday evening at the popular concert. An excellent programme was sub- rmtted by Messrs. Greenough and Oittings and Misses Bridge mid Barnes (songs). The Warrington Apollo Quartet Party contributed several selections in an admirable manner, and Mr. Cornelius Furness scored S tames with his Lancashire sketches. The chairman was councillor Evans and the Rev. J. Manning also took part in the meeting. Mr. Brough acted as accom- panist. On Saturday evening next the whole enter- tainment will be provided by Mr. Brough's Juvenile Choir.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-12-02_WidnesExaminer_art0131" n="art0131">
        <head>LOCAL EDUCATION.</head>
        <p>LOCAL EDUCATION.</p>
        <p>DISTRIBUTION 9F PRIZES IN THE PARR HALL.</p>
        <p>Vice-Chancellor Dale and the Aims of Education.</p>
        <p>THE WORK IN WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>The Parr Hall was filled in every part on Thursday evening when the annual presentation of prizes and cer- tificates to the students of the School of Art, Secondary school a ndTechnical Institute took place.VimChancellor Dale, M.A., of the University of Liverpool, distributed the awards and delivered a very interesting address on the aims of education which was listened to with great attention. Prior to the meeting a short concert was held and was much appreciated by the audience who warmly applauded the various items. Mr. W. H. Payton Mus. Bac.' gave several organ solos with his customary taste and skill. Selections by the secondary school orchestra were a pleasing feature, the violincello solo in one of these items being given by Master Wilfred M. Barton. Haydn's "Toy symphony" proved especially popular. A pianoforte duet "Tarantella " (Ralf) was creditably given by Miss Daisy Mold and Miss Isabel Price. Later in the evening Miss Lena Duckworth who possesses a very sweet voice sang "The Swallows" (Cowen) in a very praiseworthy manner and was heartily encored. A part song by some of the Secondary School scholars and teachers was also well received.</p>
        <p>The meeting was presided over by Alderinan Bennett (chairman of the Education Committee) who was supported by Vice-Chancellor Dale and a large gathering of members of the Education Committee and Town Council,and the staffs of the schools.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-12-02_WidnesExaminer_art0132" n="art0132">
        <head>PROGRESS OF EDUCATION IN WARRINGTON.</head>
        <p>THE PROGRESS OF EDUCATION IN WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>The CHAIRMAN in his opening address said the teachers had been sowing the good seed. Some of it had fallen by the way side and some, perhaps, on stony places, but a good deal of it, he thought, had brought forth fruit, some thirty, some sixty and some a hundred fold. The results of the examinations were in the main satisfactory. Alderman Bennett quoted the figures in the various departments which have already been summarised in the" Examiner." Their scheme of education, he continued, was gradually evolving and he thought as it evolved it ,;•.•as justifying itself, and even those who had been disposed to criti- cise the Education Committee in its early stages were beginning to realize that it was doing extremely good work. In the elementary schools the staffs bad been materially strengthened, many important structural alterations had been made, and the results were seen in the great improvement in the reports of the inspectors. Plans had been passed for the erection of two new schools on the most modern principles, and he thought that in regard to elementary education Warrington would soon be able to bold up its head with any town in the north of England. Scholar- ships had been organized by which all classes of chil- dren were catered for, and opportunities given them to pursue their studies beyond the ordinary stage. The evening schools had been entirely re-organized, and thoroughly merited the name of continuation schools. They were valuable feeders to the Technical Institute, which had suffered in the past from the defici- ency in the earlier stages of education. The manufacturers could help the Committee in regard to technical education more particularly and in regard to all education more or less very materially and several public-spirited firms in the town were working hand in hand with the committee and doing all they could to encourage the younger members of their staffs to attend the classes and so qualify themselves for the higher appointments which no doubt they would ulti- mately hold. The Secondary school had met a real want and the committee could not admit the whole of the children who made application. Since their last meeting the negotiations with the Grammar School bad been practically completed and in a very abort time they hoped that ancient foundation—the oldest educe tional establishment in Warrington—would be brought to some extent under the control of the committee and that it would be worked on lines which would be of even .gre tter advantage to the future generation than the school had been for many previous generations. A higher elementary school had been talked of and possibly a high school for girls in some distant period, but those schemes were outside the committee's immediate purview. After expressing pleasure that the committee was in such intimate touch with the sister universities of Manchester and Liver- pool, the Chairman proceeded to give advice to the students, likening the pursuit of knowledge to the ascent of mountains. It was not given to all of them to climb the loftier mountains and he who would attempt the Alps had better start with Hill Warren. (Laughter,) They must begin at the beginning and every step they took would help and strengthen them for better work and loftier ambitions. The best reward of their studies, said Alderman Bennett in conclusion, was entirely independent of mere success or failure. Knowledge was like virtue and was very largely its own reward. (Applause).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-12-02_WidnesExaminer_art0133" n="art0133">
        <head>PRIZE LIST.</head>
        <p>THE PRIZE LIST.</p>
        <p>Vice-Chancellor DALE then distributed the prizes as follows :</p>
        <p>Scßoof. OF ART :—Maud K. Browne, first class certi- ficate model drawing; Ellen Daintith and Emily K. Robinson,second class certificates model drawing; Mabel Walker, second class certificate light and shade : Agnes E. Richardson, second class certificate in light and shade, and perspective ; Lilian Wright, first class cer- tificate model drawing and second class freehand;Jave E. Pearson, first class certificate drawing on the blackboard, seconds in light and shade and geometrical ; William E. R. Collins and Harold R. Iddon, second class certifi- cates, freehand; Frank S. Phillip., first class certificate model drawing ; William J. Welsby, second class certi- ficate freehand; Isaac A. Simpson, second class certificates in architecture, and building construction; Paul W. Brunt, second class certificates in drawing bn the black- board, model and freehand : Dobson T. Clark, first class certificates in model drawing, drawing on the black- board and freehand, seconds in perspectivo, light and shade, and geometrical. Board of Education elementary drawing certificate ; William H. emu, Sfirst class cer- tificate in drawing on the blackboard,seconds in perepec- tive, freehand, light and shade, practical inorganic chemistry and theoretical inorganic chemistry, and Board of Education elementary drawing certificate ; John Clarke, second class certificate freehand, and box of compasses ; Arthur Clare, first class certificate model drawing second class freehand and box of colours. SECONDARY SCHOOL AND PUPIL TEACHERS' CENTRE —Form I : Minnie Grounds, drawing, needlework. head master's prize for best relief map : Mildred Lowe; class peize, physics, French ; Percy Crampton, best pro- gress mathematics; Edward Lidiard, chemistry, Reginald Wallington, woodwork. Form II : May Baker, best progress needlework. chemistry; Monica Vernon, French, physics : William T. Eccles, class prize mathematics, English ; Thomas . Groarke history and geography ; Theodore Parry, wood- work, headmaster's prize for Brushwork design; Oswald White, history, geography, drawing. Form 111. : Margaret A. G. Ashton, class prize ; Eliza Ball, French ; Julia Sexton, best progress ; Ethel Shepherd, English, history, geography. needlework; Ada J. West, drawing ; James U. Simcock, mathematics, physics, chemistry. Form IV, : Winifred A. Brock, best progress ; Marion Collin, drawing ; Annie I, Jackson, history and geography. needlework ; Florence M. Lowe, English ; Dora E. Parsonage, class prize, mathematics and chemistry ; Jessie Upson, French ; Charles M. Price, woodwork, physics. Form V. : Annie Dallow, best progress ; Evaegeline L. Dell, needlework : Lucy M. Fawcett, class prize, physics, chemistry; Dorothy M. Johnson, mathematics, English, drawing May Kirby, history and geography ; Annie Upson, French ; Joseph Benion, woodwork. Form VI. : Gertrude Atherton, French; Edith M. Isaac, English, history and geography; Marian Jolley, mathematics and certificate in geometry; Marion Price, needlework; Elsie Swaine, class prize, drawing and certificates in freehand and model; Arthur Clark, physics; Fred IL Jolley, chemistry, woodwork, physics note-book prize and certificates in freehand and geometry. Form VII.: Annie Bell, physics, English essay; Ethel Bunch, mathematics, needlework and certificate in freehand drawing; Kathleen M. Cole, historical essay; Frances B. Dunne, English; Annie G. Edgar, history and geography, chemistry: 'Attica Warburton, French, chemistry note-book, prize; Percy F. Boardman, woodwork; Stanley C. Jones. best pro- gress and certificates in model and geometrical drawing. FIRST YEAR Pura, TEACHERS :—Beatrice Antrolons, needlework; Gertrude Farrell. best progress and certifi- cate in model drawing ; Mary Gandy, class prize, mathematics, general science ; Frances Jolley, history and geography; Lucy Travis, English ; Eva Wood, drawing ; John Kenny, language. SCHOLARSHIF PUPIL TEACHERS :—Annie Daintith, class prize, mathematics; Eva Tsylor, drawing ; Edith Townley, needlework and certificate in freehand draw- ing; Mary Wright, English, history and geography ; Alfred Cragg, language. TEC HNIC A L INSTITUTE :—Mary A. Blake end Elizabeth Caldwell, certificates in dresscutting ; Elsie M. Greenough, bookkeeping; Maria Hemmings, Ada Hesketh and Maggie Holcroft, dresscutting; Agnes McGrath, shorthaad ; Mary G. Mainwaring, dress- cutting; Agnes Parr, certificates in dresscutting and millinery ; Emma Parr, certificate in dresscutting ; Florence Pemberton, certificate in shorthand ; Jane Sankey, certificates in dresscutting and practical , cookery; Beatrice Speakman,certificate in dresscutting; Sarah Webb, certificate in practical cookery ; Martha M. Ward. advanced certificate in French with distinc- tion; also awarded prize by Union of Lancashire and Cheshire Institutes. Courses : Nellie James, Lucy Cranigan, and Achi Monks, domestic; Stella Hamilton, I commercial, and certificate in shorthand; Mabel E.</p>
        <p>!Pearce, commercial, and certificate in sick nursing ; Eveline Wood, commercial, and certificates in French and shorthand ; Marion Campbell, domestic, and certi- ficates in human physiology, hygiene, and sick nursing; Catherine Grounds, Annie Hunstone, Lily Hughes, domestic,and certificates in dresscutting and millinery ; Elizabeth Lloyd, certificates in principles of cookery, practical cookery, and millinery: Edith Reddish, certificate in dresscutting; Edward Bowers, certificate in arithmetic ; Percy Clark, certificate in shorthand; Ernest F. Cli fie, certificate in book-keeping ; Albert Corradine, certificate in arithmetic ; William Dennett, certificate in arithmetic ; Edwin P. Ford, certificate in arithmetic and shorthand ; Ernest Hay- Ward, certificate in bookkeeping, English and Spanish; Hubert J. Irldon, certificate in arith- metic ; John S. Smith, certificate in short- hand and bookkeeping ; Alfred S. Wildman, certifi- cate in shorthand; William G. Brown, certificates in building construction and carpentry and joinery ; William Drury, certificate in building construction and woodwork; Thomas Evans, certificate in machine con- struction ; Thomas Malone,certiticates in building con- struction, applied mechanics, and mechanical engineer- ing ; Sydney Massey, certificates in building construc- tion, woodwork, and carpentry and joinery; James McKay, certificate in woodwork ; Andrew Smith, 1 certificates in machine construction, applied mechanics, I and mechnical engineering; Fred Whittaker, certificate in building construction ; Thomas H. Ambler, first class certificate in tanning, and awarded the bronze medal of the City and Guilds of London Institute ; Joseph Fillingham and Bertram Ireland, certificates in tanning ; James H. Unsworth, certificates in plumbing awarded by the City and Guilds of London Institnte and the Plumbers' Company: Stanley C. Rathbone, first class certificate in book- keeping with distinction (first place in the whole of Lancashire and Cheshire.) Also awarded book prize by Union of Lancashire and Cheshire Institutes ; Simeon Bailey, commercial ; Joseph Welding, commercial; William J. Lake, building; Fred Sherratt, engineering; Arthur I. Newton, commercial, certificates in magnetism and electricity, and Spanish ; William IC Wood, commercial, certificates in bookkeeping and short- hand : Charles Bradshaw, preliminary, certificate in workshop arithmetic; Albert Wright, building certificates in woodwork and building constracion ; James Garnet, engineering, certificate in workshop arithmetic ; George T. Rushton, engineering, certifi- cates in machine construction, applied mechanics, mechanical engineering, and steam; Thomas A. Wilkin- son, engineering, certificates in steam, applied mechanics and mechanical engineering; Thomas Wicks, certificates in steam, applied mechanics, mathematics and mechanical engineering;Horace .1. Smith, chemical, certificates in theoretical inorganic chemistry, practical inorganic chemistry, and workshop arithmetic; Norman Nield, certificates in theoretical inorganic chemistry and iron and steel ; William Turner, acting teachers, certificates in model drawing and mathematics.</p>
        <p>THE VICE-CHANCELLOR'S ADDRESS. THE TRUE AIM OF EDUCATION.</p>
        <p>Vice-Chancellor DALE then delivered an interesting address. Referring to the prizes he had distributed he said he was delighted to see that in the parcels contain- ing works on boilers, income tax, quantity surveyings, and other matters too abstruse for persons like himself (laughter)—there were volumes of poetry and fiction with which he could claim to be not altogether unacquainted. The schools in connection with which that prize distribution was being held fermed a very important part of the educational system of the Borough. He wanted particularly to ask his hearers what they supposed the end of Education and the real object of classes of that kind to be. What he was going to say, he would say not with the least pretence of being an authority on education or philosophy or any horrible things of that kind—(laughter)---but simply as one for more than twenty years had had to do with boys and girls, with young men and young womeu, and with the parents of both. What he ventured to say was practically drawn from the experence one had in dealing with human beings. Might he suggest in the first place the enor- mous importance of having a worthy conception of education? (Hear,hear.) If they had a wrong idea of what it was or is low idea of its aims, they would inevitably go astray from the very !beginning, they would muddle the work that they attempted to do, and they would do nothing that was worthy of doing in any shape or form. Not only would they fail to do what was worth doing. but in some cases he feared they would do positive harm. A few weeks ago he was reading the report of a conference on technical education held in one of the colonies, which commenced its work by defining what technical education was. Various definitions were collected from the forty members of the assembly and set out in a kind of competitive examination. At last they brought out one definition as a kind of senior wrangler or prize pig—(laughter)—at the head of the list, and agreed that it was the definition by the help of which they were going to work out their salvation. It read: "Technical education is teat form of education which helps a man to get a living." When he read it for the first time it seemed to him that the definition like charity would cover a multitude of sins. (Laughter.) With that definition it would be perfectly easy and logical to describe as a technical institute that famous Academy kept by Mr. Fagin, in which the Art- ful Dodge took honours and handkerchiefs, and in which poor Oliver Twist got plucked. (Laughter.) They could also include in it as a subject of technical instruction the art or science which helped one to imitate the handwriting of other people—(langhter)— and he was quite sure it would include easily and with- out violence that peculiar application of mechanical skill that enabled one to model a key that would open the front door of any house in a street. (Laughter.) The definition could, in fact, be extended so as to embaace the science of crime in its widest range. [le did not assume that was the intention of the eminent author of the definition or of the very respectable gentlemen who adopted it as their maxim, but even if they added to the definition the word " honestly " it was, in his view, incomplete. Tt was no doubt the duty of every man to get a living and to get it honestly. He con- fessed that he paid most rates, which were an inevit- able element in modern civilization, with resignation, but there was one rate which he paid not only with reluctance bet with repugnance, after making all allowance for the misfortunes and calamities that harrassed human life—he meant that rate which, in part if not in whole, represented the toll or the tax that thrift and industry paid to idleness and vice. All educa- tion whether technical or not, should help a man to get a living, but if wage earning became the end, even in the case of technical education, and if once they began to measure the value and worth of education by its money value or its market value,they would get an educa- tion that was stunted, squalid and degraded that had neither life nor growth nor force. (Applause.) If he had to define technical education he would say it was that form of education that enabled a man or woman to do the best work of which he or she was capable with hand or with brain, that form of education that taught them to understand the work they did, to love that work an to find in it the joy that came from efficient and useful service. (Applause.) He would ask the recipients of the prizes never ho think of the results that were immediate or vniatonal. Let them remember that they were born to he men and women and that men and women were not machines. in their work they found their highest happiness. The younger scholars he would advise to chose subjects that were keys—subjects that unlocked the gates of a new world and gave them aims, interests and powers that would last as long as life itself, that would give them interests and pleasures that would keep them fresh and always make them free. They should set themselves to work so as to add to the stature of their minds and consciences and they could do that by living with men who were great. In the second place he would urge thorn to think first of interests and secondly of information. To his mind it did not matter so much what a person knew as what he cared for. In the third place he would ask them by all means to acquire that supreme and invaluable power of facing difficulties. Whatever progress our school education had made in recent years one defect he noticed with a considerable amount of fear. It appeared to him sometimes that too much was done for the scholar and too many difficulties were smoothed away from his path. He was not one of these who would make school repulsive or leave difficulties without a cause, but, for the practical purposes of life, to be able to surmount difficulties to be able to do the thing which it was not pleasant to do, was a faculty beyond price. He thought they would agree as to the importance of the work entrusted to educational committees. All muni- cipal work was important but the work was of excep- tional importance because it affected the life of the future so profoundly and vitally. He sometimes thought that some of them were apt to take themselves too seriously and not to take their work seriously enough. In the Book to whose solemn pages they turned for guidance in hours of darkness and for strength in hours of doubt, they were warned that if they buried their talents they would not escape con- I demnation. They must use all which they had received which was wholesome and which made not only for I their own good but also for the wet fare and the progress of the human race. (Applause.) If it was given him to choose between falling short of opportunities in his own life or of starving or crippling the life of another, so that he should be weak when he was meant to be strong, or poor, when he was meant to be rich, he would not hesitate for a moment which he would choose for he knew which was the greater sin and which deserved the heavier penalty. (Hear, hear.) He trusted they would recognize the great responsibility which they bore in that matter and that they would not only recognize their responsibility but would discharge it. They had the young in their keeping and he trusted they might be enabled so to do their part as to secure that supreme blessing—the blessing of knowledge which crowned, consumated and completed—for the nation which would be strong or secure the august and priceless inheritance of freedom. (Applause). Canon 'MORLEY STEVJ'NSON moved a vote of thanks to Vice-Chancellor Dale for his services. Mr. E. S. A. ROI:SON seconded. Doting the past year the technical classes he thought had been !fairly successful. In the commercial classes they had achieved a high standard of excellence, over 90 per cent of the total number of students having passed the various examinations. In the Technical Institute they gained</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-12-02_WidnesExaminer_art0134" n="art0134">
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, December 2, 1905.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-12-02_WidnesExaminer_art0135" n="art0135">
        <p>the first place in Lancashire and Cheshire in book-keep- ing, the third place in Spanish and the sixth in French. In the technological classes they had also had a success- ful year and had achieved a great success in the class for building construction one student obtaining a second prize and special bronze medal and another a third prize from the Worshipful , Company of Carpenters and Joiners. In the Science ' section they were hardly so successful. Certain depart- ments such as chemistry showed a high standard of excellence but the engineering department had hardly done itself justice. That had been remedied by the I committee by the appointment of a special director of the department.Othei things were however required for , the completion of the equipment in the way of engines, &amp;c., so that the students might obtain a practical knowledge. In the Secondary school the inspector's report was very satisfactory. He believed if they took very seriously the remarks made by the Vice Chancellor and coupled with it zeal and enthusiasm their results in the future would be better than in the past. (Applause.) The vote was heartily accorded and Vice-Chancellor DALE briefly responded. Mr. A. J. JOLLEY moved a vote of thanks to Alder- man Bennett for his services in the chair. Some people looked upon Alderman Bennett as a dreamer and an idealist. That was quite true. Alderman Bennett held lofty ideals but since he had been appointed chairman of the Education Committee he had coupled with those ideals thorou;hly sound, practical business methods. (Applause). Councillor FULTON SMITH seconded, and the vote was accorded very heartily. Alderman BENNETT having briefly responded the pro- ceedings concluded with the singing of the National Anthem.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-12-02_WidnesExaminer_art0136" n="art0136">
        <head>NOTES ON GAMES AND ATHLETICS. Rugby Football.</head>
        <p>NOTES ON GAMES AND ATHLETICS. Rugby Football.</p>
        <p>[By " Ovalist";</p>
        <p>To-day will probably witness the biggest and most interesting Rugby •match in the history of the game. The New Zealanders, with a record of 23 victories and no defeats, and an aggregate score of 694 points to 22 points, meet the chosen fifteen of England at the Crystal Palace. On the two last Saturdays they have vanquished Scotland and Ireland, and to-day they will in all probability assert an unquestionable superiority over the representatives of Old England. Intense interest in the match has been aroused by the " All Blacks' " wonderful record and there may be a crowd of anything from 50,000 to 100,000 at the Palace. A fair number of Warringtonians are making the journey to ,London, and for once in a way I too am forsaking Warrington in order to see this battle of the giants of the game. I will tell readers of this column about it next week, and shall probably have some comparisons to make between the style of play as affected by the New Zealanders and that practised here.</p>
        <p>Not much has been expected '.from Warrington of late, but they disappointed their supporters on Saturday. With a strong forward team and a back division strengthened by the timely inclusion of the Ex-Ranger Widdeson, they were expected to have more than an outside chance at Swinton. Instead of repeating last season's fine performance they cut up badly and were well-beaten by 12 points to 3 points. Swinton played a very dashing game and at times made the Warrington men look somewhat second-rate. The only excuse is to be found in the fact that Dame Fortune gave Warrington a very cruel blow in the first five minutes. A. lofty Swinton punt looked like sending the ball dead, when it struck the cross-bar and rebounded clean into the hands of a home threequarter who, of course, scored the easiest try imaginable. The goal- kick was successful and Warrington were thus five points in arrears through nothing but a misadventure which they could not have avoided.</p>
        <p>The succeeding play to the interval, was rather in Warrington's favour. They showed plenty of vigour, and one really fine piece of combination inaugurated cleverly by Hockenhall resulted in Widdeson scoring the only point for Warrington. They attacked per- sistently for sometime, but there was nothing like the sting of old in the aggressive movements and the Blues eventually beat them back. The scene of operations was changed to the Warrington line, and a premature attempt at clearance was penalised in front of the goal, and Swinton secured another couple of easy points. In the second half, Warrington appeared dispirited and made no sort of show. The forwardS collapsed and Swinton quite over-played them, five more points being deservedly obtained. One or two fairly decent efforts were made by the Warrington backs, but they came to nothing.</p>
        <p>Individually, Swinton were very little superior, but : their men played with much more dash and energy I than Warrington—though how Mach is attributable Ito the score in the first five minutes one cannot tell. It certainly influenced the course of the game. One of the few gratifying features from a Warrington point of view, was the success of Widdeson who played a smart game all the afternoon, and was well looked after by Isherwood. He looks as if he would shortly be in his best form. J. Davies was the prin- cipal failure, and his slowness behind the pack was in marked contrast to the work of Hockenhall and the Swinton pair. Hallam too, was far from being the Hallam of old and compared very unfavourably with the brilliant play of Dixon on the, other side. The forwardia may have had an off-day or they may have given up early on. Anyhow they were not in the hunt with the active Swinton eight, and will probably have heard some plain official English by now. -- Swinton struck me as being a very persevering side, with plenty of enthusiasm. They have made a big advance since last season, and such men as Dixon, Wallwork, Flynn, and Williamson are playing excel- lent football. The forwards work like steam engines and seem to be completely rejuvenated. On Saturday's form, Dixon, the full back, is the best custodian in Lancashire by a very long " chalk."</p>
        <p>The League table up-to-date is as follows For. Agst. Per-</p>
        <p>_ P. W. L. D. P. G. T. P. G. T. P. centage 101dharn l3 10 2 12118 38 150 10 4 32 8076 Leeds Bro'ton R 10 8 2 01617 39 151 11 10 52 8000 Halifax ll 7 1 31714 20 88 7 5 29 77.27 Runcorn Keighley ll 8 3 01612 23 93 10 9 47 73.54 Salford lO 7 3 01418 30 126 9 7 39 • 70.00 Bradford lO 6 2 2 1-1 19 33 137 810 46 70.00 Leigh Hull K. R 11 7 3 11512 15 69 813 55 68.18 Himslet 9 6 3 0 1215 20 90 616 GO 6666 Hull Swinton.— 11 7 4 01112 13 63 812 52 6363 St. Helens 8 5 3 010 1322 92 10 12 56 62450 Hndlield lO 5 4 11111 27 103 913 57 55.00 Wigan Dewsbury l2 5- 6 11110 16 68 13 17 77 4583 Warrington 12 5 6 11114 18 82 12 19 81 4583 York ll 4 6 1 911 11 55 915 63 4090 Wak'ileld Tll 3 5 3 913 9 53 15 24 104 4090 Bramley Castleford 7 2 4 1 5 7 4 26 11 18 76 35.07 Millom 6 1 4 1 3 6 7 33 14 21 91 25•00 Normanton 8 1 5 2 4 3 1 9 920 78 2500 Pontefract 10 2 7 1 5 7 5 29 14 16 76 2500 !Barrow lO 2 t 8 0 4 610 42 17 33 133 2000 Morecambe 9 1 7 1 3 5 9 37 17 33 133 16.66 Batley lO 1 R 1 3 7 3 23 19 19 95 1500 Widnes 8 1 7 0 2 2 4 16 12 18 78 1250 RochdaleH 12 0 9 3 3 3 8 30 14 31 130 1250 Brighouseß 9 0 8 1 1 5 5 25 17 33 133 5.50 The points are two for a win and one for a draw. Tha percentage is calculated upon the basis of the total points possible.</p>
        <p>Warrington should most certainly improve their position in the coarse of the next four matches. To- day they go to Widnes with an excellent chance of winning. then follow Runcorn home, Dewsbury away and St. Helens home. Eight points out of these four games is not too much to expect from the cup-holders, but I shall be surprised if they get more than six. A very different disnlay from last week's will have to be given to-day if Widnes are to be beaten.</p>
        <p>, The team for Widnes is : Knight, full back Widdeson, Isherwood, Jordan and Harris, three- quarters ; Hockenhall and Brooks, half backs; Preston, -Boardman, Themas, Naylor, Swift, Hartley. 'Heath, and Harmer forwards. The changes are all due to the weak performance at Swinton, and if they only make the remaining players play for their places there will be something gained. It is a pleasure to see Naylor back again. If not clever, he is a sterlinr,km. scrummager.</p>
        <p>The " A " team suceßsaftilly aeoonnted for Cliadder- ton on Saturday before a fine " gate." There was a suspicion of slackness forward up to half-time, but in the sot-minding stages the Warrington men asserted themselves and put on tett points. Jones made a satisfactory debut at ceuPre, but had few opportunities.</p>
        <p>'To-day against Widnei " A " two fresh Lads in Furness (local) and Cockcroft (Chaddertm) gtt a trial. Hallam, J. Davies, Cooke and Belton are all tempo- rarily on the retired list. The selected "A " teaua is :—C. Ashton, full back; Kenyon: Jones, Bennett, and Furness, threequarters ; G-arside and Lloyd, half- backs; Lunt, E. Lunt, Ashton, exkcroft and Jolley forwards.</p>
        <p>Stockton Heath Rugby Unionists visited Chester College, a fast and exciting game resulting. For the visitors 3. H. R. Jones who made his first appearance at full back played a grand game, his saving and tackling being fine. The tbreequarters combined well and some good passing resulted. Langley w-ho also made a first appearance at centre played a good game and should develop into a capable man. A. V. Ferguson and E. G. Holden both played a good game, the latter getting in some good runs. The halves H. Davenport and A. Bradshaw both did well and started some fine passing. The forwards to a man played hard and more often than not beat the College eight for possession although hardly as good in the loose. K. Gibbs, A. Cooke and J. Daintith were the pick of the eight. To-day the team meet Kersal " A " at home with whom they have an account to settle. In the first match of the season Kersal defeated the "Heathens " by 20 points to 3 and the younger club intend avenging that reverse. The following team, has been selected: J. H. R. .Jones; W. Bouchier, P. H izlehu rst,G. Langley, and A. Neild ; H. Davenport (cant.) and A. Bradshaw; W. Barker, A. Cooke, J. Daintith, A. H. Fawcett, K. Gibbs, G. W. Owen, R. DI. Swinton and W. Smith.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-12-02_WidnesExaminer_art0137" n="art0137">
        <head>Hockey.</head>
        <p>Hockey.</p>
        <p>Latchford met Eccles at Latchford on Saturday last, and the match resulted in an easy win for the home team by ilgoals to 1. Tha opening play proved veryeven,both sides attacking in turn, and it was soma time before Knight opened Latchford's account after good combina- tion on the part of the home team. Eccles attacked and this time equalised, but Latchford afterwards asserted themselves and drew ahead, Cliff e scoring. Latchford 2, Eccles lat half-time. From the restart however, there was practically only one team in it, although on two occasions the visitors might with a bit of luck have scored.Thishowever,also applied to the home team as besides the five goals they added Cliffs 2, Knight 2, Callan 1. The easiest of chances Were missed by over eagerness, on at least three occasions. The home forwards played with great dash, particularly the right wing, and easily beat the opposing defence.</p>
        <p>The seconds played at Eccles and the game resulted in a win for Latchford by 2 goals to nil.</p>
        <p>To-day Latchford play North •Nranchester at home. Team: goal, W. Unsworth: full backs, R. Hallows and T. Dixon; half-backs, W. Knight, J. B. Wilkinson and A. H. Rathbone; forwards. W. H. Spensley, E. F. Cliffe, D. Knight, .1. C Ohm, J. R. Claway. Second team match cancelled owing to County match.</p>
        <p>Stockton Heath defeated BrooklaTld at Arpley by 3-0, all the goals being put on in the last 20 minutes. The second team also did well in winning at 13rook1ands by 4-0.</p>
        <p>To-clay's match is cancelled on acconnt of the County match between Cheshire and Westmorland, which takes place on the Arpley enclosure, Two strong teams have been chosen, and if the rain holds off a good game should result.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-12-02_WidnesExaminer_art0138" n="art0138">
        <head>MAYORAL" AT HOME:'</head>
        <p>THE MAYORAL" AT HOME:'</p>
        <p>The Mayor and Mayoress (Alderman and Mrs. W. Bolton), held an a. At Home ' at the Town Hall on Thursday afternoon. Althougli the wsather was un- favourahle,there was from three to 6 o'clock an almost a continuous stream of visitors and the function was a pleasant and successful one. AS usual the Mayor and Mayoress received their guests in the Council Chamber. The entrance hall had been prettily decorated with chrysanthemums and choice plants by Mr. Whitaeld, the Corporation head gardener and presented a bright and animated appearance. Mr. A. Starkey's band was in attendance and discoursed a selection of light music in highly creditable style. The programme was as , follows :</p>
        <p>Grand March " Pensioners " E,St. Quentin' Overture " Gilderay " E. Berger. Waltz " Memories " J. Strauss. 2nd Polish Dance in A Minor P. Czynski. Entr'acte "sweet Briar" W.[-I. Squire. Flute Solo.. .. . ... "Alice where art thou" .l. Ascher. (Mr. J. Gilbert.) Valse Lento " Mon Ami "...J. Warwick Moore. Fonta.sia "A day in the country" H. Bogetti. Intermezzo " Rendez Vous " W, Aldeter. Three Dances " Drei Tanze " G.Sarakowski. Entr'acte " Eatase d'Arnour "...Raymond Rose. (From "The Tempest.") March " International " C. 3. Roberts.</p>
        <p>"God Save the King." In one of the committee rooms refreshments were served, the catering, which was in the hands of gr. and Mrs. Pow-is, giving every satisfaction. There was also a smoke-room which was largely patronized.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-12-02_WidnesExaminer_art0139" n="art0139">
        <head>TOWN COUNCIL.</head>
        <p>TOWN COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>Business of the Next Meeting. THE WAGE OF CORPORATION EMPLOYEES.</p>
        <p>The monthly meeting of the Town Council will be held on Tuesday evening next at six o'clock when the follow- ing among other business will be under consideration :</p>
        <p>HEALTIL COMMITTEE_AND 'UNSOUND FOOD.</p>
        <p>At a meeting of the Health Committee the Town Clerk read a letter from the Local Government Board asking for the observations of the Council on an applica- tion of the Manchester Port Sanitary Authority to be invested with powers to seize unsound articles intended for the food of man. It was resolved : That in the opinion of the committee, it is desirable that the Man- chester Port Sanitary Authority should be invested with the powers applied for and that the Town Clerk write to the Local Government Board supporting the applica- tion."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-12-02_WidnesExaminer_art0140" n="art0140">
        <head>INSPECTION OF FAXING MATETUALS.</head>
        <p>INSPECTION OF FAXING MATETUALS.</p>
        <p>The Chairman of the Pavingand Sewerage Committee (Alderman Tinnion), the deputy chairman (Counciilor Roberts), Alderman Hutchinson and Councillor Pem- berton have been appointed a sub-committee to inspect and report from time to time upon the delivery and quality of paving materials purchased by thecommittee.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-12-02_WidnesExaminer_art0141" n="art0141">
        <head>COAL AND SLA:CfC TENDERS.</head>
        <p>COAL AND SLA:CfC TENDERS.</p>
        <p>At a meeting of the Water Committee the tender of the Warrington Slate Company Ltd. for the supply of slack to be delivered at Winwick and Houghton Green was accepted, as was also the tender of Messrs. Richard Evans and Co. for the supply of coal to be delivered at Winwick, Houghton Green, Appleton and Dank House.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-12-02_WidnesExaminer_art0142" n="art0142">
        <head>w cmt covEns.</head>
        <p>w cmt covEns.</p>
        <p>At 41 meeting of the Electricity and Tramways Com- mittee the tender of :Messrs. NV. Rouse and Son, of Heckrhondwike, for four car top covers at £279 18s. 6d. was accepted. The following new smile of charges for electric energy for power purposes was adopted : Load Factor Charge per Unit Per Cent. Pence. 40 PO</p>
        <p>90 TrIE NIINISILTM WAGE DITEiTION AGAIN-21s. PROPOSED. Councillor Barton will move that the following motions become Standing Orders of the Council: (1) No able-bodied man employed under the Cor- poration and having reached 21 years of age, shall be paid less than at the rate of twenty-one shillings per week. (2) That each able-bodied man with two years' ser- vire or more be given one week's holiday each year. ,The above resolutions to come into force on or as soon after the Ist January next as practicable.</p>
        <p>THE ENGINE AT WINWICK—MOTION TO RESCIND THE connACT. Councillor Pemberton will move: (1) That the reso- lntion of July Rh, 1905 accepting the tender of Messrs. Jamss Simpson and Son, Linited, for compounding the engine at Winwick be rescinded. (2) That any resolu- tion in regard to spending any money on this engine (Water Lily) be rescinded. ANOTHER WATER-WORKS LOAN. Councillor Lord will ask the Chairman of the Water Committee, )1). Has application been made to the Local Government Board for powers to borrow the sum of £20,510 for waterworks purposes as passed by the Council on January 3rd last? (2). If so, on what date was the application made? (3). If not, why not ?</p>
        <p>A curious fact has just been brought to light as to the disposal of the cast-off sabres of the French army and, for the matter of that, of almost every army in Europe. They all go to Germany, that Elsyium of the "cheap and nasty,' to be in due time revended among the subject uative raceslof every Power in the world, including those of Germany herself.</p>
        <p>50 GO 70 80</p>
        <p>•94 98 •S 2 70 '7O</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-12-02_WidnesExaminer_art0143" n="art0143">
        <head>THREE NEW NOVELS.</head>
        <p>THREE NEW NOVELS.</p>
        <p>" The House of Mirth," by Edith Wharton ; Macmillan and Co., Gs. Mrs. Wharton's new novel " The House of Mirth " is the first"long story she has published since the issue of" The Valley of Decision," a book which raised her into the front rank of American women authors. The new work is one which will, unquestionably, enhance her reputation. We did not at first find it absorbingly interesting—the multitude of minor characters is rather distracting—but eventually the fortunes of the heroine, Lily Bart, lay hold of one with no uncertain grip. The scene is laid principally in New York, and many phases of American high society are admirably represented. Lity Bart is a penniless orphan nominally in charge of Mrs. Peniston, ti well-to-do but old-fashioned aunt whose hum-drum existence do not at all suit the tastes of her brilliant and beautiful niece The latter finds the houses of her many friends more congenial, and as she has found the knack of making herself indispensable to her hostesses she has no lack of invitations, and the people she meets, though perhaps needlessly numerous for the purposes of the story, are very cleverly sketched and form an admirable reflex of the tone of New York society. After a time Miss Bart's position became somewhat of a hollow mockery to her and she resolves to secure a husband wealthy enough to furnish her with all the luxury and ease that her nature craves. She therefore turns her back on the love of Lawrence Selden, though her, heart dictates the opposite course. Even then she lets her oppoituni tie 3 slip. Financial embarassment follows with the usual complications.. Lily Bart sinks in the social scale, but her honour she keeps 1 untarnished. The hour of her final despair is ennobled by resistance to temptation, and a profound act of renunciation. Her 'visit to Selden in his rooms on the last evening of her life is one of the many fine passages which abound in the book. Once again Selden sees her, but she lies then sleeping her last ' sleep in the shabby bedroom of a cheap boarding- house. And as he stands looking at the sweet face and mute lips, the mists melt, understanding flashes into his soul, and in the serene light of comprehension "he knelt by the bed and bent over her, draining their last moment to its lees ; and in the silence there passed between them the won l which made all clear."</p>
        <p>"Soprano," a portrait by F. Marian Crawford, Macmillan and Co., Os. _ .</p>
        <p>There is all Mr:Crawford's charm of style and artistic delineation of character in his new novel "Soprano." No matter how small a part such and such a persan may have in the story, the character is never carelessly drawn in one of Mr. Crawford's novels, and frequently his drallatis peronae are works of creative art. For once in a way, in " Soprano " IMr. Crawford leaves his favourite land of Italy and makes Paris the scene of his story. There is little of the gay life of the gay city in the book, and the scene might, as a matter of fact, have been pitched almost anywhere. Nevertheless the author knows Paris almost as well as Rome and his descrip- tions of and stray monologues on Paris and Parisians are always interesting. The heroine— or perhaps heroines—is a singet, a soprano, and some of the episodes take place behind the stage of the Opera House. There is, however, nothing stagey in the narrative and most of the life in it is lived in an every- day atmosphire. Margaret Donne is the prot‘gee of Mrs. Rushmore', a rich American widow, who, having no children of her own, opens her home to the orphaned daughter of an old friend. Margaret is twenty-two, good-looking, self-reliant, and clever, and the possessor of a marvellous soprano voice which eventually., leads her to the top of the professional tree. Her training brings her into association with Madame Bonanni,the prima donna, perhaps the finest character in the look. Once a French peasant, she is a charm- ing personality " half child, half genius, and all mother." It is her great love for her son that causes her to sacrifice what is sweetest and dearest in her life, and though her sins are great and many, she rises to heroic heights. The men are Edmund Lushington, critie,who eventually turns out to be Bonanni's son,and Constantine Logotheti, a Greek financier of educated tastes but somewhat barbaric notions-The story centres round these three. Logotheti on his first acquaint- ance with Margaret, felt "that he had met his natural mate. . . . yet the sensation wav not the result of a purely physical attraction. ft brought with it a satisfaction of artistic taste that was an unmarred pleasure in itself." The character oathe subtle Greek is very cleverly drawn, and a very exciting interest surrounds the contest between him and Lushington for Margaret's favour. The book ends with the intima- tion that only the first part of the story has been told, and this is its Most unsatisfactory feature. Margaret's career has only just commenced, and though we learn which of her suitors ha A fougd favour, it is easy to imagine that her future may yet be rich in dramatic incident. Perhaps Mr. Crawford will oblige at an early date. A more extended acquaintance with the characters'in " Soprano " will, we think, be generally desired.</p>
        <p>"Kipps," by H. G. Wells; Macmillan and Co., Os. We are told by the title-page that " Kirms" is the i story of 'a simple soul. It s. Arthur Kipps is very humble, very ordinary, and very simple. In him Mr. Wells has a character which he has evidently studied to good purpose. The story is plainly designed to present the life of a typical member of the small shopkeeping class in all it pitiful liMitation and feebleness, and beneath a treatment deliberately kindly and genial there lurks a sustained and exhaustive criticism of the ideals and ways of life of the great mass of middle-class English people. The characters grouped about the central figure of " Kipps " present contrasted influences in the commonplace English environment. All are based upon the most careful study of living types, and before all things the author has sought the glow of reality. This " story of a simple soul " is told with a subdued humour and an unforced pathos that quickly secure the attention and sympathy of the reader, and the varying fortunes of -poor ignorant, blunder- ing Kipps are followed with the liveliest interest. The story is inimitably clever and indicates another phase in Mr. Wells' genius. It is fall of witty sayings and acute deductions, and gives a rare insight into the grey and colourless lives of the class from whielt the hero springs. One fault we have to find with the book. While not wishing any curtailment of Kipps' none too rosy existence, we think the author has given us rather too much biography. He might, with advantage, have curtailed the history before a tend- ency to weariness creeps in. Most of the cleverest ' writing in the book is in the earlier chapters, and the effort to maintain the standard is rather obviously strained towards the end. One paragraph only we quote, yet it is an excellent sample of the whole, and shows the author's insight into the little things of life which gives the book much of its fascination. The quotation is from the narrative of the hero's boyhood "His aunt and uncle were already high on the bill of life when first he came to them. They had married for comfort in the evening or, at any rate, in the late afternoon of their days. They were at first nornore. than vague figures in the background of proximate realities, such realities as familiar chairs and tables, quiet to ride and drive, the newel of the staircase, kitchen` furniture, pieces of firewood, the boiler tap, old newspapers, the cat, the High-street the back yard, and the flat fields that are always so near in that little town. He knew all the stones in the yard individually, the creeper in the corner, the dustbin and the mossy wall, better than many men know the faces of their wives. There was a corner under the ironing-board which, by means of a shawl, could be made under the propitious gods, a very decent cnbby-house, a corner that served him for several years as the indisputable hub of the world, and the stringy places in the carpet, the knobs upon the dresser, and the several corners of the rag hearthrug his uncle had made, became essen- tial parts of his mental foundation." " Micky," by Evelyn Sharp ; Macmillan and Co., Os.. Miss Evelyn Sharp's new book, " Micky," is a delightful story of a little boy of six who lives in n. world of his own imagining, peopled with fairies and dragons, and beautiful princesses who are shut up in towers and are rescued by wonderful princes. The writer evidently loves her little hero. and his character is truly rendered. " Micky," like its pre- decessor "The Yeungest Girl in the School," is a book to be enjoyed by grown-ups as well as by chil- dren.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-12-02_WidnesExaminer_art0144" n="art0144">
        <head>"SIR NIGEL."</head>
        <p>"SIR NIGEL."</p>
        <p>The publishers of "The Strand Magazine" have been fortunate in securing a new serial from the pen of the well-known writer Sir Arthur Cowl Doyle. The name la 'Sir Nigel" and the scene is laid -in the Middle Ages. somewhat earlier than "The White Company." It haithe advantage over the latter book of being a broader picture of the age and delling more with the big events of national history of that time. The story begin sin "The Strand Magazine" Christmas Double Number and will run for a veer. Noted as this familiar magazine is for the excellence of its contents, it is confidently anticipated that " Sir Nigel" will excel in vivid and stirring interest any serial that has vet appeared.</p>
        <p>Two men were on Wednesday found suffocated in the stokehold of the steam lifeboat Qneen, at Birkenhead. They were left in charge of her. tind before going to sleep for the night they clesel the ventilator, thus leav- ing no outlet for the furnrce fumes.</p>
      </div>
