      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-12-02_WidnesExaminer_art0150" n="art0150">
        <head>Lessons From the Wreck of the Hilda. Sermon by the 'Rey. John Yonle.</head>
        <p>Lessons From the Wreck of the Hilda. Sermon by the 'Rey. John Yonle.</p>
        <p>At Wycliffe Church on Sunday evening the Rev. John Yonge preached a sermon on " Lessons from the wreck of the Hilda," taking for his text the mords : " Neither shalt thou be afraid of destruction, when it cometh " (Job v, 21.) He said he brought before them those words as befitting their consideration of the lamentable catastrophe which had touched their hearts so deeply that week. He also selected that text because they might indulge the hope that there were at least some of that ship's company who did then and there marvel- lously experience how God kept tranquil the soul that trusted Him, let happen whatever would. If that were so, then they might reasonably believe that their confidence in a redeeming God awakened a similar trust In others. A survivor had declared—and Ihis deolanition was wonderfully suggestive—that in that hour of sudden catastrophe there was no con- fusion. The statement seemed marvellous. Was it that fear had paralysed or mesmerized them? or was it that some commanding influence from some heroic souls then upheld and controlled by a faith that could stand the sudden strain, passel over the whole ship's com- pany, enabling them to encounter the unlooked-for death without panic, even composed and calm? He supposed they would never know. Such causes, had, however, produced such consequences in similar conr ditions. The preacher then vividly related the circumstances of the wreck of the Hilda almost at the very end of the voyage from Southampton to Great Maio, a voyage across a sea which was said to be sown with dangers. Only six of the 114 passengers survived. The disaster was one more terrible illustration of the peril of that very channel passage which some of them might have ventured when on holiday, possibly never realizing the grim, ghastly, wreck-strewn pathway over which they safely steamed. Dwelling upon the lessons of the calamity the preacher asked if the sea of life, across which every soul was voyaging, was not thickly strewn with rocks which threatened disaster to such as might be thoughtless or unexperienced ? How many bad set sail in life, regard- less of its dangers,confldent in their own integrity,their own capacity to refuse evil and ,to go straight and to escape mischief. And, alas, how many of these had gone crash on to the rocks of evil and sin, and even found a dread destruc- tion there. He urged all the young members of the congregation not to risk life without realising its perils as well as its pleasures, ano not to be unwilling to learn from the teachings of the Bible or from the dearly- bought experiences of those they might deem unduly Inarrow and strict. They might learn a precious lesson from Captain Gregory, the captain of the ill-fated vessel, who when once asked how he sailed his vessel in a fog said "There is only one way in such circumstances, and that is your compass, your watch, and trust in God." Let that statement be to them as a parable. Let the Bible be their compass, let experience of the wap of God be their guide, and let trust in Him be their spirit, their attitude, and their resolute and steadfast habit of life. Then would they sail the sea of life safelfY and confidently and avoid the hidden or menacing rocks.</p>
        <p>Wycliffe P.S.A. Society. ; l'here was a good meeting of this Society on Sunday last, when Mr. W. H. J. Jenkins presided, an spoke of the great ideals of a Christ-like life—which was a beautiful life, a pattern life, and a helpful life. He made reference to the unemployed question, which to him was no political party question, but a citizen ques- tion, and called upon all Christ's followers to remedy this sad state of things. To his mind it was very sad that while all this suffering was taking place, one man should at the same time be making and receiving his £8,000,000 for one year's interest.—Mr. Moulson, of Salford, was the speaker, and spoke upon" Workers for Christ." Everyone could do something in his own peculiar way towards helping the lot of others. Mr. C. Cotton and Mr. A. Healey Rlso took part lathe meeting. Mr. W. H. Payton presided at the organs</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-12-02_WidnesExaminer_art0151" n="art0151">
        <head>Scripture Union Meeting.</head>
        <p>Scripture Union Meeting.</p>
        <p>The scripture Union's monthly meeting at St. Luke's Church, Liverpool-road, was held on Sunday, under the chairmanship of Miss Wheelden, there being a very fair attendance of members. An interesting address was given by Mr. T. Taker, on" God's word ; T am the trite Vine arid My Father is the Husbandman." Mr. Warburton and I'Mr. Graham also took part in the meeting.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-12-02_WidnesExaminer_art0152" n="art0152">
        <head>STOCKTON HEATH.</head>
        <p>STOCKTON HEATH.</p>
        <p>TREE ANTING.—On Saturday afternoon there was another, hut informal, tree planting day at Stockton Heath. Trees were planted in Ellesmere-road, Fairfield- road, and Whitefleld-road, the whole of the number placed at the disposal of the committee being disposed of. A Committee meeting was held on Monday evening last when it was arranged to order further trees and guards making a total of 100. To enable the committee to do this Maher subscriptions are required and it is hoped that all who are desirous to see the tree planting extensively carried out this season and have not yet subscribed, will intimate their intentions to the Committee nt once. Lady Greenslt has kindly consented to give the Committee consider- able further assistance.</p>
      </div>
